#!/usr/bin/python3 -IB
"""Claude Code PreToolUse hook (Bash): refuse disabling GitHub Actions workflows, or Actions itself, on any repo.
Part of fork-kit/agent-guards (README.md there: install, settings entry, the machine file's "agent-guards" section).

Owner rule ("guards that refuse, not trust"). Why: a GitHub security log showed workflows.disable_workflow events
with the user agent "GitHub CLI ... Agent/claude-code_...": agents disabled workflows with the owner's gh login.

Best effort against mistakes, not a sandbox; the wall is a token that cannot do it. An agent runs as the
user who owns this hook, so one that sets out to get round a command-text hook can. Where the guard stops reading
(a limit, a file too big, out of time, its own crash) it refuses when the text it did not read names a guarded token.
A file too big to read whole, or one it cannot open, counts only where a shell or interpreter runs it (or a script
is copied); data handed to a tool passes as before.
How it reads a command (by parsing, not by pattern):
  1. Shell text, as a shell reads it: quotes, backslashes, $'..', comments, redirections, heredocs, && ; | chains,
     $(..) `..` <(..) (their insides are commands too), plain NAME=value and `for NAME in ..` variables (every value
     a name gets in the text is tried). Text it cannot know (other $VARs, $(..) output, "$@", {a,b}) stays in the
     word as a hidden mark, it is never dropped.
  2. `gh` arguments, as gh reads them (cobra/pflag, gh 2.102): flags before the subcommand, `--flag=value`,
     `--flag value`, joined short flags (`-iXPUT`, `-XPUT`, `-X=PUT`), and which `gh api` flags take a value, so a
     value that looks like a flag (`-p -XGET`, `--jq --method=GET`) is a value. The last -X/--method wins. An
     unknown flag, a flag without its value, or hidden text where a flag or the method may stand makes the call a
     write to whatever guarded path is in sight.
  3. Guarded tokens. After decoding (backslashes, \\uXXXX, \\xXX, %XX, quotes, case, /./ and /x/../) it looks for:
     .../actions/workflows/<id>/disable, `workflow disable`, .../actions/permissions, branches/<b>/protection,
     rulesets/<id>, and the GraphQL mutations deleteBranchProtectionRule, deleteRepositoryRuleset and
     updateRepositoryRuleset. A command that names one of them passes only when the guard can PROVE it harmless:
       - the command word only handles text (echo, cat, grep, rg, sed, jq ..: the DATA list below)
       - gh, with a gh command this reader knows (rule 2 decides read or write)
       - `curl -q ..` / `wget --no-config ..` as a plain read (the tool's read-only flags and nothing else)
       - git commit/log/grep/diff/add ..: text git itself never runs
       - shell text (bash -c '..', a .sh file, ssh host '..', a heredoc into sh): read again by these same rules
     Every other use is refused: a command word the guard does not know (taskset, chrt, unshare, sg, screen, tmux,
     at, expect, make, a project tool, a word from a variable), inline code or a script in any other language
     (python -c, node -e, a .py file), an HTTP client without a flag table, gh under an alias or an extension.
     A shell comment in a file read for CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE (~/.bashrc, a sourced file) is no command (#1057), as
     the shell reads it. The command's own text and Python, JS and other code are read whole, comments too: a
     command can read its own comments ($BASH_EXECUTION_STRING), and a guess at another language's is a way past.
  4. Known command words only widen what passes; an unknown word never hides what follows it. The words behind it
     are read as a command of their own (`taskset 1 bash -c ..`), its quoted arguments as shell text
     (`sg users -c '..'`), and gh is still gh under a name the command gives it (`ln -s /usr/bin/gh g; ./g ..`).
     Files it is handed are looked at too: a shell script is read in full, a file that looks like code in another
     language (.py/.js/.. name, a `#!` line, the executable bit) may not name a guarded token. An interpreter's
     script is found behind its own words (`deno run x.ts`, `python3 -m x`, `node -r ./x.js`). A wrapper is a
     wrapper only under its own name or in a system folder: `./sudo` and the `nice` of `bash nice` are files.
     Files that carry arguments are read: `curl -K`, `wget -i`, `xargs -a`.
  5. Flow. Token text that goes on (a pipe into anything but a DATA word, a file, $(..), an exported variable, a
     file that names a token and gets another name with cp/mv/ln/install, dd of=, sed w or awk print >) in a
     command that also runs a word the guard cannot read is refused: `echo '<the command>' | at now`,
     `cp x.txt run.sh && bash run.sh`, `curl -q -X PUT "$(cat url.txt)"`.
  6. It answers in time: the hook has a 10 s limit and a hook that is not done by then does not stop the
     command, so 5 s after the hook process started the guard stops reading. It then skims the command (the skim,
     below decide_text): refused when the command or a file it names holds a guard word (a guarded token, gh, an
     HTTP client, `workflow`, `disable`, claude/codex, a refuseScripts line) or the skim runs out of its 2 s too,
     passed otherwise, answered by 8 s at the latest. Shell text past MAX_COMMANDS (10,000) commands in one call
     is skimmed the same way instead of parsed (a 2 MB script took 4 s on an idle machine). Before that:
       - fast exit: a command of plain words that cannot name or run anything guarded (ls, git status, cat a > b,
         grep ..: quick_pass() below) passes before any file is read and whatever the clock says;
       - cache: what it learns from files (a code file names a token or not, a `#!` line, the gh aliases) is kept
         60 s in <stateDir>/cache-v1.json (default ~/.local/state/actions-disable-guard), per file name + inode + size + change times.
         A file changed within the last 2 s is not cached; a missing, broken, old, foreign or odd cache is ignored
         and written new (temp file, then rename). A run whose guard file, or a link on the way to it (the kit's
         bin link and `current`), changed after the run started neither reads nor writes it: the code in memory may
         be another release's. Shell scripts are still read every time (their reading depends on the command
         around them).

Refused:
  - gh workflow disable ...                    (also through gh aliases: existing ones are expanded, new ones refused;
                                                `gh workflow <hidden>` too)
  - gh api / curl / wget / httpie to .../actions/workflows/<id or file>/disable
  - any write (not GET/HEAD, fields or --input without -X, a method-override header) to .../actions/permissions[/...]
    for a repo, org or enterprise; the one write that passes is the re-enable
    `gh api -X PUT repos/O/R/actions/permissions -F enabled=true [-f allowed_actions=all]`
  - curl and wget to a guarded path unless their own config is off: curl reads ~/.curlrc ($CURL_HOME,
    $XDG_CONFIG_HOME) unless `-q`/`--disable` is the FIRST argument, wget reads ~/.wgetrc ($WGETRC, /etc/wgetrc)
    unless `--no-config` is given, and a line there (`request = PUT`) turns a read into a write. The flag rule was
    picked over "refuse when such a file exists" because it can be read from the command text alone: the guard
    does not have to know every place the tool looks, or what the same command writes there first.
  - a path with hidden parts that can spell one of those (`"$B/disable"`, `repos/$R/actions/$X`), when it is a write;
    and, in the command itself, a `gh api` write whose path is hidden altogether (`gh api -X PUT "$URL"`)
  - DELETE of branch protection (`.../branches/<b>/protection[/...]`, also for a branch name with slashes) or of a
    ruleset, the GraphQL mutations that do the same, a write that switches one ruleset off (`enforcement=disabled`,
    updateRepositoryRuleset with DISABLED, also written with \\u escapes) or carries a body the guard cannot
    read, and (owner decision, #930) a write that leaves no required check: a protection PUT with
    required_status_checks null, left out or with empty contexts/checks, an empty PATCH of required_status_checks or
    PUT of its contexts, a ruleset write whose `rules` hold no required_status_checks rule with a check, the GraphQL
    updateBranchProtectionRule / updateRepositoryRuleset that does the same (requiresStatusChecks false, [] lists, a
    REQUIRED_STATUS_CHECKS rule with an empty list, also through a variable or the whole input as one variable from
    -F 'input[..]' fields or an --input file, and a variable the guard cannot read), by gh api (-f/-F nested
    fields, --input) or curl/wget (-d, --json, --post-data ..), and such a write whose body the guard cannot read.
    A write that keeps or adds checks passes
  - everything rule 3 cannot prove and rule 5 catches (see there)
  - `gh` with a word that is no gh command when the gh config holds YAML outside the small subset this reader
    follows (`!!str` tags, `? ` keys, anchors, flow maps ..), or sits in a folder the guard cannot read
    (GH_CONFIG_DIR, XDG_CONFIG_HOME or HOME from a variable): the word may be an alias
  - each script of the machine file's actionsDisable.refuseScripts run with its flag (a project tool that
    disables workflows itself: {"name": "garden.py", "flag": "--fix", "why": "disables ..."}), where the file is real;
    and a line that calls one in a file whose text goes on (sed e, `cat f | bash`, eval "$(cat f)", `$(cat f)`,
    `cat f | heavy bash`, `cat f | at now` ..). One rule follows what runs: a file's lines reach a program that runs
    its input (a shell, also `sudo -s` or a bare interpreter, `bash -c "$(< f)"`, `xargs -L1 env`) through any
    filters (`sort f | bash`, `nl f | cut -f2 | bash`), or a program that runs the lines it reads (awk system() or
    a pipe to a command, perl/ruby system/qx); writes by sed -f / awk -f scripts and rsync count as copies
  - a GraphQL call whose query (or a field name that can be `query`) the guard cannot read
  - a command the guard cannot finish reading in time, when the skim finds a guard word in it or a file it names
Passes: reads (GET), gh workflow enable/list/view/run, PUT .../enable, searching and writing text about all this
(rg/grep, notes, commit messages, issue and PR bodies through gh), and every command that names no guarded token.
A file that is byte for byte this guard is not read as a script (running or testing the guard is no attack).

Known over-refusals (harmless, but not provable from the text; the message says what to change):
  - a read with curl/wget without `-q` / `--no-config`, or with httpie/xh/aria2c: use `gh api`
  - code that only mentions a token (python -c "print('gh workflow disable')", a heredoc into python that edits
    a text, a .py file): change or search such text with an edit or search tool; this guard's own test file run
    directly: run it through a test runner that is handed its folder
  - a token in the words of a tool the guard does not know (`sometool -m "text about actions/permissions"`,
    awk '/actions\\/permissions/'), in the words handed to a shell function, or written to a file / piped on in
    the same command that runs such a tool
  - a code file that names a token (this guard, its tests) as an argument of a tool the guard does not know
    (`pytest tests/test_x.py`, `ruff check x.py`, a project commit tool): name the folder, or use git add
  - a code file too big to read whole, as an argument of a tool the guard does not know (`gzip -c big.js`): pipe
    it in (`cat big.js | gzip`)
  - an exported variable or a `VAR=.. command` prefix that carries a token
  - a file that only mentions a refuseScripts line, piped or substituted into a program that may run what it
    reads (a bare `python3`, `ssh host` with no command, a pipe that ends in a shell); into a script, a pager, a
    compressor, `ssh host CMD` or `claude -p` it passes (issue 999). A program that takes its code inline and
    reads stdin (python3 -c, perl -ne, node -e, ruby -ne, also with `< msg.md`) counts as one that may run it, as
    does a data reader (python3 tools/check.py < msg.md) when the same command also makes a folder, writes a code
    or dot-less file, or runs another program that may write files (`mkdir -p out; ..`, `git status; ..`), or when
    it sits in a { } group: give the file as an argument (`python3 -c '..' msg.md`, `perl -ne '..' msg.md`) or run
    the writes in a command before
  - a GraphQL body piped into gh (`echo '{..}' | gh api graphql --input -`): use `--input - <<< '{..}'` or -f query=
  - a GraphQL query from a variable or command output, also a read (`-f query="$(cat q.graphql)"`): use
    `-F query=@q.graphql`; a $variable inside "quotes" in the query (an id) is fine
  - a gh word that is no gh command (an alias) while the gh config is half-written or uses YAML outside the subset
  - a rules write to a ruleset that never had required checks (a tag ruleset), or a protection body written by the
    same command: the owner marker, or write the body file in a command before

Not covered (a text guard cannot): a value hidden in a variable, file or command output that the command gets at
run time (a download, base64, a file renamed by a tool other than cp/mv/ln/install/dd/rsync or a sed/awk program), code
that builds the words at run time or loads them from another file (import, require), a file of commands that is no
shell script and does not look like code, written by an earlier command and handed to a tool the guard does not
know (`at -f job.txt`, `make -f x.mk`, a Makefile, package.json scripts), a gh copy made by an earlier command when
there is no gh on PATH to compare it with, a compiled program that shadows a DATA or wrapper word on PATH (a text
script there is read), a PATH set by the command itself, a remote shell's own config, raw clients (openssl
s_client), GraphQL sent through curl/wget. Also not covered (a review round; each needs a command built to hide the
action; closed by the token layer):
  - known words that run code: find -exec, BASH_ENV, git diff.external / core.fsmonitor
  - protection writes that keep a check but weaken the rest: a PUT that replaces the checks with other ones, drops
    the review rules or enforce_admins, a ruleset whose conditions leave `main` out or that adds bypass actors (the
    guard cannot ask GitHub what is there now)
  - on a system without /proc the start-time check of the cache (an edited guard during a run) is skipped
  - a wall clock stepped forward across a reinstall during a run: the start-time check compares file and link
    times with the process start
  - the guard is read from source on each call (python3 -IB, no bytecode): an entry file compiled at install time
    would save some ms, a larger design (#930 item 12)
  - a command kept in a variable and run as the command word (`read -r c <<< '..'; $c`)
  - a script changed by the same command before it runs it (mv, ln, >, >>, sed -i, then bash)
A guarded call only in a script 5 or more levels deep (a.sh -> b.sh -> c.sh -> d.sh -> e.sh) passes as an unknown program
unless other text in the command flows on.
gh has no request hook, so this hook is the command-text layer; the wall is a token that cannot do it.

Escape hatch (owner only, as root): `sudo agent-guards approve [--hours N] --command '<cmd>'` approves ONE command
run in one folder, once: it prints a code and keeps in a root-owned file (lib/agent_guards/approval.py) only the
code's hash, the normalised command's hash, the folder and the end time (1 hour by default). That command, ending in
  #owner-approved:actions-disable:<code>
passes once, in that folder, before the end time; the use is recorded in the state folder and a second run, or any
other command with the code, is refused. A code seen in a transcript, the app's database or `ps` opens nothing else.
An agent cannot write the file, so it cannot approve itself, and nothing is inherited: a nested session gets the
code only when it is in its command. (The old CLAUDE_GUARD_OWNER_ALLOW_ACTIONS_DISABLE variable opens nothing any more: a variable reaches
every nested `claude -p` or `codex exec` of the session.) Fails closed: an internal error refuses commands that
look dangerous.

Nested agents: a command that starts an agent session this machine's hooks do not reach is refused, since that
session could do what this guard refuses: `claude --bare` / `--safe-mode` (safe mode runs managed hooks, but not
hooks a user wired by hand in user settings), `claude` with CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE set, and `codex` with anything but
login/logout/help/completion/mcp/--version (codex runs no Claude Code hooks). claude and codex count by name, by
their program path (.../claude/versions/<v>, @anthropic-ai/claude-code, @openai/codex), as a link to or a copy of
the installed program, as a name the same command gives one (ln -s), through npx/bunx/pnpm dlx/npm exec/bun x and
`node <their cli>`, behind `$(which claude)`, and as text in python -c, node -e, at, systemd-run .. (the same token
reading as for gh). CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE counts in the command, through env -S, from a file the command sources, from
BASH_ENV/--rcfile, and from the agents' user's shell start files (~/.bashrc ..; files those read are not followed).
A nested `claude -p` passes: managed hooks run there too, also with `--settings '{"disableAllHooks": true}'`.
This is a list, and a list of strings cannot be complete: it stops mistakes and casual misuse, not an agent set on
getting round it (other agent programs, an MCP tool that starts one, words built at run time). The wall against
that is the GitHub permissions of the token the agent holds (README, "What the guards are and are not").

Facts from the machine file (fork-kit/agent-guards/README.md): actionsDisable.stateDir (cache and log of
refusals, default ~/.local/state/actions-disable-guard) and actionsDisable.refuseScripts (default none). A missing
machine file or section gives those defaults; a broken one too, with one line on stderr: the guard never weakens.
Tests: fork-kit/agent-guards/tests/actions-disable.test.py
"""
import fnmatch, itertools, json, os, re, shlex, signal, sys, time
from stat import S_ISDIR, S_ISLNK, S_ISREG
from urllib.parse import unquote


def _settings():
    """(actionsDisable settings, problem or None). The kit's lib sits next to bin/; a copy without it keeps the
    built-in defaults and says so (on stderr, in answer())."""
    try:
        sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))), 'lib'))
        from agent_guards import config
        return config.load('actionsDisable')
    except BaseException as e:   # noqa: BLE001 - the guard works without its config
        return ({'stateDir': '~/.local/state/actions-disable-guard', 'refuseScripts': []},
                f'cannot load the kit\'s config code ({type(e).__name__}: {e})')


SETTINGS, SETTINGS_PROBLEM = _settings()
REFUSE_SCRIPTS = tuple(SETTINGS['refuseScripts'])

MARK_CMD = '#owner-approved:actions-disable:'   # + the owner's code (agent_guards.approval)
# Marks inside a word (private-use characters; the same characters in a command read as hidden text too):
HID = ''    # text the guard cannot know, as one piece of this word
WILD = ''   # text the guard cannot know that may be no word at all, or several ("$@", unquoted $X, {a,b})
DISABLE_RE = re.compile(r'actions/workflows/[^/\s]+/disable(?![\w.-])', re.I)
PERM_RE = re.compile(r'actions/permissions(?![\w-])', re.I)
PROTECT_RE = re.compile(r'branches/[^\s?#]+?/protection(?![\w-])|(?<![\w-])rulesets/[^/\s?#]+/?(?:[?#]|$)', re.I)
RULESET_RE = re.compile(r'(?<![\w-])rulesets/[^/\s?#]+/?(?:[?#]|$)', re.I)
SHELL_VALUE = re.compile(r'\$[A-Za-z_{(]|`')   # $NAME, ${..}, $(..), `..` in text ($1 is a gh alias placeholder)
# in a request body, any quoting. "evaluate" switches the rules off too: they are only reported, not enforced.
RULESET_OFF = re.compile(r'enforcement["\'\s]*[:=]["\'\s]*(?:disabled|evaluate)', re.I)
OFF_WORD = re.compile(r'disabled|evaluate', re.I)
REPO_PERM_RE = re.compile(r'^(?:https?://[^/]+)?/?repos/[^/\s]+/[^/\s]+/actions/permissions/?$', re.I)
GQL_RE = re.compile(r'deleteBranchProtectionRule|deleteRepositoryRuleset', re.I)
GQL_UPDATE = re.compile(r'updateRepositoryRuleset', re.I)   # refused when the text also says disabled
LOOSE_RE = re.compile(r'workflow\W+disable|/disable\b|actions/permissions', re.I)
# kind -> (how it looks spelled out, [(its path words, None = any one word, ... = one or more (a branch name
# may hold slashes); whether more may follow)])
GUARDED = {'disable': (DISABLE_RE, [(['actions', 'workflows', None, 'disable'], False)]),
           'perm': (PERM_RE, [(['actions', 'permissions'], True)]),
           'protect': (PROTECT_RE, [(['branches', ..., 'protection'], True), (['rulesets', None], False)]),
           'ruleset': (RULESET_RE, [(['rulesets', None], False)])}   # part of protect: one ruleset, by id
PLACEHOLDER = re.compile(r'\{(?:owner|repo|branch)\}')   # gh api fills these in from the checkout
READS = ('GET', 'HEAD')
# restricted and other sh/csh-family names too: rbash is bash (a link), rksh is ksh
SHELLS = {'sh', 'bash', 'zsh', 'dash', 'ksh', 'fish', 'busybox', 'rbash', 'rksh', 'rzsh', 'mksh', 'lksh', 'oksh',
          'pdksh', 'ksh93', 'ash', 'yash', 'posh', 'csh', 'tcsh'}
WRAPPERS = {'sudo', 'doas', 'env', 'command', 'exec', 'builtin', 'nohup', 'time', 'nice', 'ionice', 'timeout',
            'stdbuf', 'xargs', 'setsid', 'unbuffer', 'chronic', 'flock', 'eval', 'source', '.', 'do', 'then',
            'else', 'elif', 'if', 'while', 'until', '{', '}', '!', 'watch', 'script', 'runuser', 'systemd-run',
            'coproc'} | SHELLS
ARG_FLAGS = {'-u', '-g', '-n', '-k', '-s', '-I', '-P', '-L', '-d', '-E', '-C', '-p', '-o', '-e', '-i', '-l', '-a',
             '--user', '--group', '--signal', '--kill-after', '--unit', '--arg-file', '--delimiter', '--max-lines',
             '--max-args', '--max-procs', '--max-chars', '--process-slot-var'}
TEXT_ARGS = {'trap', 'ssh', 'su', 'parallel'}            # commands whose quoted arguments are shell text
SETTERS = {'export', 'local', 'declare', 'typeset', 'readonly'}
TAINTERS = {'read', 'mapfile', 'readarray', 'getopts', 'printf', 'let', 'unset', 'select'}   # set names from data
INTERPRETERS = re.compile(r'^(python[\d.]*|pypy[\d.]*|node|nodejs|deno|bun|perl|ruby|php|pwsh|lua|tclsh|osascript)$')
NET_CLIENTS = {'curl', 'wget', 'http', 'https', 'xh', 'xhs', 'curlie', 'httpie', 'aria2c'}
# gh api (gh 2.102.0, `gh api --help`): the flags that take a value, and the switches
API_VALUE = {'cache', 'field', 'header', 'hostname', 'input', 'jq', 'method', 'preview', 'raw-field', 'template'}
API_SWITCH = {'allow-escape-sequences', 'include', 'paginate', 'silent', 'slurp', 'verbose', 'help'}
API_SHORT = {'F': 'field', 'H': 'header', 'q': 'jq', 'X': 'method', 'p': 'preview', 'f': 'raw-field',
             't': 'template'}
API_SHORT_SWITCH = {'i', 'h'}
GH_VERSION = '2.102'
# gh's own commands and help topics (`gh help`, gh 2.102.0). An alias cannot take one of these names.
GH_COMMANDS = {'auth', 'browse', 'codespace', 'discussion', 'gist', 'issue', 'org', 'pr', 'project', 'release',
               'repo', 'skill', 'cache', 'run', 'workflow', 'agent-task', 'alias', 'api', 'attestation',
               'completion', 'config', 'copilot', 'extension', 'gpg-key', 'label', 'licenses', 'preview', 'ruleset',
               'search', 'secret', 'ssh-key', 'status', 'variable', 'help', 'version', 'accessibility', 'actions',
               'environment', 'exit-codes', 'formatting', 'mintty', 'reference', 'telemetry',
               'cs', 'rs', 'at', 'agent', 'agents', 'agent-tasks'}   # the short names gh has for its own commands
# In the command itself (not in script files or alias texts): refuse a `gh api` write whose path is hidden
# altogether. It stops careless calls only (a hidden value can always carry a whole command), so it is one switch.
HIDDEN_PATH_RULE = True
# curl and wget: what a plain read may carry, one table per tool because each reads flags its own way:
# (switches, flags with a value, method flags, short switches, short flags with a value, short method flag,
#  whether --flag=value is read). Anything else next to a guarded path is refused; a tool without a table
# (httpie, xh, aria2c, a hidden command word) may carry no flags at all there.
NET_TOOLS = {
    'curl': ({'--silent', '--show-error', '--location', '--fail', '--fail-with-body', '--include', '--head',
              '--verbose', '--compressed', '--get', '--globoff', '--no-buffer', '--no-progress-meter', '--http1.1',
              '--http2', '--ipv4', '--ipv6', '--disable'},
             {'--header', '--output', '--user-agent', '--max-time', '--connect-timeout', '--retry', '--retry-delay',
              '--retry-max-time', '--write-out', '--user', '--oauth2-bearer', '--url', '--dump-header'},
             {'--request'}, 'qsSLfiIvgGN46', 'HoAmwuD', 'X', False),
    'wget': ({'--quiet', '--no-verbose', '--server-response', '--verbose', '--no-config'},
             {'--output-document', '--header', '--tries', '--timeout', '--user-agent'},
             {'--method'}, 'qSv46', 'OtTU', '', True),
}
NO_FLAGS = (set(), set(), set(), '', '', '', False)
MAX_FILE, MAX_DEPTH, MAX_NEST, MAX_READINGS = 2_000_000, 2, 8, 64
HARD_FILE = 64_000_000   # a bigger file that a shell or interpreter is about to run is refused unread
TOO_BIG, NO_OPEN = f'a file over {HARD_FILE // 1_000_000} MB', 'a file the guard cannot open'
# The hook is registered with a 10 s limit, and a hook that is not done by then does not stop the command. So the
# guard stops itself earlier and refuses: a command it could not read to the end is one it cannot let through.
BUDGET = 5.0
START = time.monotonic()
QUICK = True   # the fast exit (quick_pass); a switch for the tests that compare it with the full reading
CACHE_VERSION, CACHE_LIFE, CACHE_MAX, CACHE_BYTES = 1, 60.0, 512, 1_000_000
CACHE_RACY = 2.0
CRASH_RE = re.compile(r'\bgh\b|curl|wget|httpie|\bxh\b|api\.github|workflow|disable', re.I)   # see main()
SELF = os.path.realpath(os.path.abspath(__file__)) if '__file__' in globals() else ''
RULE = ('Owner rule (2026-11-13, "guards that refuse, not trust"): no agent disables GitHub Actions workflows, or '
        'Actions itself, on any repo; re-enabling (`gh workflow enable`, PUT .../enable) is fine. If disabling is '
        'really needed, stop and ask the owner: name the repo, the workflow and why. Only the owner can approve '
        'it: they run `sudo agent-guards approve --command \'<the exact command>\'` and give you a code; that '
        f'one command, ending in {MARK_CMD}<code>, then passes once, in the folder they approved it for. Agents '
        'must not make up a code, reuse one they were not given for this, start an agent '
        'session without this machine\'s hooks, or route around this guard with scripts, aliases or other HTTP '
        'clients.')
UNREADABLE = ('If this call is harmless, write it so the guard can read it: the path and the flags spelled out in '
              'the command, variables only for ids and only inside quotes.')


PREFIX = 'Refused by actions-disable-guard: '


def reason(what, hint=''):
    """The refusal: what was refused and why, what to do instead, then the owner rule."""
    return f'{PREFIX}{what}. {"What to do instead: " + hint + " " if hint else ""}{RULE}'


class Slow(BaseException):
    """The guard is out of time for this command. Not an Exception, so no `except Exception` on the way swallows it."""


def out_of_time(*_):
    raise Slow()


SLOW_HINT = ('If it does not switch anything off, run it as smaller Bash calls (the part that calls gh or an HTTP '
             'client on its own); tell the owner if this repeats.')
SLOW = reason(f'the guard could not finish reading this command and the scripts it runs within {BUDGET:g} s of '
              'its start, so it refuses (a hook that runs out of time would let the command through unread)', SLOW_HINT)


def process_age():
    """Seconds the hook process has used of its limit. The 10 s limit counts from the start of the process, so
    Python's own start and the reading of the event count too. Loaded as a module (tests, gh-as) there is no hook
    clock: 0."""
    return since_start() if __name__ == '__main__' else 0.0


def since_start():
    """Seconds since this process started: from /proc where it is there and makes sense, else since this file was
    loaded (the first thing the process does)."""
    since = time.monotonic() - START
    try:
        with open('/proc/self/stat') as f:
            ticks = int(f.read().rsplit(')', 1)[1].split()[19])   # field 22: start time, in clock ticks since boot
        with open('/proc/uptime') as f:
            age = float(f.read().split()[0]) - ticks / os.sysconf('SC_CLK_TCK')
        if since <= age < 3600:
            return age
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        pass
    return since


def hidden(tok):
    return HID in tok or WILD in tok


# ---------- shell text -> commands of words

ORD = re.compile(r'[^ \t\n\\\'"$`<>;&|()#]+')
DQ_ORD = re.compile(r'[^"\\$`]+')
NAME = re.compile(r'[A-Za-z_]\w*')
GROUP_IDS = itertools.count()
REDIR = re.compile(r'<<<|<<-|<<|<>|<&|>>|>&|>\||<|>')
ASSIGN = re.compile(r'^([A-Za-z_]\w*)(\+?)=(.*)$', re.S)
BRACE = re.compile(r'\{[^{}\s]*(?:,|\.\.)[^{}\s]*\}')
ANSI = re.compile(r'\\(x[0-9a-fA-F]{1,2}|u[0-9a-fA-F]{1,4}|U[0-9a-fA-F]{1,8}|[0-7]{1,3}|c.|.)', re.S)
ANSI_ONE = {'n': '\n', 't': '\t', 'r': '\r', 'a': '\a', 'b': '\b', 'e': '\x1b', 'E': '\x1b', 'f': '\f', 'v': '\v',
            '\\': '\\', "'": "'", '"': '"', '?': '?'}
FUNC_RE = re.compile(r'(?:^|[;&|\n{(])\s*(?:function\s+([A-Za-z_][\w.:-]*)|([A-Za-z_][\w.:-]*)\s*\(\s*\))')


def ansi_c(s):
    """The text of a $'..' string."""
    def one(m):
        e = m.group(1)
        if e[0] in 'xuU' and len(e) > 1:
            return chr(min(int(e[1:], 16), 0x10ffff))
        if e[0] in '01234567':
            return chr(int(e, 8) & 0xff)
        if e[0] == 'c' and len(e) == 2:
            return chr(ord(e[1]) & 0x1f)
        return ANSI_ONE.get(e, '\\' + e)
    return ANSI.sub(one, s)


class Lex:
    """Shell text as a shell splits it. cmds: one dict per simple command with words (lists of pieces), redirs
    [(operator, word)] and docs [heredoc body]. A piece is (kind, text, quoted): L literal text, V a $NAME, H text
    no reader can know ($(..), $1, ${X:-y}), M the same but maybe several words ("$@"). subs: the texts inside
    $(..), `..`, <(..), which are commands too. taint: names that get a value no reader can know. Never raises:
    an unclosed quote or bracket runs to the end (a shell runs the lines before it all the same)."""

    def __init__(self, text, run=True):
        self.t, self.n = text, len(text)
        self.cmds, self.subs, self.taint, self.spans, self.notes = [], [], set(), [], []
        self.cur, self.word, self.redir, self.pending, self.depth = self.new(), None, None, [], 0
        self.groups = []   # the ( .. ) and { ..; } groups around the current command, by id
        if run:
            self.run()

    @staticmethod
    def new():
        return {'words': [], 'redirs': [], 'docs': []}

    @classmethod
    def expansions(cls, text):
        """The commands inside the $(..) and `..` of text a shell expands without reading quotes (a heredoc body)."""
        lx, i = cls(text, run=False), 0
        while i < lx.n:
            c = text[i]
            if c == '\\':
                i += 2
            elif c == '$':
                i = lx.dollar(i, True)
            elif c == '`':
                i = lx.backtick(i, True)
            else:
                i += 1
        return lx.subs

    def plain(self):
        """The text without its heredoc bodies."""
        out, at = [], 0
        for a, b in self.spans:
            out.append(self.t[at:a])
            at = b
        return ''.join(out) + self.t[at:]

    def uncommented(self):
        """The text without its comments (#1057). Heredoc bodies and quoted text stay: only what this shell
        itself skips as a comment goes."""
        out, at = [], 0
        for a, b in self.notes:
            out.append(self.t[at:a])
            at = b
        return ''.join(out) + self.t[at:]

    def lit(self, s, quoted=False):
        if self.word is None:
            self.word = []
        if self.word and self.word[-1][0] == 'L' and self.word[-1][2] == quoted:
            self.word[-1] = ('L', self.word[-1][1] + s, quoted)
        else:
            self.word.append(('L', s, quoted))

    def piece(self, kind, text, quoted):
        if self.word is None:
            self.word = []
        self.word.append((kind, text, quoted))

    def end_word(self):
        w, self.word = self.word, None
        if w is None:
            return
        op, self.redir = self.redir, None
        if op is None:
            self.cur['words'].append(w)
        elif op in ('<<', '<<-'):   # the word is the heredoc's end marker; quoted = its body is not expanded
            mark = ''.join(p[1] if p[0] == 'L' else '$' + p[1] for p in w)
            self.pending.append((mark, self.cur, any(p[0] == 'L' and p[2] for p in w)))
        else:
            self.cur['redirs'].append((op, w))

    def end_cmd(self):
        self.end_word()
        self.redir = None
        first = self.cur['words'][0] if self.cur['words'] else None
        if first == [('L', '{', False)]:
            self.groups.append(next(GROUP_IDS))
        self.cur['groups'] = tuple(self.groups)   # a pipe into a group feeds every command in it (pipe_span)
        if first == [('L', '}', False)] and self.groups:
            self.groups.pop()
        if self.cur['words'] or self.cur['redirs'] or any(c is self.cur for _, c, _ in self.pending):
            self.cmds.append(self.cur)
        self.cur = self.new()

    def run(self):
        t, n, i = self.t, self.n, 0
        while i < n:
            m = ORD.match(t, i)
            if m:
                self.lit(m.group())
                i = m.end()
                continue
            c, nxt = t[i], t[i + 1:i + 2]
            if c in ' \t':
                self.end_word()
                i += 1
            elif c == '\n':
                self.end_cmd()
                i = self.docs(i + 1) if self.pending else i + 1
            elif c == '\\':
                if nxt != '\n':   # backslash + line break joins the lines
                    self.lit(nxt or '\\', True)
                i += 2
            elif c == "'":
                j = t.find("'", i + 1)
                j = n if j < 0 else j
                self.lit(t[i + 1:j], True)
                i = j + 1
            elif c == '"':
                i = self.dquote(i + 1)
            elif c == '$':
                i = self.dollar(i, False)
            elif c == '`':
                i = self.backtick(i, False)
            elif c == '#':
                if self.word is None:   # a comment, to the end of the line
                    j = t.find('\n', i)
                    j = n if j < 0 else j
                    self.notes.append((i, j))
                    i = j
                else:
                    self.lit(c)
                    i += 1
            elif c in '<>':
                if nxt == '(':   # <(..) >(..)
                    j = self.close(i + 1, '(', ')')
                    self.subs.append(t[i + 2:j])
                    self.piece('H', '', False)
                    i = j + 1
                    continue
                if self.word is not None and all(p[0] == 'L' and not p[2] and p[1].isdigit() for p in self.word):
                    self.word = None   # 2>file: the 2 is no argument
                self.end_word()
                self.redir = REDIR.match(t, i).group()   # the next word is its target, no argument
                i += len(self.redir)
            elif c == '&':
                if nxt == '>':
                    self.end_word()
                    self.redir = '&>'
                    i += 3 if t[i + 2:i + 3] == '>' else 2
                else:
                    self.end_cmd()
                    i += 2 if nxt == '&' else 1
            elif c == '|':
                if nxt != '|':
                    self.cur['pipe'] = True   # its output is the next command's input
                self.end_cmd()
                i += 2 if nxt in ('|', '&') else 1
            elif c == ';':
                self.end_cmd()
                i += 1
                while t[i:i + 1] in (';', '&') and t[i:i + 1]:
                    i += 1
            elif c == '(':
                w = self.word
                if w and len(w) == 1 and w[0][0] == 'L' and not w[0][2] and re.fullmatch(r'[A-Za-z_]\w*\+?=', w[0][1]):
                    self.taint.add(w[0][1].rstrip('+='))   # NAME=( .. ): an array, no single known text
                    j = self.close(i, '(', ')')
                    self.subs.append(t[i + 1:j])
                    self.word = None
                    i = j + 1
                else:
                    self.end_cmd()
                    self.depth += 1
                    self.groups.append(next(GROUP_IDS))
                    i += 1
            else:   # ')': the end of a ( .. ) group, or of a `case` pattern
                if self.depth:
                    self.end_cmd()   # the group's last command, still inside it
                    self.depth -= 1
                    self.groups.pop() if self.groups else None
                else:
                    self.cur['pat'] = True
                    self.end_cmd()
                i += 1
        self.end_cmd()

    def dquote(self, i):
        t, n = self.t, self.n
        self.lit('', True)
        while i < n:
            c = t[i]
            if c == '"':
                return i + 1
            if c == '\\' and t[i + 1:i + 2] in ('$', '`', '"', '\\', '\n') and i + 1 < n:
                if t[i + 1] != '\n':
                    self.lit(t[i + 1], True)
                i += 2
            elif c == '$':
                i = self.dollar(i, True)
            elif c == '`':
                i = self.backtick(i, True)
            else:
                m = DQ_ORD.match(t, i)
                self.lit(m.group() if m else c, True)
                i = m.end() if m else i + 1
        return n

    def dollar(self, i, q):
        t = self.t
        c = t[i + 1:i + 2]
        if c == '(':   # $(..) and $((..))
            j = self.close(i + 1, '(', ')')
            self.subs.append(t[i + 2:j])
            self.piece('H', '', q)
            return j + 1
        if c == '{':
            j = self.close(i + 1, '{', '}')
            body = t[i + 2:j]
            if NAME.fullmatch(body):
                self.piece('V', body, q)
            elif re.fullmatch(r'[@*]|[A-Za-z_]\w*\[[@*]\]', body):
                self.piece('H' if q and '*' in body else 'M', '', q)
            else:   # ${X:-y}, ${X/a/b}, ${#X}, ${!X}: unknown text; ${X:=y} also sets X
                m = NAME.match(body)
                if m and re.match(r':?=', body[m.end():]):
                    self.taint.add(m.group())
                self.subs += Lex(body).subs
                self.piece('H', '', q)
            return j + 1
        m = NAME.match(t, i + 1)
        if m:
            self.piece('V', m.group(), q)
            return m.end()
        if c in ('@', '*') and c:
            self.piece('H' if q and c == '*' else 'M', '', q)
            return i + 2
        if c and c in '0123456789?$!#-':
            self.piece('H', '', q)
            return i + 2
        if c == "'" and not q:   # $'..'
            j = i + 2
            while j < self.n and t[j] != "'":
                j += 2 if t[j] == '\\' else 1
            self.lit(ansi_c(t[i + 2:j]), True)
            return j + 1
        if c == '"' and not q:   # $".."
            return self.dquote(i + 2)
        self.lit('$', q)
        return i + 1

    def backtick(self, i, q):
        t, j = self.t, i + 1
        while j < self.n and t[j] != '`':
            j += 2 if t[j] == '\\' else 1
        self.subs.append(re.sub(r'\\([`$\\])', r'\1', t[i + 1:j]))
        self.piece('H', '', q)
        return j + 1

    def close(self, i, opener, closer):
        """Index of the bracket that closes the one at i (quotes and backslashes respected); the end if none does."""
        t, n, depth = self.t, self.n, 0
        while i < n:
            c = t[i]
            if c == '\\':
                i += 2
                continue
            if c == "'":
                j = t.find("'", i + 1)
                i = n if j < 0 else j + 1
                continue
            if c == '"':
                i += 1
                while i < n and t[i] != '"':
                    i += 2 if t[i] == '\\' else 1
                i += 1
                continue
            if c == opener:
                depth += 1
            elif c == closer:
                depth -= 1
                if depth == 0:
                    return i
            i += 1
        return n

    def docs(self, i):
        """Read the heredoc bodies that start at line start i; the index after the last one."""
        t, n = self.t, self.n
        for mark, cmd, quoted in self.pending:
            start, body = i, []
            while i < n:
                j = t.find('\n', i)
                j = n if j < 0 else j
                line, i = t[i:j], j + 1
                if line.strip() == mark.strip():
                    break
                body.append(line)
            text = '\n'.join(body)
            self.spans.append((start, min(i, n)))
            cmd['docs'].append(text)
            if not quoted:   # an unquoted marker: the shell runs $(..) and `..` in the body
                self.subs += self.expansions(text)
        self.pending = []
        return i


def parse(text):
    """(every simple command of the text, also those inside $(..) `..` <(..); the text without heredoc bodies;
    names that get a value no reader can know; the pieces left unread at the 400-piece limit, which decide_text
    refuses or marks unknown: never a shorter command list without saying so)."""
    cmds, taint, queue, plain, done = [], set(), [text], None, 0
    while queue and done < 400:
        lx = Lex(queue.pop(0))
        done += 1
        plain = lx.plain() if plain is None else plain
        for c in lx.cmds:
            c['sub'] = done > 1   # inside $(..), `..`, <(..): its output goes into another command
        cmds += lx.cmds
        taint |= lx.taint
        queue += lx.subs
    return cmds, plain, taint, queue


def word_text(w):
    """The text of a word made of literal pieces only, else None."""
    return ''.join(p[1] for p in w) if all(p[0] == 'L' for p in w) else None


def split_assign(w, any_quoting=False):
    """(name, '+' for +=, the value's pieces) when the word is NAME=value, else None. A shell wants NAME= bare;
    declare/export/local take it quoted too (`declare "X=y"`)."""
    lead = ''
    for k, (kind, text, quoted) in enumerate(w):
        if kind != 'L' or (not any_quoting and (k or quoted)):
            return None
        name, eq, rest = text.partition('=')
        if not eq:
            lead += text
            continue
        m = re.fullmatch(r'([A-Za-z_]\w*)(\+?)', lead + name)
        if not m:
            return None
        return m.group(1), m.group(2), ([('L', rest, quoted)] if rest else []) + list(w[k + 1:])
    return None


def runs_unseen_code(texts):
    """Whether eval, source, `.` or trap is the command (or a wrapper before it), not one of its arguments:
    `git add .` and `--jq .` set no variables."""
    lit = [HID if x is None else x for x in texts]
    h, _ = head(lit)
    return any(os.path.basename(x) in ('eval', 'source', '.', 'trap') for x in (lit if h is None else lit[:h + 1]))


def variables(cmds, taint):
    """What the text says about its variables. vals: name -> every value it gets anywhere in the text, as (from a
    `for`, pieces). Not by order: a name set twice has two values and both are tried, so a harmless first value
    hides nothing. Any word that looks like NAME=value counts (also after `then`, `do`, `env`). Names set from
    data (read, +=, arrays, unset) are left out, so they read as hidden. open: other code may set any name (eval,
    source, trap, declare -n), so every name is also tried as hidden. standalone: names set for the commands that
    follow (`X=y`, `export X=y`), not only for one command (`X=y cmd`)."""
    vals, taint, open_world, standalone = {}, set(taint), False, set()
    for c in cmds:
        ws = c['words']
        texts = [word_text(w) for w in ws]
        setter = any(x in SETTERS for x in texts)
        found = [a for a in (split_assign(w, setter) for w in ws) if a]
        for name, plus, value in found:
            vals.setdefault(name, []).append((False, value))
            if plus:
                taint.add(name)
            if setter or len(found) == len(ws):
                standalone.add(name)
        for n, x in enumerate(texts):
            if x == 'for' and n + 1 < len(ws):
                name = texts[n + 1]
                if name and NAME.fullmatch(name) and n + 2 < len(ws) and texts[n + 2] == 'in':
                    vals.setdefault(name, []).extend((True, w) for w in ws[n + 3:])
                elif name:
                    taint.add(name)
            if x in TAINTERS and (x != 'printf' or '-v' in texts):
                taint.update(y for y in texts[n + 1:] if y and NAME.fullmatch(y))
        if runs_unseen_code(texts) or \
                (setter and any(x and re.fullmatch(r'-[A-Za-z]*n[A-Za-z]*', x) for x in texts)):
            open_world = True
    return {'vals': {n: v for n, v in vals.items() if n not in taint}, 'open': open_world,
            'set': set(vals) | taint, 'standalone': standalone}


def var_texts(name, env, stack=()):
    """Every text $name can be; HID for what cannot be known."""
    vals = env['vals']
    if name not in vals or name in stack or len(stack) > 4:
        base = env.get('base', {}).get(name)   # $HOME as the hook got it, when the text never sets it
        if base and name not in env['set'] and not hidden(base):
            return [base, HID] if env['open'] else [base]
        return [HID]
    out = []
    for from_for, value in vals[name]:
        texts = ['']
        if from_for and any(not q and (k != 'L' or re.search(r'[{}*?\[]', t)) for k, t, q in value):
            texts = [HID]   # for x in $LIST *.sh {a,b}: the shell makes the values, they are not in the text
            value = ()
        for kind, text, _ in value:
            opts = [text] if kind == 'L' else var_texts(text, env, stack + (name,)) if kind == 'V' else \
                [WILD if kind == 'M' else HID]
            texts = [a + b for a in texts for b in opts]
            if len(texts) > 32:
                return [HID]
        out += [x for x in texts if x not in out]
    if env['open'] and HID not in out:
        out.append(HID)
    return out if len(out) <= 32 else [HID]


# Quoted text and $X values take no part in {a,b} expansion, quoted text also none in * ? [..] matching: their
# characters are swapped for private ones while a word is put together, and swapped back when it is done.
SHIELD_QUOTED = str.maketrans('{},.*?[', '')
SHIELD_VALUE = str.maketrans('{},.', '')
UNSHIELD = {v: k for k, v in SHIELD_QUOTED.items()}
GLOB = re.compile(r'[*?]|\[[^\]\s]+\]')
IFS = ' \t\n'


def finish(tok, wild):
    """A word, once put together: an unquoted {a,b} or * ? [..] becomes hidden text (the shell fills it in from
    the folder), and the word gets the WILD mark when it may come out as several words or as none."""
    while True:
        tok, k = BRACE.subn(HID, tok)
        if not k:
            break
        wild = True
    if GLOB.search(tok):
        wild = wild or GLOB.match(tok) is not None   # a leading * can match a file named like a flag
        tok = GLOB.sub(HID, tok)
    tok = tok.translate(UNSHIELD)
    return tok + WILD if wild and WILD not in tok else tok


def expand_word(w, bind):
    """The tokens one word becomes. Quoted text stays one piece. An unquoted $X with a known value is split at
    blanks (at $IFS when the text sets it). Unknown text is HID inside the word; when it is unquoted the shell may
    split it anywhere, so the word also gets the WILD mark: it may be no word at all, or several."""
    out, cur, has, wild = [], '', False, False
    ifs = bind.get('IFS', IFS)
    for kind, text, quoted in w:
        if kind == 'L':
            cur += text.translate(SHIELD_QUOTED) if quoted else text
            has = True
            continue
        val = WILD if kind == 'M' else HID if kind == 'H' else bind.get(text, HID)
        if quoted or kind == 'M':
            cur += val.translate(SHIELD_QUOTED)
            has = True
            continue
        if hidden(val) or hidden(ifs):
            cur += val.translate(SHIELD_VALUE)
            has = wild = True
            continue
        for k, part in enumerate(re.split('[' + re.escape(ifs) + ']+', val) if ifs else [val]):
            if k and has:
                out.append(finish(cur, wild))
            if k:
                cur, has, wild = '', False, False
            if part:
                cur += part.translate(SHIELD_VALUE)
                has = True
    if has:
        out.append(finish(cur, wild))
    return out


def readings(cmd, env):
    """The token lists a command can be: one per combination of the values its variables have in the text."""
    names = []
    for w in cmd['words']:
        names += [p[1] for p in w if p[0] == 'V' and p[1] not in names]
    if names and 'IFS' in env['set'] and 'IFS' not in names:
        names.append('IFS')
    opts, total = [], 1
    for name in names:
        o = var_texts(name, env)
        if name == 'IFS':   # also the value it has before the text sets it
            o = [IFS] + [x for x in o if x != IFS]
        if total * len(o) > MAX_READINGS:
            o = [HID]
        total *= len(o)
        opts.append(o)
    out = []
    for combo in itertools.product(*opts):
        bind, toks = dict(zip(names, combo)), []
        for w in cmd['words']:
            toks += expand_word(w, bind)
        out.append(toks)
    braced = brace_reading(cmd['words'], out[0] if len(out) == 1 else None)
    return out + ([braced] if braced else [])


def brace_reading(words, plain):
    """One more reading when a word of only literal text has an unquoted {a,b} or {1..3}: the words the shell
    makes of it (`{bash,run.sh}` runs `bash run.sh`, `b{a,}sh f` runs `bash bsh f`). The first reading keeps such
    a word as hidden text; this one lets the rest of the guard see a command word or a file it spells. Only when
    the command has no variables (plain: its one reading), and at most 32 words come out of one word."""
    if plain is None or not any(not q and k == 'L' and BRACE.search(t) for w in words for k, t, q in w):
        return None
    toks = []
    for w in words:
        if not all(k == 'L' for k, _, _ in w) or not any(not q and BRACE.search(t) for _, t, q in w):
            toks += expand_word(w, {})
            continue
        texts = [''.join(t.translate(SHIELD_QUOTED) if q else t for _, t, q in w)]
        while any(BRACE.search(x) for x in texts):
            texts = [y for x in texts for y in brace_once(x)]
            if len(texts) > 32:
                return None
        toks += [finish(x, False) for x in texts if x]   # an empty unquoted result is no word
    return toks


def brace_once(text):
    """The texts one (innermost) brace of text makes: {a,b,} parts, {1..3} / {a..e} / {1..9..2} ranges; a range
    bash does not expand stays as it is (with its braces swapped for private ones, so it is not tried again)."""
    m = BRACE.search(text)
    pre, body, post = text[:m.start()], m.group()[1:-1], text[m.end():]
    if ',' in body:
        return [pre + x + post for x in body.split(',')]
    r = re.fullmatch(r'(-?\d+|[A-Za-z])\.\.(-?\d+|[A-Za-z])(?:\.\.(-?\d+))?', body)
    if r and r.group(1).lstrip('-').isdigit() == r.group(2).lstrip('-').isdigit():
        num = r.group(1).lstrip('-').isdigit()
        a, b = (int(r.group(1)), int(r.group(2))) if num else (ord(r.group(1)), ord(r.group(2)))
        step = abs(int(r.group(3) or 1)) or 1
        vals = range(a, b + 1, step) if a <= b else range(a, b - 1, -step)
        if len(vals) <= 32:
            return [pre + (str(v) if num else chr(v)) + post for v in vals]
    return [pre + m.group().translate(SHIELD_QUOTED) + post]


def segments(cmd):
    """One token list per simple command. gh-as (an app-token wrapper of gh) relies on this: its text has
    one word per gh argument, and each must stay one token here."""
    cmds, _, taint, _ = parse(cmd)
    env = variables(cmds, taint)
    return [r[0] for r in (readings(c, env) for c in cmds) if r[0]]


def quote(tok):
    return "'" + tok.replace("'", "'\\''") + "'"


ASSIGN_RE = re.compile(r'^[A-Za-z_]\w*(\[[^\]]*\])?=')
DUR_RE = re.compile(r'^\d+(\.\d+)?[smhd]?$')


def is_assign(tok):
    return ASSIGN_RE.match(tok) is not None


def is_dur(tok):
    return DUR_RE.match(tok) is not None or tok == '{}'


_HEADS = {}   # head() of a word list: one shell text asks it of each command ~20 times (a 400 KB script: 1 M calls)


def head(toks):
    """(index of the command word after assignments and wrappers, which of shell/eval/xargs wrapped it). The words
    alone decide it, so the answer is kept (a frozenset, so no caller changes a kept answer)."""
    key = tuple(toks)
    got = _HEADS.get(key)
    if got is None:
        if len(_HEADS) > 20_000:
            _HEADS.clear()
        h, via = head_of(key)
        got = _HEADS[key] = (h, frozenset(via))
    return got


def head_of(toks):
    i, via, prev_flag, script_next = 0, set(), False, False
    while i < len(toks):
        t = toks[i]
        base = os.path.basename(t)
        if prev_flag:
            prev_flag = False
        elif is_assign(t) or t in ('export', 'local', 'declare'):
            pass
        elif t == 'function':   # function NAME { ..
            i += 1
        # A wrapper only under its own name or in a system folder (`./sudo` is a file of the project), and not as
        # the word a shell takes as its script (`bash nice` runs the file `nice` of the folder).
        elif base in WRAPPERS and not script_next and ('/' not in t or os.path.dirname(t) in STD_BINS):
            if base in SHELLS or base in ('source', '.'):
                via.add('shell')
                script_next = base != 'busybox'
            if base in ('eval', 'xargs'):
                via.add(base)
        elif t.startswith('-'):
            prev_flag = t in ARG_FLAGS
        elif is_dur(t):
            pass
        elif base == 'uv' and i + 1 < len(toks) and toks[i + 1] == 'run':
            i += 1
            via.add('shell')
        else:
            return i, via
        i += 1
    return None, via


def positional(args, valflags):
    out, i = [], 0
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        if a in valflags:
            i += 2
            continue
        if not a.startswith('-'):
            out.append(a)
        i += 1
    return out


# ---------- guarded paths

def forms(tok):
    """The spellings of a path a server may take as the same: percent-decoded, /./ and /x/../ resolved."""
    out, t = [tok], tok
    for _ in range(3):
        u = unquote(t)
        if u == t:
            break
        out.append(u)
        t = u
    for s in list(out):
        r = re.sub(r'(?<!:)/(?:\.?/)+', '/', re.sub(r'/\.(?=[?#]|$)', '', s))
        k = 1
        while k:   # every /x/.. (no round limit, as in canon())
            r, k = re.subn(r'/(?!\.\.?/)[^/?#]+/\.\.(?=/|$|[?#])', '', r)
        if r not in out:
            out.append(r)
    return out


def spells(tok, want):
    """Whether a word is `want`, or has hidden parts that can make it so."""
    if not hidden(tok):
        return tok == want
    rx = '.*'.join(re.escape(x) for x in re.split(f'[{HID}{WILD}]+', tok))
    return re.fullmatch(rx, want, re.S | re.I) is not None


def seg_match(segs, pat, more):
    """Whether path words with hidden ones among them can spell pat. A hidden word stands for one or more words
    of pat (a variable can hold `a/b`), and at least one of pat's own words must be visible."""
    last = len(segs) - 1

    def can(s, want):
        return want is None or spells(s, want)

    def go(si, pi, seen):
        if pi == len(pat):
            return seen and (more or si > last)
        if si > last:
            return False
        if pat[pi] is ...:
            return any(go(sj, pi + 1, seen) for sj in range(si + 1, last + 2))
        s = segs[si]
        if not can(s, pat[pi]):
            return False
        if not hidden(s):
            return go(si + 1, pi + 1, seen or pat[pi] is not None)
        seen = seen or (pat[pi] is not None and len(s.replace(HID, '').replace(WILD, '')) >= 3)
        return any(go(si + 1, pj, seen) for pj in range(pi + 1, len(pat) + 1))

    return any(go(i, 0, False) for i in range(len(segs)))


def url_path(form):
    """A path or URL (one form of a word) without its scheme and host, and without its query string or fragment."""
    return re.split(r'[?#]', re.sub(r'^[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*://[^/]*', '', form, flags=re.I), maxsplit=1)[0]


def path_cuts(path):
    """The paths a request may go to: the path, and the path cut at each hidden or glob part. An unquoted `?` is a
    glob to the shell (left as written when it matches no file) and `$Q` may hold one, so the query string may start
    at any of them: `graphql?x=1` and `.../protection"$Q"x` are the GraphQL endpoint and the protection. Every path
    check reads its path through this (protect_kinds, may_be_graphql)."""
    return [path] + [path[:i].rstrip('/') for i, c in enumerate(path) if c in (HID, WILD)]


def hits(tok, kind):
    """2: the word spells the guarded path; 1: hidden text in it could complete it; 0: neither."""
    rx, pats = GUARDED[kind]
    best = 0
    for f in forms(tok):
        if rx.search(f):
            return 2
        g = PLACEHOLDER.sub(HID, f)
        if hidden(g):   # also cut at each hidden or glob part: an unquoted `?` may start the query string
            for path in path_cuts(url_path(g)):
                segs = [x.lower() for x in path.split('/') if x]
                if rx.search(path) or any(seg_match(segs, pat, more) for pat, more in pats):
                    best = 1
    return best


def far_hits(tok, kind):
    """hits() for the word as written and as the far end may read it (\\uXXXX, %XX and backslashes decoded)."""
    best = hits(tok, kind)
    if best < 2 and ('\\' in tok or '%' in tok):
        best = max(best, hits(canon(tok), kind))
    return best


def visible_path(tok):
    """Whether a path shows any text at all besides hidden parts and gh placeholders."""
    return re.search(r'[A-Za-z0-9]', PLACEHOLDER.sub('', tok).replace(HID, '').replace(WILD, '')) is not None


GRAPHQL_END = re.compile(r'(^|/)graphql/?$', re.I)


def may_be_graphql(tok):
    """Whether a `gh api` path can be the GraphQL endpoint: it ends in `graphql` in some spelling, hidden text in
    it can complete that word, or it shows no text at all. `repos/o/r/issues/comments/$id` cannot."""
    if not visible_path(tok):
        return True
    for f in forms(PLACEHOLDER.sub(HID, tok)):
        path = url_path(f)
        if any(GRAPHQL_END.search(p.rstrip(HID + WILD)) for p in path_cuts(path)):
            return True
        if hidden(path) and seg_match([x.lower() for x in path.split('/') if x], ['graphql'], False):
            return True
    return False


def certain_path(args):
    """The path of a `gh api` call the guard cannot read fully, when the path itself is still certain: every word
    before the first word that is no flag reads cleanly, and that word starts with plain text. gh api takes exactly
    one path, so hidden text after it can add flags but cannot swap the path. None when it is not certain."""
    if len(args) > 200:
        return None
    for i, a in enumerate(args):
        _, pos, bad = api_parse(args[:i] + ['x'])
        if bad or len(pos) > 1:
            return None
        if pos == ['x']:   # this word is no flag value
            if a.startswith('-') and api_parse(args[:i] + ['--include'])[1] != ['--include']:
                continue   # a flag
            return None if a[:1] in ('', HID, WILD) else a
    return None


def override_header(value):
    """Whether a header (key:value) may change the request method on the server."""
    name = value.partition(':')[0]
    return hidden(name) or 'method' in name.lower()


# ---------- gh

def looks_risky(text):
    """Whether text that is no command line (a config, a request body) mentions what this guard refuses."""
    t = re.sub(r'["\'\\]', '', text or '')
    return LOOSE_RE.search(t) is not None or GQL_RE.search(t) is not None


def yaml_text(s):
    s = s.strip()
    if len(s) > 1 and s[0] == s[-1] == "'":
        return s[1:-1].replace("''", "'")
    if len(s) > 1 and s[0] == s[-1] == '"':
        return ansi_c(s[1:-1])
    return s


def gh_config(ctx):
    """(the gh aliases [(the words of the name, the expansion)], whether a config may hold an alias this reader
    missed) from the gh config of the hook's environment and of every config folder the command text points gh
    at (GH_CONFIG_DIR=.. gh ..). A name can be several words (`pr wd` is `gh pr wd`)."""
    env = ctx['env']
    dirs = [env.get('GH_CONFIG_DIR') or os.path.join(env.get('XDG_CONFIG_HOME') or os.path.join(
        env.get('HOME', os.path.expanduser('~')), '.config'), 'gh')] + list(ctx.get('cfg', ()))
    memo, key = ctx.setdefault('memo', {}), ('gh config', tuple(dirs))
    if key not in memo:
        memo[key] = gh_config_cached(dirs, ctx)
    return list(memo[key][0]), memo[key][1], memo[key][2]


YAML_KEY = r"""(?:[A-Za-z0-9_][\w .-]*?|'[^'\\]*'|"[^"\\]*")"""
YAML_ODD = re.compile(r'[!&*{\[|>?%@`"\']|- |:(?:\s|$)')   # the start of a value this reader does not follow


def yaml_value_plain(v):
    """Whether the value on a key's line is one of the few forms the reader below reads the way gh does: plain
    text, one quoted text ('..' or ".." with \\\\ \\" \\n \\t only), or the start of a | or > block."""
    v = v.strip()
    if not v or re.fullmatch(r'[|>][+-]?\d*', v):
        return True
    if v[0] in '"\'':
        return re.fullmatch(r"'(?:[^']|'')*'" + '|' + r'"(?:[^"\\]|\\[\\"nt])*"', v) is not None
    return not YAML_ODD.match(v)


def odd_yaml(text):
    """Whether a gh config holds YAML outside the small subset the reader below follows: tags (`!!str`), `? ` keys,
    anchors and `*` references, flow maps, escapes in keys, quoted values over several lines, tabs, `---`. The
    guard does not try to read more YAML; it refuses a gh word that may be an alias from such a config."""
    top = inside = block = False
    base = None
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if not line.strip() or line.lstrip().startswith('#'):
            continue
        indent = len(line) - len(line.lstrip())
        if '\t' in line[:indent]:
            return True
        if not indent:
            m = re.fullmatch(r'(%s)\s*:(?:\s+(.*))?\s*' % YAML_KEY, line)
            if not m or not yaml_value_plain(m.group(2) or ''):
                return True
            value = (m.group(2) or '').strip()
            top, inside, base, block = True, m.group(1).strip('"\'') == 'aliases', None, value[:1] in ('|', '>')
            if inside and value:   # aliases: {..}, aliases: *x
                return True
        elif not top:   # the whole map is indented: the reader below looks for `aliases:` at the line start
            return True
        elif not inside or (block and base is not None and indent > base):
            continue
        elif base is None or indent <= base:
            m = re.fullmatch(r'\s+(%s)\s*:(?:\s+(.*))?\s*' % YAML_KEY, line)
            if not m or not yaml_value_plain(m.group(2) or '') or (base is not None and indent != base):
                return True
            base, block = indent, (m.group(2) or '').strip()[:1] in ('|', '>')
        elif YAML_ODD.match(line.strip()):   # a plain value that goes on in the next line
            return True
    return False


def gh_config_now(dirs):
    out, risky, odd = [], False, False
    for cfg in dict.fromkeys(dirs):
        path = os.path.join(cfg, 'config.yml')
        text = read_text(path)
        if text is None:
            risky = risky or os.path.exists(path)   # too big, binary, no file: it cannot be read here, gh can
            continue
        found, inside, cur, base = [], False, None, 0
        for line in text.splitlines():
            m = re.match(r'^(["\']?)aliases\1\s*:\s*(.*)$', line)
            if m:
                inside, cur = True, None
                for k, v in re.findall(r'["\']?([^,{}:"\']+)["\']?\s*:\s*([^,{}]*)', m.group(2)):   # aliases: {a: b}
                    found.append([k.split(), yaml_text(v)])
                continue
            if not inside or not line.strip() or line.lstrip().startswith('#'):
                continue
            if not line.startswith((' ', '\t')):
                inside = False
                continue
            indent = len(line) - len(line.lstrip())
            m = re.match(r'^\s+(["\']?)([^:"\']+?)\1\s*:(?:\s+(.*)|\s*)$', line)
            if m and (cur is None or indent <= base):
                base, value = indent, (m.group(3) or '').strip()
                cur = [m.group(2).split(), '' if re.fullmatch(r'[|>][+-]?\d*', value) else yaml_text(value)]
                found.append(cur)
            elif cur is not None:   # a value on the next lines (| and > blocks)
                cur[1] = (cur[1] + '\n' + line.strip()).strip()
        out += [(w, e) for w, e in found if w]
        risky = risky or (looks_risky(text) and not any(looks_risky(e) for _, e in found))
        odd = odd or odd_yaml(text)
    return out, risky, odd


def alias_forms(exp):
    """An alias text as written and with its line breaks as blanks (a YAML `>` block folds them; gh splits a
    plain alias at any blank)."""
    exp = exp.strip()
    return list(dict.fromkeys([exp, ' '.join(exp.split())]))


def alias_text_reason(exp, ctx):
    for x in alias_forms(exp):
        # the bare text is read as a command too (a `!` the reader lost); its words are no command of this text,
        # so it has its own flow and no gate on unknown words
        r = decide_text(x[1:], ctx) if x.startswith('!') else \
            decide_text('gh ' + x, ctx) or decide_text(x, dict(ctx, flow={}, nogate=True))
        if r:
            return r
    return None


def sub_candidates(args, value_flags=('-R', '--repo'), switches=('--help', '--version', '-h')):
    """Indexes of the words that may be the subcommand. cobra takes the first word that is no flag and no flag
    value, and which flags take a value depends on the command, so after a flag this table does not know both
    readings count; a hidden word may be a flag too."""
    out, value, maybe = [], False, False
    for i, a in enumerate(args):
        if value:
            value = False
            continue
        if a.startswith('-') and len(a) > 1:
            value = a in value_flags
            maybe = not value and a not in switches and '=' not in a
            continue
        out.append(i)
        if not maybe and not hidden(a):
            break
        maybe = False
    return out


def gh_reason(args, ctx, sure=True):
    """Why these gh arguments are refused, or None. sure: the command word is `gh` for certain (not a hidden
    word or a shell function that may be gh)."""
    top = sure and ctx['depth'] == 0
    cands = sub_candidates(args)
    says = re.search(r'disable|actions/permissions', re.sub(r'["\'\\]', '', ctx.get('all') or ''), re.I)
    # A config folder nobody can read here may hold any alias. An alias cannot take the name of a gh command, so
    # `GH_CONFIG_DIR=$(mktemp -d) gh pr list` passes; a word that is no gh command may be an alias.
    if top and ctx.get('cfg_hidden') and cands and (says or args[cands[0]] not in GH_COMMANDS):
        return reason('`gh` with a config folder the guard cannot read (GH_CONFIG_DIR, XDG_CONFIG_HOME or HOME '
                      'set from a variable or command output) and a word that is no gh command, or in a command '
                      'that names `disable` or `actions/permissions`: the folder may hold an alias that disables '
                      'Actions', 'Write the folder out in the command.')
    if top and cands and hidden(args[cands[0]]) and says:
        return reason('`gh` with a subcommand the guard cannot read (an array, a variable, "$@"), in a command '
                      'that also names `disable` or `actions/permissions`', UNREADABLE)
    for i in cands:
        sub, after = args[i], args[i + 1:]
        guess = hidden(sub)   # `gh "$X" ..`: X may be workflow or api
        for name in (('workflow', 'api', 'alias') if guess else (sub,)):
            r = gh_sub_reason(name, args[:i] + after, after, ctx, guess, top)
            if r:
                return r
    aliases, risky, odd = gh_config(ctx)
    aliases += ctx.get('gh_set', [])
    sub_ctx = dict(ctx, depth=ctx['depth'] + 1)
    first = args[cands[0]] if cands else ''
    if ctx['depth'] >= MAX_DEPTH:   # too deep to expand an alias: what it stands for is read only for tokens
        if not first or first in GH_COMMANDS:
            return None
        exps = [e for _, e in aliases]
        if risky or odd or any(hidden(e) for e in exps):
            return reason(f'`gh {redact(first)}` may be an alias, in a script that scripts run, too deep for the '
                          'guard to expand, and the gh config is one it cannot read in full')
        return stopped('\n'.join(exps), f'`gh {redact(first)}` may be an alias that names disabling Actions, in a '
                       'script that scripts run, too deep for the guard to expand', ctx['flow'])
    for words, exp in aliases:
        if words is None:   # an alias of any name
            if not first or first in GH_COMMANDS:
                continue
            j = cands[0]
        else:
            at = 0
            for j, a in enumerate(args):
                if spells(a, words[at]):
                    at += 1
                    if at == len(words):
                        break
            else:
                continue
        rest = ' '.join(quote(a) for a in args[j + 1:])
        if any(decide_text(x[1:] if x.startswith('!') else f'gh {x} {rest}', sub_ctx) for x in alias_forms(exp)):
            return reason(f'the gh alias `{" ".join(words or ["?"])}` expands to a command that disables Actions')
    if risky and sure and first and first not in GH_COMMANDS:   # also when it is an alias this reader did find
        return reason(f'`gh {redact(first)}` is no gh command, and the gh config holds text about disabling Actions '
                      'that the guard cannot read as an alias', 'Tell the owner: the gh config needs a look.')
    if odd and sure and first and first not in GH_COMMANDS:
        return reason(f'`gh {redact(first)}` is no gh command, and the gh config holds YAML the guard does not read '
                      '(a tag like `!!str`, a `? ` key, an anchor, a flow map, an escape in a key ..), so the word '
                      'may be an alias the guard cannot see', 'Tell the owner: the gh config needs a look.')
    return None


def gh_sub_reason(sub, rest, after, ctx, guess, top):
    if sub == 'workflow':
        for j in sub_candidates(after):
            if after[j] == 'disable':
                return reason('`gh workflow disable` disables a GitHub Actions workflow')
            if hidden(after[j]) and spells(after[j], 'disable') and not guess:
                return reason('`gh workflow` with a subcommand the guard cannot read (it may be `disable`)',
                              UNREADABLE)
        return None
    if sub == 'api':
        return api_reason(rest, ctx, top)
    if sub == 'alias':
        verb = [a for a in after if not a.startswith('-')][:1]
        tail = after[after.index(verb[0]) + 1:] if verb else []
        pos = [a for a in tail if a == '-' or not a.startswith('-')]   # --clobber and -s/--shell are switches
        # blind: the call is not known to be `gh alias <verb>` (a hidden word stands where `alias` or the verb
        # would be), or it only prints help. Then only alias text that can be read and disables is refused.
        blind = guess or not verb or hidden(verb[0]) or bool({'--help', '-h'} & set(tail))
        if verb and (verb[0] == 'set' or hidden(verb[0]) and not guess):
            exp = ' '.join(pos[1:])
            if exp in ('-', ''):   # the alias text comes on stdin
                known = ctx.get('stdin')
                if exp == '-' and not blind and (known is None or hidden(known) or SHELL_VALUE.search(known)):
                    return reason('`gh alias set .. -` with alias text the guard cannot read (a pipe, a file that '
                                  'is not there yet, a variable)', 'Write the alias text out in the command.')
                if (known is not None and alias_text_reason(known, ctx)) or looks_risky(stdin_texts(ctx)[0]):
                    return reason('`gh alias set` from stdin with an alias that disables Actions')
                return None
            if hidden(exp) and blind:
                return None
            if hidden(exp):
                return reason('a gh alias with text the guard cannot read (a variable or command output)',
                              'Write the alias text out in the command.')
            if any(a == '--shell' or re.fullmatch(r'-[a-z]*s[a-z]*', a) for a in tail) and not exp.startswith('!'):
                exp = '!' + exp
            if alias_text_reason(exp, ctx):
                return reason('a gh alias that disables Actions')
        if verb and (verb[0] == 'import' or hidden(verb[0]) and not guess):
            src = pos[0] if pos else '-'
            text = ctx.get('stdin') if src == '-' else read_named(src, ctx)
            if text is None and not blind:
                return reason('`gh alias import` of a file or pipe the guard cannot read',
                              'Import a file that is there before the command runs.')
            for _, exp in yaml_aliases(text or ''):
                if alias_text_reason(exp, ctx):
                    return reason('`gh alias import` of an alias that disables Actions')
            if looks_risky(text if text is not None else ctx.get('raw')):   # also what the lines above cannot read
                return reason('`gh alias import` of text about disabling Actions')
    return None


def yaml_aliases(text):
    """[(the words of the name, the expansion)] of the `name: expansion` lines of a gh alias import file."""
    out = []
    for line in text.splitlines():
        m = re.match(r'^\s*(["\']?)([^:#"\']+)\1\s*:\s*(.*)$', line)
        if m and m.group(2).split():
            out.append((m.group(2).split(), yaml_text(m.group(3))))
    return out


def text_aliases(toks, stdin, ctx):
    """The gh aliases a command of the text sets up itself (`gh alias set`, `gh alias import`): they are not in the
    gh config yet when the guard reads the text, and a later command of the same text may use them. A name of
    None stands for any name (an import the guard cannot read)."""
    for i, t in enumerate(toks):
        if os.path.basename(t) != 'gh':
            continue
        rest = [a for a in toks[i + 1:] if a == '-' or not a.startswith('-')]
        if rest[:2] == ['alias', 'set'] and len(rest) > 3:
            exp = ' '.join(rest[3:])
            exp = (HID if stdin is None or SHELL_VALUE.search(stdin) else stdin) if exp == '-' else exp
            shell = any(a == '--shell' or re.fullmatch(r'-[a-z]*s[a-z]*', a) for a in toks[i + 1:])
            return [(rest[2].split() or None, '!' + exp if shell and not exp.startswith('!') else exp)]
        if rest[:2] == ['alias', 'import']:
            src = rest[2] if len(rest) > 2 else '-'
            text = stdin if src == '-' else read_named(src, ctx)
            return [(None, HID)] if text is None else yaml_aliases(text)
    return []


def stdin_texts(ctx):
    """What a command reads on stdin: its heredoc or <<< text; when it comes from a pipe or a file, the command
    text itself stands in (what is piped in is most often written there)."""
    return [ctx['stdin']] if ctx.get('stdin') is not None else [ctx.get('raw') or '']


def file_or_text(path, ctx):
    """A file the command names, or the command text when the file is not there to read (it may be written by
    the same command)."""
    text = read_named(path, ctx)
    return ctx.get('raw') or '' if text is None else text


def api_parse(args):
    """`gh api` arguments as gh's flag reader (pflag) takes them: (flags [(long name, value)], positionals, what
    went wrong or ''). `--` ends the flags; `--name=value` or `--name value`; short flags join (`-iXPUT`), a short
    flag's value is the rest of the word (`-XPUT`, `-X=PUT`) or the next word; a flag that takes a value takes the
    next word whatever it looks like (`-p -XGET`: the preview is "-XGET")."""
    flags, pos, bad = [], [], ''
    if any(WILD in a for a in args):
        bad = 'an unquoted variable or command output, which may add flags: put it in double quotes'
    i, n = 0, len(args)
    while i < n:
        a = args[i]
        i += 1
        if a == '--':
            pos += args[i:]
            break
        if not a.startswith('-') or a == '-':
            pos.append(a)
            continue
        if a.startswith('--'):
            name, eq, val = a[2:].partition('=')
            if hidden(name):
                bad = bad or 'a flag name the guard cannot read'
            elif name in API_SWITCH:
                pass
            elif name not in API_VALUE:
                bad = bad or f'the flag --{redact(name)}, which gh api {GH_VERSION} does not have'
            elif eq:
                flags.append((name, val))
            elif i < n:
                flags.append((name, args[i]))
                i += 1
            else:
                bad = bad or f'--{name} without its value'
            continue
        s = a[1:]
        while s:
            c, s = s[0], s[1:]
            if c in API_SHORT_SWITCH:
                s = '' if s[:1] == '=' else s   # -i=false
                continue
            if c not in API_SHORT:
                bad = bad or ('a flag the guard cannot read' if c in (HID, WILD) else
                              f'the flag -{c}, which gh api {GH_VERSION} does not have')
                break
            if len(s) > 1 and s[0] == '=':
                val = s[1:]
            elif s:
                val = s
            elif i < n:
                val = args[i]
                i += 1
            else:
                bad = bad or f'-{c} without its value'
                break
            flags.append((API_SHORT[c], val))
            s = ''
    return flags, pos, bad


def api_reason(args, ctx, top=False):
    flags, pos, bad = api_parse(args)
    if not bad and len(pos) > 1 and any(a[:1] in (HID, WILD) for a in pos):
        bad = 'a hidden word that may be a flag'   # `gh api "$X" path`: X may be -XPUT
    exact = not bad
    method, fields, inputs, typed = None, [], [], []
    override = any('x-http-method-override' in a.lower() for a in args)
    for name, val in flags:
        if name == 'method':
            method = val
        elif name in ('field', 'raw-field'):
            fields.append(val)
            typed.append((name == 'field', val))
        elif name == 'input':
            inputs.append(val)
        elif name == 'header' and override_header(val):
            override = True
    eff = 'POST' if method is None and (fields or inputs) else 'GET' if method is None else \
        '?' if hidden(method) else method.upper()
    if override and eff in READS:
        eff = 'POST'
    write = not exact or eff not in READS
    cands = pos if exact else args   # an unreadable call: any word may be the path
    how = f'a {eff} with' if exact and eff != '?' else 'a call the guard cannot read fully ' \
        f'({bad or "the method is hidden"}), so it counts as a write, with'
    best = {k: max([far_hits(a, k) for a in cands] or [0]) for k in GUARDED}
    if best['disable'] == 2 or (best['disable'] and write):
        return reason('a call to the GitHub `.../actions/workflows/<id>/disable` endpoint' if best['disable'] == 2
                      else f'{how} `gh api` to a path that hidden text can turn into `.../actions/workflows/<id>/'
                      'disable`', '' if best['disable'] == 2 else UNREADABLE)
    if best['perm'] and write:
        if exact and eff == 'PUT' and not inputs and not override and len(pos) == 1 and REPO_PERM_RE.match(pos[0]) \
                and not hidden(pos[0]) and reenables(fields):
            return None   # re-enabling Actions for a repo
        return reason(f'{how} `gh api` to GitHub `.../actions/permissions` (turns Actions off or limits it)',
                      '' if exact and best['perm'] == 2 and eff != '?' else UNREADABLE)
    if write:
        srcs = [('', s) for s in inputs] + [(k + '=', v[1:]) for k, _, v in (f.partition('=') for f in fields)
                                            if v.startswith('@')]   # -F key=@file reads the value from a file
        known = [(pre, ctx.get('stdin') if s == '-' else read_named(s, ctx)) for pre, s in srcs]
        unread = any(b is None or hidden(b) for _, b in known)
        # a file that is not there yet is often written by the same command, so its text is in the command
        bodies = [ctx.get('raw') or '' if b is None else pre + b.strip() for pre, b in known]
        seen = [canon(x) for x in list(args) + bodies]   # as the far end reads them: \u0064isabled is disabled
        if best['protect'] and (not exact or eff in ('DELETE', '?')):
            return reason(f'{how} `gh api` to branch protection or a ruleset: a DELETE there removes the rules '
                          'that keep `main` behind CI and review (changing them with PUT/PATCH/POST is not '
                          'refused)', '' if exact and eff == 'DELETE' else UNREADABLE)
        if best['ruleset'] and (any(re.fullmatch(r'enforcement=(disabled|evaluate|.*[%s%s].*)' % (HID, WILD), f,
                                                 re.S | re.I) for f in fields)
                                or any(RULESET_OFF.search(canon(x, cue=False)) for x in list(args) + bodies)):
            return reason(f'{how} `gh api` that switches a ruleset off (enforcement=disabled or evaluate)')
        if best['ruleset'] and unread:
            return reason(f'{how} `gh api` to a ruleset with a body the guard cannot read (a pipe, a file that is '
                          'not there yet, a file named by a variable): it may switch the ruleset off',
                          'Write the body to a file in a command before this one and name the file in full.')
        if best['protect'] and exact:   # a write that leaves no required check (DELETE and unread ones are above)
            kinds = set().union(*[protect_kinds(a) for a in pos]) if pos else set(ALL_KINDS)
            if inputs:   # with --input the fields go into the query string
                parsed = [json_body(b) for _, b in known[:len(inputs)]]
            else:
                parsed = [gh_fields_body(typed, ctx)]
            why = emptying_reason(kinds, parsed, '?' if override else eff, f'{how} `gh api` that sends')
            if why:
                return why
        # GraphQL only when the path can be the GraphQL endpoint: a comment posted to `repos/o/r/issues/$n/comments`
        # with its text from a file named by a variable is no GraphQL call
        path = certain_path(args)
        gql = any(may_be_graphql(a) for a in pos) if exact else path is None or may_be_graphql(path)
        if gql and any(GQL_RE.search(x) for x in seen):
            return reason('a GraphQL mutation that deletes branch protection or a ruleset')
        # the off word may stand alone in a variable (-F e=EVALUATE, `"variables": {"e": "EVALUATE"}`): read without
        # the cue words, as for a ruleset body (canon() blanks text that names no guarded word)
        if gql and any(GQL_UPDATE.search(x) for x in seen) and (unread or any(
                OFF_WORD.search(canon(x, cue=False)) for x in list(args) + bodies)):
            return reason('a GraphQL updateRepositoryRuleset that switches the ruleset off (enforcement: DISABLED '
                          'or EVALUATE), '
                          'or one with a body the guard cannot read')
        if gql:   # gh sends every -f/-F field but `query` as a GraphQL variable (input[rules][][type]=.. nests)
            hid_q = next((f for _, f in typed if spells(f.partition('=')[0], 'query') and (
                hidden(f.partition('=')[0]) or gql_hidden(f.partition('=')[2]))), None)
            if hid_q is not None:
                return reason('a GraphQL call whose query (or a field name) the guard cannot read (command output, '
                              'a variable): it may delete or empty branch protection or a ruleset',
                              'Write the query to a file in a command before this one and pass it with '
                              '`-F query=@<file>`; give ids and numbers as variables, never inside the query: '
                              '`-F n="$N"` with `query($n: Int!)` and `number: $n`, `-f id="$ID"` with `$id: ID!`.')
            vb = gh_fields_body([(m, f) for m, f in typed if f.partition('=')[0] not in ('query', 'operationName')],
                                ctx, keep_hidden=True)
            gvars = [vb] + [j['variables'] for _, b in known[:len(inputs)] for j in [json_body(b)]
                            if isinstance(j, dict) and j.get('variables') is not None]
            why = gql_empties([canon(gql_code(x), cue=False) for x in list(args) + bodies], gvars)
            if why:
                return reason(f'a GraphQL update of branch protection or a ruleset that {why}: that takes `main` '
                              'out from behind CI as surely as a delete',
                              'An update that keeps (or adds) the required checks passes.')
        if gql and unread:
            return reason('a GraphQL call with a body the guard cannot read (a pipe, a file that is not there yet, '
                          'a file named by a variable): it may delete branch protection or a ruleset',
                          'Write the query to a file in a command before this one and name the file in full.')
    if top and HIDDEN_PATH_RULE and write and (eff not in READS or any(WILD in a for a in args)) \
            and not any(visible_path(a) for a in pos):
        return reason(f'{how} `gh api` to a path that is hidden altogether (a variable or command output), so the '
                      'guard cannot tell it from the Actions settings', UNREADABLE)
    return None


def reenables(fields):
    """Whether gh api fields are exactly `enabled=true`, with at most `allowed_actions=all`."""
    seen = set()
    for f in fields:
        k, _, v = f.partition('=')
        if hidden(f) or (k, v) not in (('enabled', 'true'), ('allowed_actions', 'all')):
            return False
        seen.add(k)
    return 'enabled' in seen


# ---------- writes that empty branch protection or a ruleset (the guard cannot ask GitHub what is there now, so a
# write that replaces the checks must name at least one; one that only adds checks passes)

PROTECT_KINDS = (('ctx', re.compile(r'branches/.+/protection/required_status_checks/contexts$')),
                 ('rsc', re.compile(r'branches/.+/protection/required_status_checks$')),
                 ('prot', re.compile(r'branches/.+/protection$')),
                 ('ruleset', re.compile(r'(?:^|/)rulesets/[^/]+$')))
ALL_KINDS = frozenset(k for k, _ in PROTECT_KINDS)
GQL_EMPTYING = re.compile(r'updateRepositoryRuleset|updateBranchProtectionRule', re.I)
# in a GraphQL text: a field set to nothing, or to a $variable (looked up in the variables)
GQL_EMPTY = re.compile(r'\b(requiresStatusChecks|requiredStatusCheckContexts|requiredStatusChecks|rules)\s*'
                       r'["\']?\s*:\s*(?:(false|null|\[[\s,]*\])|\$(\w+))', re.I)   # commas are blanks: [,] is []


def protect_kinds(tok):
    """Which protection endpoints a path can be: the whole protection (a PUT replaces it), its required checks, its
    list of checks, one ruleset. Hidden text at the end can be any of them. The query string is split off first,
    also where it may start at hidden text: an unquoted `?` is a glob to the shell (left as written when it matches
    no file) and `$Q` may hold one, so `.../protection?x=1` and `.../protection"$Q"x` are the protection too."""
    out = set()
    for f in set(forms(PLACEHOLDER.sub(HID, tok))) | ({canon(tok, cue=False)} if '\\' in tok else set()):
        path = url_path(f).rstrip('/').lower()
        if path[-1:] in (HID, WILD):
            return set(ALL_KINDS)
        out |= {k for k, rx in PROTECT_KINDS for p in path_cuts(path) if rx.search(p.replace(WILD, HID))}
    return out


def gh_fields_body(typed, ctx, keep_hidden=False):
    """The JSON body `gh api` builds from -f/-F fields (key[sub]=v, key[]=v, `key[]` for an empty list; -F reads
    true/false/null/numbers and @file), or None when part of it cannot be read. keep_hidden: a value with hidden
    text stays in as that text (an id from a variable); a hidden key is still None."""
    root = {}
    for magic, f in typed:
        if hidden(f) and not (keep_hidden and not hidden(f.partition('=')[0])):
            return None
        key, eq, val = f.partition('=')
        m = re.fullmatch(r'([^\[\]]+)((?:\[[^\[\]]*\])*)', key)
        if not m:
            return None
        parts = [m.group(1)] + re.findall(r'\[([^\[\]]*)\]', m.group(2))
        if not eq:
            if parts[-1] != '':
                return None
            value, parts = [], parts[:-1]
        elif magic and val.startswith('@'):
            value = ctx.get('stdin') if val == '@-' else read_named(val[1:], ctx)
            if value is None:
                return None
        elif magic and val in ('true', 'false', 'null'):
            value = {'true': True, 'false': False, 'null': None}[val]
        elif magic and re.fullmatch(r'-?\d+', val):
            value = int(val)
        else:
            value = val
        cur = root
        for i, p in enumerate(parts):
            last = i == len(parts) - 1
            nxt = None if last else parts[i + 1]
            if isinstance(cur, list):
                if p != '':
                    return None
                if last:
                    cur.append(value)
                    break
                if not cur or not isinstance(cur[-1], dict) or nxt in cur[-1]:
                    cur.append({} if nxt != '' else [])
                cur = cur[-1]
                continue
            if not isinstance(cur, dict) or p == '':
                return None
            if last:
                cur[p] = value
            else:
                if p not in cur:
                    cur[p] = [] if nxt == '' else {}
                cur = cur[p]
    return root


def json_body(text):
    """A request body as JSON, or None when it is no JSON (or holds hidden text)."""
    if text is None or hidden(text):
        return None
    try:
        return json.loads(text)
    except ValueError:
        return None


def has_checks(value):
    return isinstance(value, list) and len(value) > 0


def empties(kind, body, eff):
    """Why a body sent to this kind of protection endpoint leaves no required check, or ''."""
    if kind == 'prot':   # PUT replaces the whole protection
        rsc = body.get('required_status_checks') if isinstance(body, dict) else None
        if not isinstance(rsc, dict) or not (has_checks(rsc.get('contexts')) or has_checks(rsc.get('checks'))):
            return 'a branch protection write with no required check (required_status_checks null, left out or ' \
                'with empty contexts/checks)'
    elif kind == 'rsc':
        given = [body[k] for k in ('contexts', 'checks') if k in body] if isinstance(body, dict) else [None]
        if given and not any(has_checks(v) for v in given):
            return 'a write to the required status checks that empties them'
    elif kind == 'ctx' and eff != 'POST':   # POST adds checks; PUT replaces the list
        lst = body.get('contexts') if isinstance(body, dict) else body
        if not has_checks(lst):
            return 'a write that replaces the list of required checks with an empty one'
    elif kind == 'ruleset' and isinstance(body, dict) and 'rules' in body:   # rules replace the ruleset's rules
        rules = body['rules'] if isinstance(body['rules'], list) else []
        if not any(isinstance(r, dict) and str(r.get('type', '')).lower() == 'required_status_checks'
                   and isinstance(r.get('parameters'), dict)
                   and has_checks(r['parameters'].get('required_status_checks')) for r in rules):
            return 'a ruleset write whose rules hold no required status check (rules [] or without a ' \
                'required_status_checks rule that names a check)'
    elif kind == 'ruleset' and not isinstance(body, dict):
        return 'a ruleset write whose body is no JSON object'
    return ''


def emptying_reason(kinds, bodies, eff, how):
    """A refusal when a write to protection endpoints (kinds) may leave no required check. bodies: the parsed
    bodies, None for one the guard cannot read."""
    if not kinds:
        return None
    if any(b is None for b in bodies) or not bodies:
        return reason(f'{how} a body the guard cannot read (no JSON, a pipe, a file that is not there yet, hidden '
                      'text) to branch protection or a ruleset: it may remove the required checks',
                      'Write the body as JSON to a file in a command before this one and name the file in full.')
    for kind in sorted(kinds):
        for b in bodies:
            why = empties(kind, b, eff)
            if why:
                return reason(f'{how} {why}: that takes `main` out from behind CI as surely as a DELETE',
                              'A write that keeps (or adds) the required checks passes.')
    return None


def gql_hidden(text):
    """Whether GraphQL text has hidden parts outside its "strings" (an id in quotes from a variable is fine; text
    the guard cannot read where the operation, a field or a value goes is not)."""
    i, n, quoted = 0, len(text), False
    while i < n:
        c = text[i]
        if quoted and c == '\\':
            i += 2
            continue
        if c == '"':
            quoted = not quoted
        elif not quoted and c in (HID, WILD):
            return True
        i += 1
    return False


def gql_code(text):
    """GraphQL text as the rules below read it: each "string" and \"\"\"block string\"\"\" stands as one word
    (`"_"`) and # comments are gone, so `name: "requiredStatusChecks: [ci]"` or a comment names no kept check. A
    JSON request body (--input) gives its query (its variables are read as variables). Before canon(), which drops
    all quotes."""
    try:
        j = json.loads(text) if text.lstrip()[:1] == '{' else None
    except ValueError:
        j = None
    if isinstance(j, dict) and isinstance(j.get('query'), str):
        text = j['query']
    out, i, n = [], 0, len(text)
    while i < n:
        c = text[i]
        if c == '#':
            end = text.find('\n', i)
            i = n if end < 0 else end
        elif text.startswith('"""', i):
            end = re.search(r'(?<!\\)"""', text[i + 3:])   # \""" is a quote inside a block string
            out.append(' "_" ')
            i = n if end is None else i + 3 + end.end()
        elif c == '"':
            i += 1
            while i < n and text[i] not in '"\n':
                i += 2 if text[i] == '\\' else 1
            out.append(' "_" ')
            i += 1
        else:
            out.append(c)
            i += 1
    return ''.join(out)


def gql_unset(v):
    """A GraphQL value that turns a required-check setting off: false, null, an empty list, or text the guard cannot
    read."""
    return v is None or v is False or v == [] or (isinstance(v, str) and (
        hidden(v) or re.fullmatch(r'\s*(?:false|null|\[[\s,]*\])?\s*', v, re.I) is not None))


def rules_keep_checks(rules):
    """A ruleset's rules (GraphQL input) hold a REQUIRED_STATUS_CHECKS rule that names at least one check."""
    return isinstance(rules, list) and any(
        isinstance(r, dict) and str(r.get('type', '')).upper() == 'REQUIRED_STATUS_CHECKS'
        and isinstance(r.get('parameters'), dict) and isinstance(r['parameters'].get('requiredStatusChecks'), dict)
        and has_checks(r['parameters']['requiredStatusChecks'].get('requiredStatusChecks')) for r in rules)


def gql_value_empties(v, ruleset, depth=0):
    """Why a GraphQL variable's value (the whole input, as gh builds it from input[..] fields or an --input file's
    "variables") leaves no required check, or ''."""
    if depth > 32:
        return 'nests deeper than the guard reads'
    if isinstance(v, list):
        return next((r for r in (gql_value_empties(x, ruleset, depth + 1) for x in v) if r), '')
    if not isinstance(v, dict):
        return ''
    for k, x in v.items():
        key = k.lower()
        if key == 'requiresstatuschecks' and not (x is True or (isinstance(x, str) and x.strip().lower() == 'true')):
            return f'sets {k} to {json.dumps(x) if not hidden(json.dumps(x)) else "a value the guard cannot read"}'
        if key in ('requiredstatuscheckcontexts', 'requiredstatuschecks') and not isinstance(x, dict) \
                and gql_unset(x):
            return f'sets {k} to nothing (an empty list, null, or a value the guard cannot read)'
        if key == 'rules' and ruleset and not rules_keep_checks(x):
            return 'sets the ruleset rules without a REQUIRED_STATUS_CHECKS rule that names a check'
        why = gql_value_empties(x, ruleset, depth + 1)
        if why:
            return why
    return ''


GQL_KEPT = re.compile(r'requiredStatusChecks["\']?\s*:\s*(?:\[[\s,]*[^\]\s,]|\$\w)', re.I)   # a list that names a check


def gql_empties(texts, variables=()):
    """Why a GraphQL updateRepositoryRuleset / updateBranchProtectionRule may leave no required check, or ''.
    texts: the query and bodies as written; variables: the variables objects gh sends (from -f/-F fields, None when
    the fields cannot be read, and from an --input file)."""
    joined = '\n'.join(t for t in texts if t)
    if not GQL_EMPTYING.search(joined):
        return ''
    if any(v is None for v in variables):
        return 'takes variables the guard cannot read'
    ruleset = GQL_UPDATE.search(joined) is not None
    known = {}
    for v in variables:
        if isinstance(v, dict):
            known.update(v)
    for v in variables:
        why = gql_value_empties(v, ruleset)
        if why:
            return why
    for m in GQL_EMPTY.finditer(joined):
        if m.group(2):
            return f'sets {m.group(1)} to {m.group(2)}'
        var = m.group(3)   # its value: -F var=.., or "var": .. in a variables object
        if var in known:
            if gql_unset(known[var]) or (m.group(1).lower() == 'rules' and ruleset
                                         and not rules_keep_checks(known[var])):
                return f'sets {m.group(1)} from ${var} to nothing (or to a value the guard cannot read)'
            continue
        vals = re.findall(r'(?:^|[\s"\'{,])%s["\']?\s*[:=]\s*(false|null|\[[\s,]*\]|[^\s,}]+)' % re.escape(var), joined,
                          re.I | re.M)
        vals += ['[]'] if re.search(r'(?:^|\s)%s\[\](?:\s|$)' % re.escape(var), joined, re.M) else []
        if not vals or any(re.fullmatch(r'false|null|\[[\s,]*\]', v, re.I) for v in vals):
            return f'sets {m.group(1)} from ${var} to nothing (or to a value the guard cannot find)'
    # a variable that holds the whole input (input: $input) must be there to read: gh sends null for one it lacks
    for var in dict.fromkeys(re.findall(r'(?<![\w$])\w+\s*:\s*\$(\w+)', joined)):
        if var not in known and not re.search(r'(?:^|[\s"\'{,])%s["\']?\s*[:=]' % re.escape(var), joined, re.M):
            return f'takes ${var}, whose value the guard cannot find'
    if ruleset and re.search(r'\brules(?:["\']?\s*:|\])', joined, re.I) and not GQL_KEPT.search(
            '\n'.join([joined] + [json.dumps(v, ensure_ascii=False) for v in variables])):
        return 'sets the ruleset rules without a required status check'
    return ''


# ---------- curl, wget, httpie

def net_reason(word, args, ctx):
    """curl, wget, httpie and the like next to a guarded path. Only a call that is plainly a read passes: the
    tool's known read-only flags and nothing the guard cannot read. (Each tool reads flags its own way, so there
    is one table per tool, and a tool without a table may carry no flags at all.)"""
    best = {k: max([far_hits(a, k) for a in args] or [0]) for k in GUARDED}
    if best['disable'] == 2:
        return reason('a call to the GitHub `.../actions/workflows/<id>/disable` endpoint')
    if not (best['disable'] or best['perm'] or best['protect']):
        return None
    paths = [a for a in args if any(far_hits(a, k) for k in GUARDED)]
    if plain_read(NET_TOOLS.get(word), args, paths):
        return None
    if best['perm'] or best['disable']:
        return reason('a request to GitHub `.../actions/permissions` (or to a path that hidden text can turn into '
                      'a guarded one) that is not plainly a read: it may turn Actions off or limit it',
                      'A read passes with the tool\'s own config off and read-only flags only: `curl -q -sS -L '
                      '-f -H .. -o ..` (-q first), `wget --no-config -q -O ..`.')
    if any('DELETE' in a.upper() or hidden(a) for a in args if a not in paths):
        return reason('a DELETE (or a request the guard cannot read) to branch protection or a ruleset: it removes '
                      'the rules that keep `main` behind CI and review')
    kinds = set().union(*[protect_kinds(a) for a in paths])
    if kinds:   # a write that leaves no required check
        why = emptying_reason(kinds, net_bodies(word, args, paths, ctx), '?', 'a request with')
        if why:
            return why
    if best['ruleset']:   # a body that switches the ruleset off, written out or in a file (-d @file)
        files = [m.group(1) for a in args for m in [re.search(r'@([^@=\s]+)$', a)] if m and a not in paths]
        texts = [read_named(f, ctx) for f in files]
        if any(t is None or RULESET_OFF.search(canon(t, cue=False)) for t in texts) \
                or any(RULESET_OFF.search(canon(a, cue=False)) for a in args):
            return reason('a request that switches a ruleset off (enforcement=disabled or evaluate), or one to a '
                          'ruleset with a '
                          'body file the guard cannot read')
    return None


NET_BODY = {'curl': {'-d', '--data', '--data-raw', '--data-binary', '--data-ascii', '--json', '-T',
                     '--upload-file'},
            'wget': {'--post-data', '--body-data', '--post-file', '--body-file'}}
NET_FILE = {'-T', '--upload-file', '--post-file', '--body-file'}   # these name a file; curl -d takes @file
NET_RAW = {'--data-raw', '--post-data', '--body-data'}   # these take the text as it is, @ and all


def net_bodies(word, args, paths, ctx):
    """The JSON body of a curl/wget request ([None] when the guard cannot read it: another tool, a form, more than
    one body, a body the command does not show)."""
    flags = NET_BODY.get(word)
    if flags is None or any(a.startswith(('-F', '--form')) for a in args if word == 'curl' and a not in paths):
        return [None]
    found = []
    for i, a in enumerate(args):
        name, eq, val = a.partition('=') if a.startswith('--') else (a[:2], '', a[2:]) if a[:2] in flags else (a, '', '')
        if name not in flags:
            continue
        if not eq and not val:
            val = args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else None
        found.append((name, val))
    if len(found) != 1 or found[0][1] is None:
        return [None]
    name, val = found[0]
    if name in NET_FILE or (name not in NET_RAW and val.startswith('@')):
        src = val[1:] if val.startswith('@') and name not in NET_FILE else val
        text = ctx.get('stdin') if src == '-' else read_named(src, ctx)
    else:
        text = val
    return [json_body(text)]


def plain_read(tool, args, paths):
    """Whether the arguments are a read for certain: the path, GET/HEAD, and flags from the tool's table."""
    switches, values, methods, short_switches, short_values, short_method, eq = tool or NO_FLAGS
    i, n = 0, len(args)

    def value(joined=None):   # a value that looks like a flag is refused: the tool may read it as one
        nonlocal i
        if joined is None:
            i += 1
            joined = args[i - 1] if i <= n else None
        return None if joined is None or (joined.startswith('-') and len(joined) > 1) else joined

    while i < n:
        a = args[i]
        i += 1
        if a in paths or (a.upper() in READS and not tool):   # httpie: `http GET url`
            continue
        if not a.startswith('-') or a == '-' or hidden(a) and WILD in a:
            return False
        if a.startswith('--'):
            name, has_eq, val = a.partition('=')
            if name in switches and not has_eq:
                continue
            if (name not in values and name not in methods) or (has_eq and not eq):
                return False
            val = value(val if has_eq else None)
            if val is None or hidden(val) and name in methods or (name in methods and val.upper() not in READS) \
                    or (name == '--header' and override_header(val)):
                return False
            continue
        s = a[1:]
        while s:
            c, s = s[0], s[1:]
            if c in short_switches:
                continue
            if c not in short_values and c != short_method:
                return False
            val, s = value(s or None), ''
            if val is None or (c == short_method and val.upper() not in READS) or (c == 'H' and override_header(val)):
                return False
    return True


# ---------- code and script files

def read_text(path):
    """A file's text; None for no file, a file over MAX_FILE, an error and a binary (NUL in the first 8 KB). Where a
    shell or interpreter runs the file, file_token() and unread() tell those apart."""
    if not path or not os.path.isfile(path):
        return None
    try:
        if os.path.getsize(path) > MAX_FILE:
            return None
        with open(path, 'rb') as f:
            data = f.read()
    except OSError:
        return None
    if b'\0' in data[:8192]:
        return None
    return data.decode('utf-8', 'replace')


def file_token(path):
    """The guarded token a file names, also in a file over MAX_FILE (read in 1 MB pieces that overlap by 4 KB). None
    when it names none, is not there or is a compiled binary (a NUL in the first 8 KB: today's pass); a file the
    guard cannot read at all (an error, over HARD_FILE) counts as naming one."""
    text = read_text(path)
    if text is not None:
        return named(text)
    try:
        if not path or not os.path.isfile(path):
            return None
        with open(path, 'rb') as f:
            if b'\0' in f.read(8192):
                return None
            if os.path.getsize(path) > HARD_FILE:
                return TOO_BIG
            f.seek(0)
            tail = ''
            while True:
                piece = f.read(1 << 20)
                if not piece:
                    return None
                s = tail + piece.decode('utf-8', 'replace')
                tok = named(s) or (LOOSE_RE.search(s) and 'disabling Actions')
                if tok:
                    return tok
                tail = s[-4096:]
    except OSError:
        return NO_OPEN


def unread(path, any_file=True):
    """Why a file that a shell or interpreter is about to run, and that read_text() could not give in full, is
    refused; None for no file, a compiled binary and a big file that names no token anywhere. any_file=False: only
    a file that looks like a script (its name, a `#!` line, the executable bit); data passes as before."""
    if not path or read_text(path) is not None or not os.path.isfile(path) or not (any_file or script_like(path)):
        return None
    tok, name, mb = file_token(path), redact(os.path.basename(path)), MAX_FILE // 1_000_000
    if tok == NO_OPEN:
        return reason(f'the file {name} is about to run, and the guard cannot open it to read it', 'Make it readable.')
    return tok and reason(f'the file {name} is about to run, and the guard reads files whole only up to {mb} MB: '
                          + (f'it is {tok}' if tok == TOO_BIG else f'read in pieces, it names {tok}'),
                          f'Split the file, or run the part you need from a file under {mb} MB.')


def script_like(path, unopened=True):
    """A file that looks like a script (its name, the executable bit, a `#!` line) and is no compiled binary.
    unopened: the answer for a file the guard cannot open (True where a shell or interpreter runs it)."""
    try:
        with open(path, 'rb') as f:
            head = f.read(8192)
    except OSError:
        return unopened
    return b'\0' not in head and (head[:2] == b'#!' or path.endswith(SHELL_EXT + CODE_EXT) or os.access(path, os.X_OK))


def resolve_path(tok, cwd):
    tok = os.path.expanduser(tok)
    return os.path.normpath(tok if os.path.isabs(tok) else os.path.join(cwd, tok))


def folders(ctx):
    """The folders a command of the text may run in: the hook's, and every one the text moves to with `cd`."""
    return ctx.get('cwds') or [ctx['cwd']]


def read_named(tok, ctx):
    """The text of a file the command names, looked for in every folder the command may run in; None when it is
    hidden, not there or no text."""
    if hidden(tok):
        return None
    for d in folders(ctx):
        text = read_text(resolve_path(tok, d))
        if text is not None:
            return text
    return None


def named_file(tok, ctx):
    """file_token() of a file the command names, in every folder the command may run in."""
    return None if hidden(tok) else next((t for d in folders(ctx) for t in [file_token(resolve_path(tok, d))] if t),
                                         None)


def resolve_cmd(tok, ctx, via_shell):
    """The script file a command word runs: through a shell/source any file, else only an executable one."""
    if '/' in tok:
        p = resolve_path(tok, ctx['cwd'])
        return p if via_shell or (os.path.isfile(p) and os.access(p, os.X_OK)) else None
    if via_shell:
        p = resolve_path(tok, ctx['cwd'])
        if os.path.isfile(p):
            return p
    for d in home_path_dirs(ctx['env'].get('PATH') or '', ctx['env'].get('HOME') or os.path.expanduser('~')):
        p = os.path.join(d, tok)
        if os.path.isfile(p) and os.access(p, os.X_OK):
            return p
    return None


_HOME_DIRS = {}


def home_path_dirs(path, home):
    """PATH entries under $HOME (computed once: realpath on every entry for every segment was slow)."""
    key = (path, home)
    if key not in _HOME_DIRS:
        h = os.path.realpath(home) + os.sep
        _HOME_DIRS[key] = [d for d in path.split(':') if d and os.path.realpath(d).startswith(h)]
    return _HOME_DIRS[key]


def file_reason(path, ctx, force_shell=False):
    if not path or os.path.realpath(path) == SELF:
        return None   # the guard's own text is full of what it refuses
    # One answer per file and setting: a script that calls its helpers fifty times is read once, not fifty times.
    memo = ctx.setdefault('memo', {})
    key = (path, force_shell, ctx['depth'], tuple(folders(ctx)), tuple(ctx.get('cfg', ())),
           bool(ctx.get('cfg_hidden')), repr(ctx.get('gh_set', ())))
    if key not in memo:
        memo[key] = None   # a script that runs itself: the run that is being read already answers for it
        memo[key] = deep_reason(path, ctx) if ctx['depth'] >= MAX_DEPTH else file_reason_now(path, ctx, force_shell)
    return memo[key]


def deep_reason(path, ctx):
    """A script too deep to read as shell: it and the scripts it names are read for tokens only; anything further
    counts as a program the guard does not know. A file it names that cannot be opened is no script here."""
    text = read_text(path)
    near = {resolve_path(w, d) for w in re.findall(r'[\w.~/+-]+', text or '') for d in folders(ctx)} - {path}
    hit = next((q for q in sorted(near) if os.path.isfile(q) and os.path.realpath(q) != SELF and
                script_like(q, unopened=False) and file_token(q)), None)
    what = 'a script that scripts run, too deep for the guard to read'
    return unread(path) or (hit and reason(f'{what} ({redact(os.path.basename(path))}) names the script '
                                           f'{redact(os.path.basename(hit))}, which names a guarded token',
                                           'Run the inner script as a command of its own.')) or \
        stopped(text or '', what, ctx['flow'], 'Run the inner script as a command of its own.')


def file_reason_now(path, ctx, force_shell):
    f = facts(path, ctx)
    if not f['text']:   # an interpreter's file counts only when it looks like a script (lockout care for data)
        return unread(path, any_file=force_shell)
    if f['own']:
        return None
    shellish = force_shell or path.endswith(('.sh', '.bash', '.zsh')) or f['bang'] in SHELLS
    if not shellish:
        f = facts(path, ctx, ('named',))
        if not f['text']:   # changed under the guard since it was cached
            return unread(path, any_file=force_shell)
        tok = f['named']
        return tok and reason(f'{redact(os.path.basename(path))}: a script that names {tok}. The guard reads shell '
                              'scripts only, not code in other languages, so it cannot prove this one is harmless',
                              CODE_HINT + ' Run tests of this guard through a test runner that is handed their folder.')
    text = body(path, ctx)
    if text is None:
        return unread(path, any_file=force_shell)
    sub_ctx = dict(ctx, depth=ctx['depth'] + 1, raw=text, nest=0)
    inner = decide_text(text, sub_ctx, scan_raw=False)
    if inner:   # say what in the script is refused, not only that something is
        what = inner[len(PREFIX):] if inner.startswith(PREFIX) else inner
        return PREFIX + f'the script {os.path.basename(path)} holds: {what}'
    return None


_OWN = []


def own_text():
    """The guard's own text: a copy that is the same byte for byte (the repo copy next to the installed one) is the
    guard, not a script that disables Actions."""
    if not _OWN:
        _OWN.append(read_text(SELF) if SELF else None)
    return _OWN[0]


def shebang(text):
    """The program a `#!` first line names (`#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -I` is python3), '' without one."""
    first = text.split('\n', 1)[0]
    if not first.startswith('#!'):
        return ''
    parts = first[2:].split()
    if parts and os.path.basename(parts[0]) == 'env':
        parts = [x for x in parts[1:] if not x.startswith('-') and '=' not in x]
    return os.path.basename(parts[0]) if parts else ''


# ---------- the cache: what files say, kept 60 s between hook runs

# One file per user: {"v": CACHE_VERSION, "guard": the stat of this guard file, "files": {path: entry}, "gh": {dirs:
# entry}}. A file entry: {"sig": [dev, inode, size, mtime_ns, ctime_ns], "t": unix time of the reading, "text": true,
# "own": byte-equal to this guard, "bang": the `#!` program or null without a `#!` line, "named": the guarded token
# it names or null (only once it was looked for)}. A gh entry: {"sig": [the sig or null (no file) per config.yml],
# "t", "aliases", "risky", "odd"}: gh_config_now() of those files. Every entry is checked on use; a cache that is
# not this user's own plain file, is too big, broken, from another guard file or version, counts as no cache.
# Same-user code can forge it, as it can edit this guard: best effort against mistakes, not a wall (see the top).
CACHE_FILE = 'cache-v1.json'


def state_dir(env):
    """The guard's state folder (stateDir; "~/" is this HOME); None for "~/.." without an absolute HOME (no cache)."""
    d = SETTINGS['stateDir']
    if not d.startswith('~/'):
        return d
    home = env.get('HOME') or ''
    return os.path.join(home, d[2:]) if os.path.isabs(home) else None


def file_sig(path):
    """[dev, inode, size, mtime_ns, ctime_ns] of a plain file (links followed); None for anything else. The change
    time cannot be set back by a program (utime sets only mtime), so a file written again shows a new sig."""
    try:
        st = os.stat(path)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    return [st.st_dev, st.st_ino, st.st_size, st.st_mtime_ns, st.st_ctime_ns] if S_ISREG(st.st_mode) else None


def racy(sig, now):
    """A file changed within CACHE_RACY seconds (or in the future): a change in the same clock tick as the reading
    would not show in its times, so it is not cached."""
    return sig is None or max(sig[3], sig[4]) / 1e9 > now - CACHE_RACY


def guard_sig():
    return file_sig(SELF) if SELF else None


def path_links(path, limit=40):
    """[dev, inode, mtime_ns, ctime_ns] of every symbolic link met on the way from path to the file it names (the
    kit's bin link, its `current` link, a link in a folder above them), or None when the path cannot be followed."""
    out, parts, at = [], [p for p in path.split('/') if p], '/'
    while parts:
        part = parts.pop(0)
        if part in ('', '.'):
            continue
        if part == '..':
            at = os.path.dirname(at)
            continue
        p = os.path.join(at, part)
        try:
            st = os.lstat(p)
            target = os.readlink(p) if S_ISLNK(st.st_mode) else None
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            return None
        if target is None:
            at = p
            continue
        out.append([st.st_dev, st.st_ino, st.st_mtime_ns, st.st_ctime_ns])
        if len(out) > limit:
            return None
        parts[:0] = [x for x in target.split('/') if x]
        at = '/' if target.startswith('/') else at
    return out


def start_sig():
    """The guard file's stat, taken when this module has run its top part: only when the file, and every link on
    the way to it from the path Python ran (the bin link, the kit's `current` link), were last changed before this
    process started (from /proc). An update writes the new release first and then switches `current`: the new file
    is older than a hook that opened the old one just before the switch, but the switched link is not. Python
    compiled and ran ~2000 lines before this line, and a switch in that window would give the old code in memory
    the new file's stat: then None, and this run uses no cache."""
    sig = guard_sig()
    links = path_links(os.path.abspath(__file__)) if '__file__' in globals() else []
    started = time.time() - since_start()
    times = [max(sig[3], sig[4])] + [max(x[2], x[3]) for x in links] if sig and links is not None else []
    return sig if times and max(times) / 1e9 < started - START_SLACK else None


START_SLACK = 0.05   # /proc gives the start in clock ticks (10 ms): a file changed that close to it is not trusted
# The guard file as this process found it when it started. If the file is replaced while the hook runs (an update),
# the code in memory is the old guard: it must not keep facts under the new file's stat, nor use the new one's cache.
GUARD_AT_START = start_sig()


def guard_now():
    """The guard file's stat, or None when it is not the file this process started from."""
    me = guard_sig()
    return me if me and me == GUARD_AT_START else None


def is_sig(v):
    return isinstance(v, list) and len(v) == 5 and all(type(x) is int for x in v)


def fresh(t, now):
    return type(t) in (int, float) and 0 <= now - t <= CACHE_LIFE


def file_entry_ok(e, now):
    labels = {label for label, _ in TOKENS}
    return isinstance(e, dict) and is_sig(e.get('sig')) and fresh(e.get('t'), now) and e.get('text') is True \
        and type(e.get('own')) is bool and (e.get('bang') is None or type(e.get('bang')) is str) \
        and ('named' not in e or e['named'] is None or (type(e['named']) is str and e['named'] in labels))


def gh_entry_ok(e, now):
    return isinstance(e, dict) and isinstance(e.get('sig'), list) and all(s is None or is_sig(s) for s in e['sig']) \
        and fresh(e.get('t'), now) and type(e.get('risky')) is bool and type(e.get('odd')) is bool \
        and isinstance(e.get('aliases'), list) and all(
            isinstance(a, list) and len(a) == 2 and isinstance(a[0], list) and a[0]
            and all(type(w) is str for w in a[0]) and type(a[1]) is str for a in e['aliases'])


def cache_load(env):
    """The cache file as a dict, or None: no file, not a plain file of this user that only this user can write, over
    CACHE_BYTES, not JSON, from another guard file or cache version. Never waits (O_NONBLOCK: a FIFO)."""
    d, me = state_dir(env), guard_now()
    if not d or not me:
        return None
    try:
        fd = os.open(os.path.join(d, CACHE_FILE), os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_NONBLOCK
                     | getattr(os, 'O_CLOEXEC', 0))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    try:
        st = os.fstat(fd)
        if not S_ISREG(st.st_mode) or st.st_uid != os.geteuid() or st.st_mode & 0o022 or st.st_size > CACHE_BYTES:
            return None
        data = b''
        while len(data) <= CACHE_BYTES:
            piece = os.read(fd, 1 << 16)
            if not piece:
                break
            data += piece
        if len(data) > CACHE_BYTES:
            return None
        c = json.loads(data.decode('utf-8'))
    except Exception:   # also RecursionError (deep nesting) and a half-written file
        return None
    finally:
        os.close(fd)
    if not isinstance(c, dict) or type(c.get('v')) is not int or c['v'] != CACHE_VERSION or c.get('guard') != me \
            or not isinstance(c.get('files'), dict) or not isinstance(c.get('gh'), dict):
        return None
    return c


def cache_disk(ctx):
    """The cache of this run, read once and only when a file is first looked at; None without one."""
    h = ctx.get('cache')
    if h is None:
        return None
    if 'disk' not in h:
        h['disk'] = cache_load(ctx['env'])
    return h['disk']


def facts(path, ctx, need=()):
    """What the guard knows of a file: {'text': whether read_text() gives it, and then 'own' (byte-equal to this
    guard), 'bang' (the `#!` program, None without a `#!` line), 'named' (the guarded token it names, with 'named' in
    need), '_body' (the text, with 'body' in need)}. From this run's memo, else the cache, else the file."""
    memo = ctx.setdefault('memo', {})
    key = ('facts', path)
    f = memo.get(key)
    if f is None:
        f = cached_facts(path, ctx) or fresh_facts(path, ctx)
        memo[key] = f
    if f['text'] and '_body' not in f and ('body' in need or ('named' in need and 'named' not in f)):
        f = memo[key] = fresh_facts(path, ctx)
    if f['text'] and 'named' in need and 'named' not in f:
        f['named'] = named(f['_body'])
    return f


def body(path, ctx):
    """The text of a file (read_text()), through facts()."""
    f = facts(path, ctx, ('body',))
    return f['_body'] if f['text'] else None


def cached_facts(path, ctx):
    disk = cache_disk(ctx)
    e = disk['files'].get(path) if disk else None
    if not file_entry_ok(e, time.time()) or e['sig'] != file_sig(path):
        return None
    return {k: e[k] for k in ('text', 'own', 'bang', 'named') if k in e}


_OWN_SIZE = []


def own_size():
    if not _OWN_SIZE:
        sig = guard_sig()
        _OWN_SIZE.append(sig[2] if sig else None)
    return _OWN_SIZE[0]


def fresh_facts(path, ctx):
    before = file_sig(path)
    text = read_text(path)
    if text is None:
        return {'text': False}
    after, size = file_sig(path), own_size()
    own = (after is None or size is None or after[2] == size) and text == own_text()
    f = {'text': True, 'own': own, 'bang': shebang(text) if text.startswith('#!') else None, '_body': text}
    now = time.time()
    if ctx.get('cache') is not None and before is not None and before == after and not racy(after, now):
        f.update(sig=after, t=now)
        ctx['cache'].setdefault('files', {})[path] = f
    return f


def gh_config_cached(dirs, ctx):
    """gh_config_now(dirs), from the cache while every config.yml is the same file as when it was read."""
    paths = [os.path.abspath(os.path.join(cfg, 'config.yml')) for cfg in dict.fromkeys(dirs)]
    def sigs():
        out = []
        for p in paths:
            try:
                st = os.stat(p)
            except FileNotFoundError:
                out.append(None)
                continue
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                return False   # unreadable folder, a loop: not cached
            if not S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
                return False
            out.append([st.st_dev, st.st_ino, st.st_size, st.st_mtime_ns, st.st_ctime_ns])
        return out
    key, now = repr(paths), time.time()
    before = sigs()
    disk = cache_disk(ctx) if before is not False else None
    e = disk['gh'].get(key) if disk else None
    if gh_entry_ok(e, now) and e['sig'] == before:
        return [(list(w), x) for w, x in e['aliases']], e['risky'], e['odd']
    got = gh_config_now(dirs)
    if before is not False and before == sigs() and not any(racy(s, now) for s in before if s is not None) \
            and ctx.get('cache') is not None:
        ctx['cache'].setdefault('gh', {})[key] = {'sig': before, 't': now, 'aliases': [[list(w), x] for w, x in got[0]],
                                                  'risky': bool(got[1]), 'odd': bool(got[2])}
    return got


def cache_save(ctx):
    """Add what this run learned to the cache file: under a lock, merged with the file as it is now, written to a
    temp file and renamed over it (a reader sees the old or the new file, never half of one). decide() calls it last,
    when the answer is known: any failure (a full disk, a folder that is no folder, out of time) keeps the old file
    or none, and leaves that answer as it is."""
    h = ctx.get('cache') or {}
    new_files = {p: {k: v for k, v in f.items() if not k.startswith('_')} for p, f in h.get('files', {}).items()}
    new_gh = h.get('gh', {})
    # The hook's stop_at is 1 s before the budget ends (answer()); the write may use the first half of that second.
    # A slow first run is the one whose reads most need keeping. The hook's alarm still cuts a write that runs over
    # (Slow, caught below), so the answer is never late for it.
    stop_at = ctx.get('stop_at')
    save_by = None if stop_at is None else stop_at + 0.5
    if not (new_files or new_gh) or (save_by is not None and time.monotonic() > save_by):
        return
    lock = tmp = None
    try:
        d, me = state_dir(ctx['env']), guard_now()
        if not d or not me:
            return
        os.makedirs(d, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
        st = os.lstat(d)
        if not S_ISDIR(st.st_mode) or st.st_uid != os.geteuid():
            return
        if st.st_mode & 0o077:
            os.chmod(d, 0o700)
        import fcntl
        lock = os.open(os.path.join(d, 'cache.lock'), os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT | os.O_NOFOLLOW
                       | getattr(os, 'O_CLOEXEC', 0), 0o600)
        give_up = time.monotonic() + 0.5 if save_by is None else min(time.monotonic() + 0.5, save_by)
        while True:
            try:
                fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                break
            except BlockingIOError:
                if time.monotonic() > give_up:
                    return
                time.sleep(0.01)
        now = time.time()
        cur = cache_load(ctx['env']) or {}
        files = {p: e for p, e in (cur.get('files') or {}).items() if file_entry_ok(e, now)}
        gh = {k: e for k, e in (cur.get('gh') or {}).items() if gh_entry_ok(e, now)}
        files.update((p, e) for p, e in new_files.items() if file_entry_ok(e, now))
        gh.update((k, e) for k, e in new_gh.items() if gh_entry_ok(e, now))
        files = dict(sorted(files.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1]['t'])[-CACHE_MAX:])
        gh = dict(sorted(gh.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1]['t'])[-16:])
        data = json.dumps({'v': CACHE_VERSION, 'guard': me, 'files': files, 'gh': gh},
                          separators=(',', ':')).encode('utf-8')
        if len(data) > CACHE_BYTES:
            return
        tmp = os.path.join(d, f'.cache-{os.getpid()}-{os.urandom(4).hex()}.tmp')
        fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_NOFOLLOW | getattr(os, 'O_CLOEXEC', 0), 0o600)
        try:
            view = memoryview(data)
            while view:
                view = view[os.write(fd, view):]
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
        os.replace(tmp, os.path.join(d, CACHE_FILE))
        tmp = None
        for n in os.listdir(d):   # temp files of hooks that were killed half-way
            p = os.path.join(d, n)
            if n.startswith('.cache-') and n.endswith('.tmp') and os.lstat(p).st_mtime < now - CACHE_LIFE:
                os.unlink(p)
    except BaseException:   # also Slow: the answer is already known, the cache is only a help
        pass
    finally:
        for f in ([lambda: os.unlink(tmp)] if tmp else []) + ([lambda: os.close(lock)] if lock is not None else []):
            try:
                f()
            except OSError:
                pass


# ---------- guarded tokens and the gate that fails closed

# \uXXXX is four digits and no more (JSON, JS, Python, Go): in \u0070ermissions the `e` is the next letter
ESCAPES = re.compile(r'\\+(?:u([0-9a-fA-F]{4})|u\{([0-9a-fA-F]{1,6})\}|U([0-9a-fA-F]{8})|x([0-9a-fA-F]{2}))'
                     r'|\\+([0-3][0-7]{2})|%([0-9a-fA-F]{2})')
AGENT_LABEL = 'an agent session without this machine\'s hooks (`claude --bare`, CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE, `codex exec`)'
CUES = ('disable', 'permissions', 'protection', 'ruleset', 'bare', 'safe-mode', 'claude_code_simple', 'codex')
# What may not be changed, as it looks after canon(): wherever one of these shows up, the command passes only when
# the guard can prove it is harmless (a plain read, a known allowed form, or text that goes nowhere unread).
TOKENS = [
    ('the `.../actions/workflows/<id>/disable` endpoint', re.compile(r'actions/workflows/[^/\s]+/disable(?![\w-])')),
    ('`workflow disable`', re.compile(r'workflow\b[\s\S]{0,60}?(?<![\w-])disable(?![\w-])')),
    ('`.../actions/permissions`', re.compile(r'actions/permissions(?![\w-])')),
    ('branch protection', re.compile(r'branches/[^\s?#]{1,200}?/protection(?![\w-])')),
    ('a ruleset', re.compile(r'(?<![\w-])rulesets/[^/\s?#]')),
    ('a GraphQL mutation on branch protection or a ruleset',
     re.compile(r'deletebranchprotectionrule|deleterepositoryruleset|updaterepositoryruleset')),
    # an agent session this machine's hooks do not reach (lens B r2 major 1): the same text inside python -c,
    # node -e, at, a script .. is refused as for gh. The program path counts too (.../claude/versions/2.1.3 --bare,
    # @anthropic-ai/claude-code, @openai/codex); `codex app-server` and `codex mcp ...` are no session of their own.
    (AGENT_LABEL,
     re.compile(r'(?<![\w-])claude[^\s;|&]*(?:\s+[^\s;|&]+){0,8}?\s+--(?:bare|safe-mode)(?![\w-])'
                r'|claude_code_simple'
                r'|(?<![\w-])codex[^\s;|&,]*(?:\s+-[^\s;|&]*(?:\s+[^\s;|&-][^\s;|&]*)?){0,6}?\s+'
                r'(?:exec|e|review|resume|fork|mcp-server)'
                r'(?![\w-])')),
]
# Command words that never run their arguments or their input as a command: shell keywords and builtins, and tools
# that only read, print, compare or move text and files. Text that names a guarded token may go to these. (Not
# here on purpose: awk, tar, less, vim, man, yq, patch, mapfile, xargs: each can run a command.)
DATA = frozenset('''echo printf cat head tail wc ls stat file du df basename dirname realpath readlink mkdir rmdir rm cp
    mv ln touch chmod chown tee sort uniq cut tr paste column fold fmt nl tac rev comm join diff cmp grep egrep fgrep rg
    jq date printenv id whoami hostname uname uptime nproc seq mktemp sha256sum sha1sum md5sum base64 xxd od tree ps
    pgrep kill sleep true false : test [ [[ ]] cd pushd popd pwd set shopt unset read alias unalias type which hash
    umask ulimit let shift wait exit return break continue for in case esac done fi select function getopts sed find
    typeset readonly local declare export'''.split())
STD_BINS = ('/bin', '/usr/bin', '/usr/local/bin', '/sbin', '/usr/sbin', '/opt/homebrew/bin')
WRITERS = {'tee', 'cp', 'mv', 'ln', 'sed'}                         # data words that write files themselves
READERS = {'cat', 'head', 'tail', 'tac', 'sed', 'tee'}              # data words that pass a file's text on
# data words that pass the text of the files they read on, changed or not (sort, grep, nl, cut ..)
TEXT_FILTERS = READERS | {'sort', 'uniq', 'cut', 'paste', 'column', 'fold', 'fmt', 'nl', 'rev', 'comm', 'join', 'diff',
                          'grep', 'egrep', 'fgrep', 'rg', 'jq', 'base64', 'xxd', 'od'}
COPIERS = {'cp', 'mv', 'ln'}                                        # data words that give a file another name
NO_SINK = {'tee', 'read'}   # data words that hand piped text on (a file, a variable) instead of ending it
AWKS = {'awk', 'gawk', 'mawk', 'nawk'}
# Words behind an unknown command word that the guard reads as the start of a command of their own
RUNNERS = {'sudo', 'doas', 'env', 'command', 'exec', 'nohup', 'nice', 'ionice', 'timeout', 'stdbuf', 'xargs',
           'setsid', 'unbuffer', 'chronic', 'flock', 'runuser', 'systemd-run', 'watch', 'script', 'git'}
# git subcommands that take text (a message, a pattern, a path) and run none of it
GIT_SAFE = frozenset('''commit log grep diff show add status tag blame rm mv checkout switch branch restore ls-files
    rev-parse merge notes reflog shortlog describe cat-file ls-tree rev-list stash cherry-pick reset revert
    format-patch worktree'''.split())
GIT_COMMANDS = GIT_SAFE | frozenset('''fetch push pull clone remote config init rebase bisect submodule gc clean am
    apply archive bundle fsck prune repack sparse-checkout range-diff verify-commit whatchanged show-ref symbolic-ref
    update-ref update-index for-each-ref merge-base name-rev diff-tree diff-index diff-files ls-remote count-objects
    check-ignore check-attr var version help maintenance mergetool difftool show-branch merge-tree write-tree
    commit-tree hash-object read-tree checkout-index pack-refs verify-tag stripspace interpret-trailers lfs'''.split())
GIT_RUNS = re.compile(r'--upload-pack|--receive-pack|--exec|--open-files-in-pager|-O')   # flags that run a command
GIT_RUNS_LONG = ('--upload-pack', '--receive-pack', '--exec', '--open-files-in-pager')


def git_runs(a):
    """A git argument that can run a command: GIT_RUNS, -O in a cluster of short flags (-nO), or a long flag cut
    short the way git accepts (--open=less, --upl=x)."""
    if GIT_RUNS.match(a) or re.match(r'-[A-Za-z0-9]*O', a):
        return True
    name = a.split('=', 1)[0]
    return len(name) > 2 and name.startswith('--') and any(f.startswith(name) for f in GIT_RUNS_LONG)

SED_RUNS = re.compile(r'(?:^|[;{}!,$/\d\s])e(?:\s|$)|[^\w\s][a-zA-Z0-9]*e[a-zA-Z0-9]*\s*(?:[;}\n]|$)')   # e, s///e
HOOK_PATH = re.compile(r'(^|/)(\.git|\.husky|\.githooks|hooks)/')
NO_FILE = re.compile(r'/dev/(null|stdout|stderr|tty|fd/\d+)|&?\d+|-')
SHELL_TEXT = re.compile(r'[\s;|&]')
SEPARATE = 'Run it as separate commands, or write it so the guard can read it (gh or curl as the command word).'
CODE_HINT = ('Make the call with `gh api` as a command of its own. To only change or search text that names it, use '
             'an edit or search tool instead of code.')


# ---------- the fast exit

# Words the fast exit lets through: DATA words that run no command, set no variable and read no shell text (not
# printf/read/export/alias/[ ..: they set names or take text that matters later), and git with a subcommand that
# runs none of its text. Each one is checked below for the forms of it that do run something.
QUICK_WORDS = frozenset('''echo cat head tail wc ls stat file du df basename dirname realpath readlink mkdir rmdir rm cp
    mv ln touch chmod tee sort uniq cut tr paste column fold fmt nl tac rev comm join diff cmp grep egrep fgrep rg jq
    date printenv id whoami hostname uname uptime nproc seq mktemp sha256sum sha1sum md5sum base64 xxd od tree ps
    pgrep sleep true false test cd pushd popd pwd sed find'''.split())
QUICK_GIT = GIT_SAFE
QUICK_FLOW = frozenset('if then else elif fi do done while until'.split())   # keywords in front of a command word
# Text that sends the command to the full reading wherever it stands, letters only (dis-able, Work_Flow ..):
QUICK_KEYS = ('disable', 'permission', 'protection', 'ruleset', 'workflow', 'graphql', 'mutation', 'github', 'curl',
              'wget', 'http', 'aria2') \
    + tuple(re.sub(r'[^a-z0-9]', '', os.path.splitext(r['name'])[0].lower()) for r in REFUSE_SCRIPTS)   # garden.py: garden
QUICK_NAMES = frozenset({'gh', 'xh', 'xhs', 'curlie', 'httpie'})   # as a word anywhere (/usr/bin/gh, ln -s .. gh)
QUICK_SED = frozenset({'-n', '-E', '-r', '-e', '-i', '-s', '-u', '-z', '--quiet', '--silent', '--regexp-extended',
                       '--null-data', '--expression'})
QUICK_CHARS = re.compile(r'[^\x20-\x7e\t\n]|[$`\\%!]|<<')
QUICK_MAX, QUICK_CDS = 4000, 8
# sed scripts the fast exit takes: lines printed, deleted or counted, s/a/b/ with plain flags (no e, no w file)
_SED_ADDR = r'(?:\d+|\$|/[^/\n]*/)'
_SED_ONE = (rf'\s*(?:{_SED_ADDR}(?:\s*,\s*(?:{_SED_ADDR}|[+~]\d+))?\s*)?'
            r'(?:[pdnNgGhHxlz=]|[qQ]\d*|s/[^/\n]*/[^/\n]*/[gpiI\d]*)\s*')
QUICK_SED_SCRIPT = re.compile(rf'{_SED_ONE}(?:;{_SED_ONE})*(?:;\s*)?')


def quick_words(cmd, globs=None):
    """[[(word without its quotes, whether it had quotes)] per command] of a command in the small shell subset the
    fast exit reads (plain words, '..' and ".." with no $ ` \\ inside, ; | & && || and line breaks, redirections);
    None for anything else: an unclosed quote, # ( ) { } ! outside quotes. globs: each word with * ? [ outside
    quotes (the shell may turn it into other names) is added to it, when given."""
    out, cmd_, word, quoted, started, glob, i = [], [], '', False, False, False, 0
    def end_word():
        nonlocal word, quoted, started, glob
        if started:
            cmd_.append((word, quoted))
            if glob and globs is not None:
                globs.append(word)
        word, quoted, started, glob = '', False, False, False
    while i < len(cmd):
        c = cmd[i]
        if c in '\'"':
            j = cmd.find(c, i + 1)
            if j < 0:
                return None
            word, quoted, started, i = word + cmd[i + 1:j], True, True, j + 1
            continue
        if c in '#(){}!' or (c == '|' and cmd[i - 1:i] in ('>', '&') and cmd[max(i - 2, 0):i] != '|&' and
                                 (cmd[i - 1:i] == '>' or cmd[i - 2:i - 1] in ('>', '<'))):
            return None   # >| (also zsh >>| >&|) writes a file, it is no pipe
        if c in ' \t':
            end_word()
        elif c in ';|\n' or (c == '&' and not (cmd[i - 1:i] in ('<', '>') or cmd[i + 1:i + 2] == '>')):
            end_word()
            out.append(cmd_)
            cmd_ = []
        else:
            word, started, glob = word + c, True, glob or c in '*?['
        i += 1
    end_word()
    out.append(cmd_)
    return out


def quick_sed_scripts(args):
    """The scripts of a sed command the fast exit reads (-e X, --expression X, else the first word that is no
    option); None for a form it does not read."""
    scripts, i, rest = [], 0, []
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        if a in ('-e', '--expression'):
            if i + 1 >= len(args):
                return None
            scripts.append(args[i + 1])
            i += 2
            continue
        if a.startswith('-') and a != '-':
            if a not in QUICK_SED:
                return None
        else:
            rest.append(a)
        i += 1
    if not scripts:
        scripts = rest[:1]
    return scripts or None


def quick_pass(cmd):
    """True for a command that cannot name or run anything this guard refuses, decided from its text alone, before
    any file is read: every command in it is a QUICK_WORDS word (or safe git) in a form that runs nothing, and no
    word of it can name a guarded token, gh, an HTTP client or a git hook, or one of its own command words (a file
    the text writes and then runs). Anything else, however harmless, goes to the full reading. The tests compare
    the two over every command they know and over random ones (what is fast passes there too)."""
    if not isinstance(cmd, str) or len(cmd) > QUICK_MAX or QUICK_CHARS.search(cmd):
        return False
    low = cmd.lower()
    letters = re.sub(r'[^a-z0-9]', '', low)
    plain = re.sub(r'[\'"]', '', low)
    if any(k in letters for k in QUICK_KEYS) or any(k in plain for k in ('hooks', '.git/', '.husky')) \
            or QUICK_NAMES & set(re.split(r'[^a-z0-9_.-]+', plain)):
        return False
    globs = []
    cmds = quick_words(cmd, globs)
    if cmds is None:
        return False
    cds, heads, rest = 0, set(), set()
    for words in cmds:
        while words and not words[0][1] and words[0][0] in QUICK_FLOW:
            words = words[1:]
        if not words:
            continue
        (head, quoted), args = words[0], [w for w, _ in words[1:]]
        if quoted or (head not in QUICK_WORDS and head != 'git'):
            return False
        heads.add(head)
        rest.update(p for a in args for p in re.split(r'[^a-z0-9_.+-]+', a.lower()))
        cds += head in ('cd', 'pushd', 'popd')
        if cds > QUICK_CDS:
            return False
        if head == 'find' and any(a in ('-exec', '-execdir', '-ok', '-okdir') for a in args):
            return False
        if head in COPIERS and re.search(r'[*?\[]', cmd):
            return False
        if head == 'sed':
            scripts = quick_sed_scripts(args)
            if scripts is None or not all(QUICK_SED_SCRIPT.fullmatch(t) for t in scripts) \
                    or any(SED_RUNS.search(a) for a in args):
                return False
        if head == 'rg' and any(a.startswith(('--pre', '--hostname-bin')) for a in args):
            return False
        if head == 'sort' and any(a.startswith('--co') for a in args):
            return False
        if head == 'git':
            i = 0
            while i < len(args) and args[i] in ('-C', '--no-pager', '-P'):
                i += 2 if args[i] == '-C' else 1
            if i >= len(args) or args[i] not in QUICK_GIT or any(git_runs(a) for a in args[i + 1:]):
                return False
    # echo .. > ~/.local/bin/ls; ls: a file the text may write under a name it runs, also as a glob (l?, [l]s)
    return not heads & rest and not any(quick_glob_names(g, heads) for g in globs)


def quick_shadowed(cmd, cwd, env):
    """For a command quick_pass() took: whether a command word of it has a file of that name in a PATH folder under
    $HOME (a script an earlier call left, ~/.local/bin/ls), or a cp/mv/ln of it puts files into such a folder. Then
    the full reading decides. A few stat calls, no file is read."""
    dirs = home_path_dirs(env.get('PATH') or '', env.get('HOME') or os.path.expanduser('~'))
    if not dirs:
        return False
    real = None
    for words in quick_words(cmd) or ():
        words = [w for w, _ in words]
        while words and words[0] in QUICK_FLOW:
            words = words[1:]
        if not words:
            continue
        if any(os.path.exists(os.path.join(d, words[0])) for d in dirs):
            return True
        if words[0] in COPIERS:
            real = real or {os.path.realpath(d) for d in dirs}
            home = env.get('HOME') or os.path.expanduser('~')
            if any(os.path.realpath(os.path.join(cwd or os.getcwd(), re.sub(r'^~(?=/|$)', home, a))) in real
                   for a in words[1:] if not a.startswith('-')):
                return True
    return False


def quick_glob_names(word, heads):
    """Whether a word with * ? [ outside quotes may name one of heads (each part between / = < > & : , is a glob).
    A part with [ may name anything ([[:lower:]], [^x] ..: the guard does not read classes); * and ? are read the
    way the shell does."""
    for part in re.split(r'[/=<>&:,]+', word.lower()):
        if '[' in part:
            return True
        if re.search(r'[*?]', part) and any(fnmatch.fnmatchcase(h, part) for h in heads):   # no [ here; never
            return True                                                                     # backtracks on ***
    return False


def _unescape(m):
    try:
        return chr(int(m.group(5), 8) if m.group(5) else
                   int(m.group(1) or m.group(2) or m.group(3) or m.group(4) or m.group(6), 16))
    except (ValueError, OverflowError):
        return m.group(0)


def canon(text, blank=True, cue=True):
    """Text as the program at the far end may read it: \\uXXXX, \\xXX, octal and %XX decoded (up to three rounds),
    backslashes and all quotes dropped (blank: \\n \\t \\r read as a blank first), 'a' + 'b' joined, lower case,
    /./ // and /x/../ in paths resolved. '' when no guarded word is left at all (cue=False: the text all the same,
    for a ruleset body, where `"enforcement": "evaluate"` holds no guarded word)."""
    s = text
    if '\\' in s or '%' in s:
        for _ in range(3):
            before = s
            s = ESCAPES.sub(_unescape, s)
            if s == before:
                break
        if blank:
            s = re.sub(r'\\+[ntr]', ' ', s)
        s = s.replace('\\', '')
    s = re.sub(r'["\']\s*[+.]\s*["\']', '', s)
    s = re.sub(r'["\'`]', '', s).lower()
    if cue and not any(c in s for c in CUES):
        return ''
    s = re.sub(r'(?<!:)/(?:\.?/)+', '/', s)
    k = 1
    while k:   # every /x/.. (no round limit: 21 nested ones are no way past the guard)
        s, k = re.subn(r'/(?!\.\.?/)[^/?#\s]+/\.\.(?=[/?#\s]|$)', '', s)
    return s


def named(text):
    """What guarded thing the text names after decoding (for the message), or None."""
    if not text:
        return None
    for blank in ((True, False) if re.search(r'\\+[ntr]', text) else (True,)):
        s = canon(text, blank)
        label = next((label for label, rx in TOKENS if rx.search(s)), None) if s else None
        if label:
            return label
    return None


def shell_uncomment(text):
    """Shell text without its comments, as the shell itself reads them (#1057): a guarded word in a `#` comment
    of a file the shell reads for CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE (~/.bashrc, a sourced file) is no command. Not for the command
    itself (it can read its own comments from $BASH_EXECUTION_STRING), nor for Python, JS or other code: a guess at
    another language's comments is a way past the guard (#1108)."""
    return Lex(text).uncommented()


REFUSE_TAG = "this machine's refuseScripts"   # in the label refuse_script_named() gives
MENTION_HINT = ('If the file only mentions the script (a message, notes), give it to the program as a redirect, '
                '`python3 tools/check.py < msg.md`, which is read as data, or do not pipe or substitute a file that '
                'names a refuse script.')


def unproven(label, what, hint=SEPARATE):
    hint = f'{MENTION_HINT} {hint}' if REFUSE_TAG in label else hint
    return reason(f'a command that names {label} {what}, so the guard cannot prove it is harmless', hint)


def is_gh(tok, ctx):
    """gh under its own name, or under a name the same command gave it (ln -s /usr/bin/gh g; cp gh hub)."""
    base = os.path.basename(tok)
    return base == 'gh' or (base != '' and base in ctx.get('ghs', ()))


_GH_BINS, _WHICH = {}, {}


def gh_file(path, ctx):
    """Whether an executable on disk is gh under another name: a link to it, or a copy of a gh on PATH."""
    try:
        if os.path.basename(os.path.realpath(path)) == 'gh':
            return True
        size = os.path.getsize(path)
        key = ctx['env'].get('PATH') or ''
        if key not in _GH_BINS:
            dirs = dict.fromkeys(key.split(':') + list(STD_BINS) + ['/snap/bin'])
            _GH_BINS[key] = [p for p in (os.path.join(d, 'gh') for d in dirs if d) if os.path.isfile(p)]
        for gh in _GH_BINS[key]:
            if os.path.getsize(gh) == size:
                with open(gh, 'rb') as a, open(path, 'rb') as b:
                    if a.read(65536) == b.read(65536):
                        return True
    except OSError:
        pass
    return False


def which(name, ctx):
    """The file a bare command word runs from PATH, or None."""
    key = (name, ctx['env'].get('PATH') or '')
    if key not in _WHICH:
        _WHICH[key] = next((p for p in (os.path.join(d, name) for d in key[1].split(':') if d)
                            if os.path.isfile(p) and os.access(p, os.X_OK)), None)
    return _WHICH[key]


def is_self(path, ctx):
    """Whether a file is this guard (the file itself or a byte-equal copy): running or testing it is no attack."""
    if SELF and os.path.realpath(path) == SELF:
        return True
    return facts(path, ctx).get('own', False)


def gh_names(toks):
    """The names a command gives a copy of or a link to gh (`ln -s /usr/bin/gh g`: g; `hash -p /usr/bin/gh g`). A
    source the guard cannot read (`$(command -v gh)`) may be gh too."""
    h, _ = head(toks)
    if h is None:
        return set()
    word, rest = os.path.basename(toks[h]), [a for a in toks[h + 1:] if not a.startswith('-') or hidden(a)]
    if word == 'hash' and '-p' in toks and len(rest) >= 2:
        rest = rest[:2]
    elif word not in ('ln', 'cp', 'mv', 'install', 'link', 'rsync'):
        return set()
    if len(rest) < 2 or hidden(rest[-1]) \
            or not any(hidden(a) or os.path.basename(a.rstrip('/')) == 'gh' for a in rest[:-1]):
        return set()
    name = os.path.basename(rest[-1].rstrip('/'))
    return {name} if name not in ('', '.', '..') else set()


def runner_at(toks, start):
    """Behind a command word the guard does not know: where a word it does know how to read starts (a shell, an
    interpreter, git, curl, a wrapper ..), or None. An unknown word must not hide the command that follows it."""
    for i in range(start, len(toks)):
        base = os.path.basename(toks[i])
        if base in RUNNERS or base in SHELLS or base in NET_CLIENTS or base in TEXT_ARGS or base in AWKS \
                or INTERPRETERS.match(base):
            return i
    return None


def exported(toks, flow):
    return 'export' in toks or bool(flow.get('seta')) or \
        (('declare' in toks or 'typeset' in toks) and any(re.fullmatch(r'-[A-Za-z]*x[A-Za-z]*', t) for t in toks))


def gh_gate(args, ctx, own):
    """gh passed the checks above. What is left: a first word that is no gh command (an alias the reader may not
    know, an extension: another program) and words gh hands on to another program."""
    cands = sub_candidates(args)
    first = args[cands[0]] if cands else ''
    if first and first not in GH_COMMANDS:
        if own and not ctx.get('nogate'):
            return unproven(own, f'and runs `gh {redact(first)}`, which is no gh command (an alias, an extension, '
                                 'or a word the guard cannot read)', 'Use the gh command itself, spelled out.')
        ctx['flow']['unknown'] = f'gh {redact(first)}'
    if own and first in ('extension', 'codespace', 'cs'):
        return unproven(own, f'and runs `gh {first}`, which starts other programs')
    if own and first != 'api' and '--' in args and named(' '.join(args[args.index('--') + 1:])):
        return unproven(own, 'behind `--`, where gh hands the words on to another program')
    return None


def rc_files(word, toks, h, ctx):
    """The config files curl or wget would read first, as far as the text and the hook's environment show them."""
    env = dict(ctx['env'])
    for t in toks[:h]:
        m = ASSIGN.match(t)
        if m and not hidden(t):
            env[m.group(1)] = m.group(3)
    home = env.get('HOME') or ''
    if word == 'curl':
        dirs = [env.get('CURL_HOME'), env.get('XDG_CONFIG_HOME'), home, home and os.path.join(home, '.config')]
        return [os.path.join(d, n) for d in dirs if d for n in ('.curlrc', 'curlrc')]
    return [p for p in (env.get('WGETRC'), home and os.path.join(home, '.wgetrc'), '/etc/wgetrc') if p]


def net_gate(word, toks, h, ctx, own):
    """curl, wget and the like passed net_reason. A guarded token passes only as a request path the guard can read,
    with the tool's own config switched off (a ~/.curlrc can turn a read into `request = PUT`)."""
    args = toks[h + 1:]
    files = [m.group(1) for a in args for m in [re.search(r'@([^@=\s]+)$', a)] if m]
    bodies = [ctx.get('stdin') or ''] + [read_named(f, ctx) or '' for f in files]
    if any(GQL_RE.search(c) or GQL_UPDATE.search(c) for c in (canon(x) for x in list(args) + bodies)):
        return reason('a GraphQL mutation that deletes branch protection or a ruleset, or changes a ruleset, sent '
                      f'with `{word}`: the guard reads GraphQL only through `gh api graphql`')
    no_rc = (word == 'curl' and bool(args) and not hidden(args[0]) and (args[0] == '--disable'
                                                                         or re.match(r'-q', args[0]) is not None)) \
        or (word == 'wget' and '--no-config' in args)
    hint = 'Put `-q` (or `--disable`) as the FIRST argument: curl then skips its config file.' if word == 'curl' \
        else 'Add `--no-config`: wget then skips its config files.'
    for i, a in enumerate(args):   # more arguments or addresses, from a file: curl -K/--config, wget -i/--input-file
        after = args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else ''
        short = re.match(r'-[A-Za-z0-9#:]*?%s(.*)$' % ('K' if word == 'curl' else 'i'), a, re.S) \
            if word in ('curl', 'wget') and not a.startswith('--') else None
        if short:   # also joined: -sK file, -Kfile
            name = short.group(1) or after
        elif word == 'curl' and a.startswith('--config') or word == 'wget' and a.startswith('--input-file'):
            name = a.partition('=')[2] or after
        else:
            continue
        tok = named_file(name, ctx)
        if not tok and read_named(name, ctx) is None:
            ctx['flow']['unknown'] = f'{word} with arguments from a file the guard cannot read'
        elif tok:
            return unproven(tok, f'in a file that `{word}` reads its arguments or addresses from '
                                         '(curl -K/--config, wget -i/--input-file)', 'Use `gh api` for it.')
    if any(hidden(a) for a in args):   # "$(cat url.txt)": what the same command sends on may land here
        ctx['flow']['unknown'] = f'{word} with a word the guard cannot read'
    if not no_rc:
        for p in rc_files(word, toks, h, ctx) if word in NET_TOOLS else ():
            tok = file_token(resolve_path(p, ctx['cwd']))
            if tok:
                return unproven(tok, f'in {word}\'s own config file ({os.path.basename(p)}), which {word} reads '
                                     'before its arguments', hint)
        ctx['flow']['unknown'] = f'{word} (it reads its config file first)'
    if not own:
        return None
    if word not in NET_TOOLS:
        return unproven(own, f'and uses `{word}`, an HTTP client the guard has no flag table for',
                        'Use `gh api`, or `curl -q` for a read.')
    if not no_rc:
        return unproven(own, 'with curl, which reads ~/.curlrc (or $CURL_HOME, $XDG_CONFIG_HOME) first: a line '
                             'there can turn a read into a write' if word == 'curl' else
                        'with wget, which reads ~/.wgetrc (or $WGETRC, /etc/wgetrc) first: a line there can turn a '
                        'read into a write', hint)
    if not any(hits(a, k) for a in args for k in GUARDED) or named(' '.join(toks[:h])):
        return unproven(own, f'in an argument of `{word}` that is no request path the guard can read',
                        'Use `gh api` for it.')
    return None


def git_sub(args):
    i = 0
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        if a in ('-C', '-c', '--git-dir', '--work-tree', '--namespace'):
            i += 2
        elif a.startswith('-') or hidden(a) and WILD in a:
            i += 1
        else:
            return a, i
    return '', len(args)


def git_gate(args, ctx, own, fed):
    """git with a guarded token: only subcommands that take text and run none of it (a commit message, a grep
    pattern), with nothing git would run as a command (a pager, an alias, a transport helper)."""
    sub, i = git_sub(args)
    if not sub or sub not in GIT_COMMANDS:   # an alias, git-<name> on PATH, a hidden word
        ctx['flow']['unknown'] = f'git {redact(sub)}'.strip()
    tok = own or fed
    if tok and (sub not in GIT_SAFE or named(' '.join(args[:i])) or any(git_runs(a) for a in args[i + 1:])):
        return unproven(tok, f'and runs `git {redact(sub)}` in a form that can run a command (an alias, a pager, '
                             'a transport, a config value)', 'Keep such text to commit messages and patterns.')
    return None


SED_LONG = ('--expression', '--file', '--line-length', '--in-place', '--quiet', '--silent', '--sandbox', '--debug',
            '--posix', '--regexp-extended', '--separate', '--unbuffered', '--null-data', '--zero-terminated',
            '--follow-symlinks', '--binary', '--help', '--version')


def sed_args(args):
    """sed's arguments as GNU sed reads them: (scripts, script files, input files, --sandbox). A script is the value
    of -e/--expression (also glued: -e1d, -ne1d, --expr=1d), a script file the value of -f/--file; without either
    the first word that is no flag is the script. A long flag may be cut short (--expr)."""
    scripts, files, inputs, sandbox, i, ended = [], [], [], False, 0, False
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        i += 1
        if ended or a == '-' or not a.startswith('-'):
            inputs.append(a)
        elif a == '--':
            ended = True
        elif a.startswith('--'):
            name, eq, value = a.partition('=')
            full = [f for f in SED_LONG if f.startswith(name)]
            full = full[0] if len(full) == 1 or name in full else name
            if full in ('--expression', '--file', '--line-length') and not eq:
                value, i = (args[i] if i < len(args) else ''), i + 1
            if full == '--expression':
                scripts.append(value)
            elif full == '--file':
                files.append(value)
            sandbox = sandbox or full == '--sandbox'
        else:
            for j, ch in enumerate(a[1:], 1):   # a cluster: -ne SCRIPT, -nf FILE, -i.bak, -l 80
                if ch in 'efl':
                    value = a[j + 1:]
                    if not value:
                        value, i = (args[i] if i < len(args) else ''), i + 1
                    (scripts if ch == 'e' else files if ch == 'f' else []).append(value)
                    break
                if ch == 'i':   # its suffix is the rest of the word
                    break
    if not scripts and not files and inputs:
        scripts.append(inputs.pop(0))
    return scripts, files, inputs, sandbox


SED_PLAIN = set('=dDgGhHlnNpPxzF')        # sed commands with no argument
SED_NUM = set('lLqQ')                     # ... with an optional number
SED_LINE = set('aicrRwWbtTv:')            # ... whose argument is the rest of the line (b t T v: or up to ;)


def sed_program(script):
    """What a sed script runs: (commands of `e CMD`, whether it runs its input as shell: a bare `e` or the s///e
    flag, files it writes: w/W and s///w). A script it cannot read (an unknown command, an open s///) counts as
    running its input. GNU sed's grammar: [addr[,addr]][!]cmd, commands split by ; or line breaks, { } blocks."""
    cmds, runs, writes, i, n = [], False, [], 0, len(script)

    def regex(i, delim):   # index behind the closing delim of a regex or replacement that starts at i
        while i < n and script[i] != delim:
            if script[i] == '\\':
                i += 1
            elif script[i] == '\n' and delim != '\n':
                return -1
            i += 1
        return i + 1 if i < n else -1

    def addr(i):
        if i < n and script[i] in '0123456789':
            while i < n and script[i] in '0123456789~':
                i += 1
        elif i < n and script[i] == '$':
            i += 1
        elif i < n and script[i] in '/\\':
            delim = script[i + 1:i + 2] if script[i] == '\\' else '/'
            i = regex(i + (2 if script[i] == '\\' else 1), delim) if delim else -1
            while 0 <= i < n and script[i] in 'IM':
                i += 1
        return i

    def rest_of_line(i):
        j = script.find('\n', i)
        return n if j < 0 else j

    while i < n:
        c = script[i]
        if c in ' \t\n;}':
            i += 1
            continue
        if c == '#':
            i = rest_of_line(i)
            continue
        i = addr(i)
        if 0 <= i < n and script[i] == ',':
            i += 1
            while i < n and script[i] in ' \t':
                i += 1
            if i < n and script[i] in '+~':
                i += 1
                while i < n and script[i].isdigit():
                    i += 1
            else:
                i = addr(i)
        while 0 <= i < n and script[i] in ' \t!':
            i += 1
        if i < 0 or i >= n:
            return cmds, runs or i < 0, writes
        c, i = script[i], i + 1
        if c == '{' or c == '}':
            continue
        if c == 'e':
            j = rest_of_line(i)
            text = script[i:j].strip()
            if text:
                cmds.append(text)
            else:
                runs = True
            i = j
            continue
        if c in SED_LINE:
            j = rest_of_line(i)
            if c in 'btTv:':   # a label ends at ; too (GNU)
                k = script.find(';', i, j)
                j = j if k < 0 else k
            while c in 'aic' and j < n and (len(script[:j]) - len(script[:j].rstrip('\\'))) % 2:
                j = rest_of_line(j + 1)   # a\ text\ more: each line that ends in \ goes on
            if c in 'wW':
                writes.append(script[i:j].strip())
            i = j
            continue
        if c in SED_NUM:
            while i < n and script[i] in ' \t0123456789':
                i += 1
        elif c in 'sy':
            if i >= n or script[i] in '\n\\':
                return cmds, True, writes
            delim = script[i]
            i = regex(i + 1, delim)
            i = regex(i, delim) if i >= 0 else -1
            if i < 0:
                return cmds, True, writes
            while c == 's' and i < n and script[i] in 'gpiImMe0123456789w':
                if script[i] == 'e':
                    runs = True
                if script[i] == 'w':
                    j = rest_of_line(i)
                    writes.append(script[i + 1:j].strip())
                    i = j
                    break
                i += 1
        elif c not in SED_PLAIN:
            return cmds, True, writes   # not a sed command the guard knows: it may run something
        while i < n and script[i] in ' \t':
            i += 1
        if i < n and script[i] not in ';\n}#':
            return cmds, True, writes   # more after a command (an unknown flag, a hidden part): may be `e`
    return cmds, runs, writes


def sed_reason(args, ctx, own, fed):
    """sed runs commands with `e CMD` (any address: 1e, $e, /x/e), runs its input as shell with a bare `e` or the
    s///e flag, and writes files with w. Every script it gets is read (-e, -f files, the first word): `e CMD` as
    shell text; a run of its input is refused when the input or the script names a guarded token."""
    scripts, files, inputs, sandbox = sed_args(args)
    flow, unknown = ctx['flow'], False
    for f in files:   # a file the same command writes first (printf .. > s.sed; sed -f s.sed) is not read
        text = None if std_stream(f) or written_here(f, ctx) else read_named(f, ctx)
        if text is None:
            tok = not std_stream(f) and named_file(f, ctx)
            if tok:
                return unproven(tok, 'in a script file `sed` runs (-f), which the guard cannot read in full')
            unknown = True   # stdin, a hidden name, a file that is not there yet
        else:
            scripts.append(text)
    if sandbox:   # --sandbox: sed refuses e, r and w
        return None
    runs, writes = unknown, []
    for script in scripts:   # a hidden part ($X) stays in: in a command or flag place it reads as unknown (runs),
        cmds, ran, wrote = sed_program(script)   # inside a regex or replacement it is harmless
        runs, writes = runs or ran, writes + wrote
        for text in cmds:
            flow['ran'] = True
            r = decide_text(text, dict(ctx, stdin=None, cmd={}, leak=False, targets=()))
            if r:
                return r
    tok = own or fed or next((t for t in (data_token(a, ctx, runs) for a in inputs if a != '-') if t), None)
    if runs:
        flow['ran'] = True
        if tok:
            return unproven(tok, 'where `sed` runs its text as shell commands (the `e` command or the s///e flag)')
        flow['unknown'] = 'sed e'   # its input may come from anywhere
    if writes and tok:   # sed -n 'w run.sh' p.txt: the text gets a name a later word may run
        flow['free'], flow['free_to'] = tok, 'writes to a file with `sed w`'
    return None


def data_token(tok, ctx, runs=False):
    """The guarded token in a file that cat/cp/.. hand on: its text as read_text() gives it, so data over MAX_FILE or
    that cannot be opened passes unread (lockout care), whatever its name (`cat app.js | gzip`). Only in a run
    position, where a shell or interpreter runs the text (`cat f | grep x | bash`, `eval "$(cat f)"`; runs: the
    command itself runs it, sed e), is a file read in pieces and counts when unread (file_token). A line that calls
    a refuseScripts entry counts wherever the text goes on, as any guarded token does: the guard cannot tell every
    place that runs it (`$(cat f)`, `| heavy bash`, `| bash /dev/stdin`, `| at now`, `| awk '{system($0)}'` ..), so
    a text that goes only into a program that reads it as data (ctx quiet: `| python3 check.py`, `| gzip`, see
    ends_text) passes; elsewhere the refusal names the way out (`< msg.md`). A copy under a name the text runs is
    read where it is written (segment_reason)."""
    runs = runs or ctx.get('feeds_code')
    for d in [] if hidden(tok) else folders(ctx):
        p = resolve_path(tok, d)
        f = facts(p, ctx, ('named',))
        t = f['named'] if f['text'] else runs and file_token(p)
        if t:
            return t
        # the whole text is read (the cache holds no text): only when the cached names found nothing and the
        # machine names a script at all
        t = REFUSE_SCRIPTS and f['text'] and (runs or not ctx.get('quiet')) and refuse_script_named(body(p, ctx))
        if t:
            return t
    return None


def refuse_script_named(text):
    """A machine file refuseScripts entry that a line of the text calls (its name and its flag as words on one line,
    `python3 garden.py --fix`), as a label for unproven(), or None. Text that a shell runs (sed e, cat f | bash,
    eval "$(cat f)") would run it, as the same line in a script does."""
    text = re.sub(r'\\\r?\n', '', text) if text else text   # a line split by backslash-newline is one line
    for rs in REFUSE_SCRIPTS if text else ():
        rx = r'(?<![\w.-])%s(?![\w.-])[^\n]*?(?<![\w-])%s(?![\w-])' % (re.escape(rs['name']), re.escape(rs['flag']))
        if re.search(rx, text):
            return f'`{rs["name"]} {rs["flag"]}` ({REFUSE_TAG}: {rs.get("why") or "refused"})'
    return None


RUN_FLAGS = {'rg': ('--pre', '--hostname-bin'), 'sort': ('--compress-program',)}


def run_programs(word, args):
    """The programs a data word runs by a flag: rg --pre X / --hostname-bin X (exact names), sort
    --compress-program X (also any prefix of it from --co, as GNU getopt takes). A flag with no value: WILD."""
    out = []
    for i, a in enumerate(args):
        if a == '--':
            break
        name, eq, val = a.partition('=')
        long = RUN_FLAGS.get(word, ())
        if name in long or (word == 'sort' and len(name) >= 4 and '--compress-program'.startswith(name)):
            out.append(val if eq else (args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else WILD))
    return out


def into_path_reason(args, ctx):
    """mv tools ~/.local/bin, cp -r tools/. ~/.local/bin, cp x ~/.local/bin: files that go into a PATH folder under
    $HOME become command words a later word (or call) runs, so a file among them that names a guarded token is
    refused. Up to 256 files of a folder are looked at."""
    words = [a for a in args if not a.startswith('-')]
    dest = next((a.partition('=')[2] for a in args if a.startswith('--target-directory=')), None) or \
        next((args[i + 1] for i, a in enumerate(args[:-1]) if a == '-t'), None) or (words[-1] if words else None)
    env = ctx['env']
    dirs = {os.path.realpath(d) for d in home_path_dirs(env.get('PATH') or '',
                                                         env.get('HOME') or os.path.expanduser('~'))}
    if not dest or hidden(dest) or not dirs or \
            not any(os.path.realpath(resolve_path(dest, d)) in dirs for d in folders(ctx)):
        return None
    for src in (w for w in words if w != dest):
        top = os.path.dirname(src) if hidden(src) else src   # cp tools/* ~/.local/bin: the folder tools/
        if hidden(top) or not top:
            continue
        for d in folders(ctx):
            p = resolve_path(top, d)
            try:
                with os.scandir(p) as it:
                    files = [e.path for _, e in zip(range(256), it) if e.is_file()]
            except OSError:
                files = [p] if not hidden(src) and os.path.isfile(p) else []
            for f in files:
                fx = facts(f, ctx, ('named',))
                tok = fx['named'] if fx['text'] else file_token(f)
                if tok:
                    return unproven(tok, f'in a file of the folder {redact(top)}, which this command puts into a PATH '
                                         'folder, where a command word of that name runs it')
    return None


# Interpreter flags that take the next word as their value (python3 -W ignore, node -r x, ruby -I lib, perl -I lib)
INTERP_VALUE = {'python': ('-W', '-X', '-Q'), 'pypy': ('-W', '-X', '-Q'),
                'node': ('-r', '--require', '--import', '--loader', '--experimental-loader', '--input-type', '-C',
                         '--conditions', '--title', '--env-file'),
                'ruby': ('-I', '-r', '-E', '-C', '-F'), 'perl': ('-I', '-M', '-m'), 'php': ('-d', '-c', '-z')}


def stdin_runs(toks, how, loose):
    """Whether a command runs what it reads on stdin as shell or code: a shell or interpreter with no script of
    its own (`bash`, `sh -s x`, `python3 -`), not `python3 load.py` or `awk '{..}'`, whose stdin is data."""
    if not toks or doc_kind(toks, how, loose, '') == 'data':
        return False
    h, via = head(toks)
    if bare_shell(toks) or xargs_runs_words(toks, via):
        return True
    if 'shell' in via or h is None:   # head() takes the shell's script as the command word
        return h is None or '-s' in toks[:h] or std_stream(toks[h])
    word, args, i = os.path.basename(toks[h]), toks[h + 1:], 0
    takes = next((v for k, v in INTERP_VALUE.items() if word.startswith(k)), ())
    while i < len(args):   # python3 -W ignore < f: the value of a flag is no script name
        a = args[i]
        if a in ('-c', '-e', '-m', '--eval', '-p', '--print') or not a.startswith('-') or a == '-':
            return std_stream(a)
        i += 2 if a in takes else 1
    return True


def data_gate(word, args, ctx, own, fed, loose):
    """A command word that only handles text. Its text may name a guarded token; where the text goes is noted (a
    pipe, a file, $(..)), and decide() refuses when the same command also runs a word the guard cannot read."""
    flow = ctx['flow']
    if word == 'find':   # -exec cmd ..: a command of its own
        for i, a in enumerate(args):
            if a in ('-exec', '-execdir', '-ok', '-okdir') and args[i + 1:]:
                r = segment_reason(args[i + 1:], dict(ctx, stdin=None, cmd={}, leak=False, targets=()), loose)
                if r:
                    return r
    if word == 'set' and (any(re.fullmatch(r'-[A-Za-z]*a[A-Za-z]*', a) for a in args) or 'allexport' in args):
        flow['seta'] = True
    if word in ('typeset', 'readonly', 'local', 'declare', 'export'):
        if own and exported([word] + args, flow):
            flow['env'] = own
        return None
    if word == 'sed':
        r = sed_reason(args, ctx, own, fed)
        if r:
            return r
    progs = run_programs(word, args)
    if own and progs:
        return unproven(own, f'in `{word}` arguments that can run a command (rg --pre, sort --compress-program)')
    for prog in progs:   # rg --pre ./x.sh: x.sh runs on every file, read as the command it is
        flow['ran'] = True
        r = segment_reason([prog, WILD], dict(ctx, stdin=None, cmd={}, leak=False, targets=()), loose)
        if r:
            return r
    if word in COPIERS:
        r = into_path_reason(args, ctx)
        if r:
            return r
    tok, moved = own or fed, False
    if not tok and (ctx.get('leak') and (word in TEXT_FILTERS or ctx.get('feeds_code')) or word in COPIERS):
        # cat x | ..: the file's text goes on; cp x run.sh: it gets a name the next word may run; sort x | bash,
        # nl x | cut -f2 | at now: whatever the filter, the text that goes on comes from the files it reads
        tok = next((t for t in (data_token(a, ctx) for a in args if not a.startswith('-')) if t), None)
        moved = word in COPIERS
    if not tok and ctx.get('leak') and word in TEXT_FILTERS | {'tee'} and REFUSE_SCRIPTS \
            and (ctx.get('feeds_code') or not ctx.get('quiet')):   # sort < q.txt | bash: a line of its stdin
        tok = refuse_script_named(ctx.get('stdin') or '')
    targets = list(ctx.get('targets', ())) + (args if word in WRITERS else [])
    if tok and any(HOOK_PATH.search(t) for t in targets):
        return unproven(tok, 'and writes it under .git/ or a hooks folder, where git runs it later')
    if tok and ctx.get('proven'):   # the text is a script the same command runs: it was read as shell text
        return None
    if tok and (ctx.get('leak') or moved or (own and word in WRITERS)):
        flow['free'], flow['free_to'] = tok, flow_to(word, ctx, moved)
    return None


def file_hint(word):
    """What to do instead when text that names a token goes into the words or input of a word the guard does not
    know: a file argument is data to the guard."""
    return (f'If `{word}` only handles the text, write the text to a file first (an edit tool, or a Bash call of its '
            f'own) and give `{word}` that file as an argument, not through a pipe, $(..), its input or its words.')


def flow_to(word, ctx, moved):
    """Where a data word sends text that names a guarded token, for the refusal: `writes it to the file x.md`."""
    files = [f'`{redact(t)}`' for t in ctx.get('targets', ()) if not hidden(t)][:3]
    if files:
        return f'writes to the file{"s" if len(files) > 1 else ""} {", ".join(files)}'
    if moved:
        return f'gives a file that holds it another name with `{word}`'
    if word in WRITERS:
        return f'writes to a file with `{word}`'
    cmd = ctx.get('cmd') or {}
    if cmd.get('sub'):
        return f'hands on from `{word}` into the words of another command ($(..))'
    if cmd.get('pipe'):
        return f'pipes on from `{word}`'
    return f'passes on from `{word}`'


SHELL_EXT = ('.sh', '.bash', '.zsh')
CODE_EXT = ('.py', '.pyw', '.js', '.mjs', '.cjs', '.ts', '.mts', '.cts', '.jsx', '.tsx', '.rb', '.pl', '.pm', '.php',
            '.lua', '.tcl', '.ps1', '.psm1', '.awk', '.go', '.groovy', '.kts', '.swift', '.exp', '.fish', '.nu')
LOADS = {'-r', '--require', '--import', '--loader', '-f', '--file', '-m', '--module'}   # flags that name code to run


def arg_scripts_reason(word, args, ctx):
    """Files handed to a command word the guard does not know (`taskset 1 ./x.sh`, `at -f x.sh`, `uv run x.py`,
    `chrt 1 ./tool.py`): the word may run them. A shell script is read in full, as if it were the command word
    itself. A file that looks like code in another language (its name, a `#!` line, the executable bit) may not
    name a guarded token. Plain data files (.md, .json, .yml, .txt ..) are not looked at."""
    for a in args:
        a = a.partition('=')[2] if a.startswith('--') and '=' in a else a
        if a.startswith('-') or hidden(a) or not a:
            continue
        files = cmd_files(a, ctx, False) + [(p, d) for d in folders(ctx) for p in [resolve_path(a, d)]]
        for p, d in dict.fromkeys(files):
            f = facts(p, ctx)
            if not f['text'] or is_self(p, ctx):
                r = not f['text'] and unread(p, any_file=False)
                if r:
                    return r
                continue
            if a.endswith(SHELL_EXT) or f['bang'] in SHELLS:
                r = file_reason(p, dict(ctx, cwd=d), force_shell=True)
                if r:
                    return r
            elif (a.endswith(CODE_EXT) or f['bang'] is not None or os.access(p, os.X_OK)) and \
                    facts(p, ctx, ('named',)).get('named'):
                return unproven(facts(p, ctx)['named'], f'in `{redact(os.path.basename(a))}`, a code file that is handed to '
                                             f'`{word}`, which may run it (the guard cannot follow what that word '
                                             'does with its arguments)',
                                'If the file is only data here (a path to commit, lint or copy), name its folder '
                                'instead, or use a tool the guard knows (git add, cp).')
    return None


def interp_files(kind, args, pos, ctx):
    """[(file, folder)]: the code files an interpreter runs for these arguments. The script is the first of its
    leading words that is a file (`python3 x.py a b`: x.py; `deno run x.ts`: x.ts; what follows the script is the
    script's own business), plus what a flag loads (`node -r ./x.js`, `python3 -m x`, `php -f x.php`)."""
    names = []
    if kind not in AWKS and not {'-m', '-c', '-e', '-', '--eval'} & set(args):
        names.append([a for a in pos[:3] if not hidden(a)])
    for i, a in enumerate(args):
        flag, eq, value = a.partition('=')
        if flag in LOADS and not hidden(a):
            value = value if eq else (args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else '')
            if value and not hidden(value) and not value.startswith('-'):
                if flag in ('-m', '--module'):
                    mod = value.replace('.', '/')
                    names += [[mod + '.py'], [mod + '/__main__.py']]
                else:
                    names += [[value], [value + '.js'], [value + '.rb'], [value + '.pl']]
    out = []
    for group in names:   # of a group, the first name that is a file
        for name in group:
            found = [(p, d) for d in folders(ctx) for p in [resolve_path(name, d)] if os.path.isfile(p)]
            if found:
                out += found
                break
    return out


AWK_STRING = r'"(?:[^"\\\n]|\\.)*"'   # an awk "string" literal
# awk calls that run a command built from what it reads: system(EXPR) and print | EXPR, EXPR | getline (not a
# "literal" alone), a coprocess |&
AWK_RUNS = re.compile(r'\bsystem\s*\((?!\s*%s\s*\))|(?<!\|)\|&?(?!\|)\s*(?!getline\b)(?=[A-Za-z_$(])'
                      r'|[\w$)\]]\s*\|&?\s*getline\b' % AWK_STRING)
# perl and ruby; also `do+$_` (a unary plus), ruby `load $_` and instance_eval/class_eval (eval after a `_`)
PERL_RUNS = re.compile(r'(?:\b|_)(?:system|exec|qx|spawn|popen|eval|open|open[23]|readpipe|IO\.popen|Open[23]|syscall)\b'
                       r'|\b(?:do|require)\s*[$("\'+]|\bload\b|`|%x')


def code_args(args, perl, script=False):
    """perl/ruby arguments: (-e code, perl -E code, input files: the words after it, which -n/-p and <>/ARGF read).
    script: also give the script file, the first word when there is no -e (None without one)."""
    code, inputs, i, ended = [], [], 0, False
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        i += 1
        if ended or a == '-' or not a.startswith('-'):
            inputs.append(a)
            if not code:   # no -e: the first word is the script file, the words after it its input
                return (code, args[i:], a) if script else (code, [])
        elif a == '--':
            ended = True
        else:
            for j, ch in enumerate(a[1:], 1):   # a cluster: -lne CODE, -I lib
                if ch == 'e' or ch == 'E' and perl:
                    value = a[j + 1:]
                    if not value:
                        value, i = (args[i] if i < len(args) else ''), i + 1
                    code.append(value)
                    break
                if ch in 'IMmrCxFi' or ch == 'E' and not perl:   # the rest of the word is the value (or the
                    if j == len(a) - 1 and ch in 'IMmrE':          # next word for -I lib, -r lib, ruby -E enc)
                        i += 1
                    break
    return (code, inputs, None) if script else (code, inputs)


def awk_pipe_runs(prog):
    """An awk `print | "command"` whose command the guard does not read as one pipe stage: more than one simple
    command in the literal (`"true; sh"`), or a literal joined to more text (`"sort" ";sh"`, `"sort" x`)."""
    for m in re.finditer(r'\|&?\s*(%s)' % AWK_STRING, prog):
        after = prog[m.end():].lstrip(' \t')
        if re.search(r'[;&|`$()<>\n\\]', m.group(1)[1:-1]) or (after and after[0] not in ';}\n#'):
            return True
    return False


def input_runs_reason(kind, args, ctx):
    """awk '{system($0)}' q.txt, awk '{print | "sh"}' q.txt, perl -ne 'system $_' q.txt: the lines of the input
    files run as commands, as in `cat q.txt | sh`. A "command" awk pipes its text into is read as that pipe
    (`cat q.txt | sort -u` passes), a system("command") as shell text."""
    script = None
    if kind in AWKS:
        progs, vars_, inputs, unread = awk_args(args, ctx)
        runs = unread or any(AWK_RUNS.search(p) or awk_pipe_runs(p) for p in progs)
        for prog in progs:   # getline < "file": that file is input too
            for m in re.finditer(r'\bgetline\b[^;{}\n<]*<\s*(%s|[A-Za-z_]\w*)' % AWK_STRING, prog):
                t = m.group(1)
                inputs.append(t[1:-1] if t.startswith('"') else vars_.get(t, WILD))
        for prog in progs:
            for m in re.finditer(r'\bsystem\s*\(\s*(%s)\s*\)' % AWK_STRING, prog):
                r = decide_text(m.group(1)[1:-1], dict(ctx, stdin=None, cmd={}, leak=False, targets=()))
                if r:
                    return r
            for m in re.finditer(r'\|&?\s*(%s)' % AWK_STRING, prog):
                for f in [a for a in inputs if a != '-' and not hidden(a)][:8]:
                    r = decide_text(f'cat {shlex.quote(f)} | {m.group(1)[1:-1]}',
                                    dict(ctx, stdin=None, cmd={}, leak=False, targets=()))
                    if r:
                        return r
    elif os.path.basename(kind).startswith(('perl', 'ruby')):
        code, inputs, script = code_args(args, os.path.basename(kind).startswith('perl'), script=True)
        text = None if script is None or std_stream(script) or written_here(script, ctx) \
            else read_named(script, ctx)   # perl run.pl q.txt: the script is read as its -e code would be
        code += [] if text is None else [text]
        runs = any(PERL_RUNS.search(c) for c in code) or (script is not None and text is None)
    else:
        return None
    tok = runs and REFUSE_SCRIPTS and refuse_script_named(ctx.get('stdin') or '')   # awk '{system($0)}' < q.txt
    if tok:
        return unproven(tok, f'in the input of `{kind}`, whose lines it runs as commands (system, a pipe to a '
                             'command, qx)')
    for f in inputs if runs else ():
        tok = f != '-' and data_token(f, ctx, runs=True)
        if tok:
            return unproven(tok, f'in the file {redact(f)}, whose lines `{kind}` runs as commands (system, a pipe '
                                 'to a command, qx)')
    return None


def launcher_gate(toks, h, ctx, own, fed, loose):
    """A command word the guard does not know. With a guarded token in its words or its input it is refused: the
    guard cannot tell what the word does with them. Without one, the words behind it are still read (a shell, an
    interpreter, a script file), and decide() refuses when token text flows anywhere in the same command."""
    word = toks[h]
    name = redact(os.path.basename(word)) if not hidden(word) else 'a command word from a variable'
    if (own or fed) and not ctx.get('nogate'):
        return unproven(own or fed, f'and runs `{name}`, a command word the guard does not know (a launcher, a '
                                    'project tool, a renamed program ..)', file_hint(name))
    i = runner_at(toks, h + 1)
    r = (i is not None and segment_reason(toks[i:], ctx, loose)) or arg_scripts_reason(name, toks[h + 1:], ctx)
    if r:
        return r
    ctx['flow']['unknown'] = name
    return None


def flow_reason(flow):
    """The whole command read: token text that went somewhere, next to a word the guard could not read."""
    if flow.get('free') and flow.get('unknown'):
        to = flow.get('free_to') or 'passes on (a pipe, a file, a variable)'
        return unproven(flow['free'], f'in text it {to}, and it also runs `{flow["unknown"]}`, which the guard '
                                      'cannot read and which could run that text',
                        file_hint(flow['unknown']) if to.startswith(('pipes', 'hands on')) else
                        f'If nothing here switches anything off, run them as separate Bash calls: the part that {to} '
                        f'in one, the part with `{flow["unknown"]}` in another. Each passes on its own.')
    if flow.get('env') and (flow.get('unknown') or flow.get('ran')):
        return unproven(flow['env'], 'in an exported variable, which the programs run after it may take as a '
                                     'command (PAGER, GIT_SSH_COMMAND ..)')
    return None


# ---------- decision

def path_script(tok, ctx):
    """A text file (a script, not a compiled program) of this name in a PATH folder under $HOME: `ls` there is
    that script, not the system's ls, and is read as one (an earlier call may have left it)."""
    return next((p for p, _ in cmd_files(tok, ctx, False) if facts(p, ctx)['text']), None)


def cmd_files(tok, ctx, via_shell):
    """[(file, folder)]: the script files a command word may run, one per folder the command may run in."""
    out = []
    for d in folders(ctx):
        p = resolve_cmd(tok, dict(ctx, cwd=d), via_shell)
        if p and os.path.isfile(p) and all(p != q for q, _ in out):
            out.append((p, d))
    return out


def flag_values(toks, h):
    """The words head() took as the value of a wrapper's flag (`sudo -u NAME`). Which flags take a value depends
    on the wrapper, so such a word may be the command word itself (`env -i bash x.sh`, `bash -e x.sh`)."""
    end = len(toks) if h is None else h
    return [i for i in range(1, end) if toks[i - 1] in ARG_FLAGS and not toks[i].startswith('-')]


CODEX_SAFE = {'login', 'logout', 'help', 'completion', 'mcp', '--version', '-V', '--help', '-h'}
# codex's options that take a value (codex --help): `codex -m o3 exec x` runs `exec`, not `o3`
CODEX_VALUE = {'-c', '--config', '--enable', '--disable', '--remote', '--remote-auth-token-env', '-i', '--image',
               '-m', '--model', '--local-provider', '-p', '--profile', '-s', '--sandbox', '-C', '--cd', '--add-dir',
               '-a', '--ask-for-approval'}
# claude words that start no session (a session started with CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE from a file is what matters)
CLAUDE_TOOLS = {'mcp', 'config', 'update', 'doctor', 'install', 'auth', 'plugin', 'plugins', 'setup-token',
                'migrate-installer', 'logs', 'purge', 'import', 'auto-mode', '--version', '-v', '--help', '-h'}
NO_HOOKS_ENV = 'CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE'   # bare mode: Claude Code runs no hooks, managed ones neither
# where the programs are installed: the native build's version folder, the npm packages
AGENT_PATH = re.compile(r'/claude/versions/|@anthropic-ai/claude-code(?:/|$)|@openai/codex(?:-[\w-]+)?(?:/|$)', re.I)
AGENT_PKGS = {'@anthropic-ai/claude-code': 'claude', '@openai/codex': 'codex', 'claude': 'claude', 'codex': 'codex'}
PKG_RUNNERS = {'npx': (), 'bunx': (), 'pnpx': (), 'pnpm': ('dlx',), 'yarn': ('dlx',), 'npm': ('exec', 'x'),
               'bun': ('x',)}
# the shell start files of the agents' user: a `claude` session the Bash tool starts may get its variables from them
START_FILES = ('.bashrc', '.bash_profile', '.bash_login', '.profile', '.zshrc', '.zshenv', '.zprofile', '.zlogin')
KNOWN_WORDS = DATA | QUICK_WORDS | WRAPPERS | NET_CLIENTS | {'git', 'gh', 'ssh', 'make', 'npm', 'pnpm', 'yarn'}
_AGENT_BINS = {}


def agent_path_kind(path):
    m = AGENT_PATH.search(path or '')
    return None if not m else ('codex' if 'codex' in m.group(0).lower() else 'claude')


def agent_copy(path, ctx):
    """'claude'/'codex' when a file is a copy of the installed program (same size, same first 64 KB)."""
    try:
        size = os.path.getsize(path)
        for kind in ('claude', 'codex'):
            key = (kind, ctx['env'].get('PATH') or '')
            if key not in _AGENT_BINS:
                p = which(kind, ctx)
                _AGENT_BINS[key] = os.path.realpath(p) if p else None
            real = _AGENT_BINS[key]
            if real and real != path and os.path.isfile(real) and os.path.getsize(real) == size:
                with open(real, 'rb') as a, open(path, 'rb') as b:
                    if a.read(65536) == b.read(65536):
                        return kind
    except OSError:
        pass
    return None


def agent_kind(tok, ctx, script=False):
    """'claude' or 'codex' when a word runs one of them: by name, by a name the same command gave it (ln -s ..),
    by the path of its program (.../claude/versions/2.1.3, @openai/codex), by a link to it or by a copy of the
    installed one; 'maybe' for a name the same command gave a program it cannot read; else None. script: the
    word is a script an interpreter runs (`node cli.js`)."""
    if not tok or hidden(tok):
        return None
    base = os.path.basename(tok).lower()
    if base in ('claude', 'codex'):
        return base
    given = ctx.get('agents', {}).get(base)
    if given:
        return given
    kind = agent_path_kind(tok)
    if kind or (not script and '/' not in tok and (base in KNOWN_WORDS or INTERPRETERS.match(base))):
        return kind
    p = resolve_path(tok, ctx['cwd']) if script or '/' in tok else which(tok, ctx)
    if not p or not os.path.isfile(p):
        return None
    real = os.path.realpath(p)
    return agent_path_kind(real) or {'claude': 'claude', 'codex': 'codex'}.get(os.path.basename(real).lower()) \
        or agent_copy(real, ctx)


def agent_names(toks, ctx):
    """The names a command gives a link to or a copy of claude or codex (`ln -s ~/.local/share/claude/versions/2.1.3
    cc`: cc), as gh_names() does for gh. A source the guard cannot read gives 'maybe'."""
    h, _ = head(toks)
    if h is None or os.path.basename(toks[h]) not in ('ln', 'cp', 'mv', 'install', 'link', 'rsync'):
        return {}
    rest = [a for a in toks[h + 1:] if not a.startswith('-') or hidden(a)]
    if len(rest) < 2 or hidden(rest[-1]):
        return {}
    kinds = [agent_kind(a, ctx) or ('maybe' if hidden(a) else None) for a in rest[:-1]]
    kind = next((k for k in kinds if k and k != 'maybe'), None) or next((k for k in kinds if k), None)
    name = os.path.basename(rest[-1].rstrip('/')).lower()
    return {name: kind} if kind and name not in ('', '.', '..') else {}


def package_agent(toks, h, ctx):
    """(kind, the agent's own words) when the command word runs claude or codex through a package runner (npx,
    bunx, pnpm dlx, npm exec ..) or an interpreter (`node .../@anthropic-ai/claude-code/cli.js`); else (None, [])."""
    base, rest = os.path.basename(toks[h]).lower(), toks[h + 1:]
    if INTERPRETERS.match(base):
        i = next((i for i, a in enumerate(rest) if not a.startswith('-')), None)
        kind = i is not None and agent_kind(rest[i], ctx, script=True)
        return (kind, rest[i + 1:]) if kind else (None, [])
    if base not in PKG_RUNNERS or (PKG_RUNNERS[base] and rest[:1] and rest[0] not in PKG_RUNNERS[base]) \
            or (PKG_RUNNERS[base] and not rest):
        return None, []
    rest = rest[1:] if PKG_RUNNERS[base] else rest
    pkgs, i = [], 0
    while i < len(rest):
        a = rest[i]
        if a in ('-p', '--package'):
            pkgs.append(rest[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(rest) else '')
            i += 2
            continue
        if a.startswith('--package='):
            pkgs.append(a.partition('=')[2])
        elif a in ('-c', '--call'):   # npm exec -c 'text': shell text, read by the shell reading
            return None, []
        elif a != '--' and not a.startswith('-'):
            break
        i += 1
    word = rest[i] if i < len(rest) else ''
    names = [re.sub(r'(?<=.)@[^/]*$', '', p.lower()) for p in pkgs + [word] if p]
    kind = next((AGENT_PKGS[n] for n in names if n in AGENT_PKGS), None)
    return (kind, rest[i + 1:]) if kind else (None, [])


def start_files(ctx):
    """Why a `claude` session may get CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE from a file the command or its shell reads (a sourced
    file, BASH_ENV/ENV, --rcfile, the user's shell start files), as far as the guard can see; else None. Not
    followed: files those files read in turn."""
    for f in ctx.get('sourced', ()):
        if hidden(f):
            return 'the command reads a file the guard cannot name (`. "$F"`) that may set ' + NO_HOOKS_ENV
        text = read_named(f, ctx)
        if text is None:
            return f'the command reads {f}, which the guard cannot read, and it may set {NO_HOOKS_ENV}'
        if NO_HOOKS_ENV.lower() in shell_uncomment(text).lower():
            return f'the command reads {f}, which names {NO_HOOKS_ENV}'
    if re.search(r'(?<![\w$])(?:BASH_ENV|ENV)=', ctx.get('all') or ''):
        return f'the command sets BASH_ENV or ENV: a file the shell reads first may set {NO_HOOKS_ENV}'
    home = ctx['env'].get('HOME') or ''
    for name in START_FILES if os.path.isabs(home) else ():
        text = read_text(os.path.join(home, name))
        if text and NO_HOOKS_ENV.lower() in shell_uncomment(text).lower():
            return f'~/{name} names {NO_HOOKS_ENV}'
    return None


def nested_agent(toks, h, ctx):
    """Why this command word starts an agent session without this machine's hooks, or None."""
    if h is None:
        return None
    tok, args = toks[h], toks[h + 1:]
    kind = agent_kind(tok, ctx)
    shown = os.path.basename(tok) if kind and kind != 'maybe' else kind or ''
    if not kind:
        kind, pargs = package_agent(toks, h, ctx)
        if kind:
            args, shown = pargs, f'{os.path.basename(tok)} ... {kind}'
    if not kind and hidden(tok):
        # `$(which claude) --bare`, `env $(cat vars) claude -p x`: the word is unknown, what follows may say more
        j = next((j for j in range(h + 1, len(toks)) if agent_kind(toks[j], ctx) in ('claude', 'codex')), None)
        if j is not None:
            if agent_kind(toks[j], ctx) == 'claude' and claude_starts(toks[j + 1:]):
                return (f'`{os.path.basename(toks[j])}` behind a word the guard cannot read (`env $(..)`, `$X`), '
                        f'which may set {NO_HOOKS_ENV}, starts a session that may run without this machine\'s hooks')
            return nested_agent(toks, j, ctx)
        if re.search(r'(?<![\w-])codex(?![\w-])', ctx.get('raw') or '', re.I) and codex_sub(args) not in CODEX_SAFE:
            return ('a program the guard cannot name, in a command that names codex (`$(which codex) exec`), may start '
                    'an agent session that runs no Claude Code hooks')
        kind, shown = 'maybe', 'a program the guard cannot name'
    if kind in ('claude', 'maybe'):
        flags = [a for a in args if a in ('--bare', '--safe-mode') or a.startswith(('--bare=', '--safe-mode='))]
        if flags:
            return f'`{shown} {flags[0]}` starts a session that runs without this machine\'s hooks'
    if kind == 'claude':
        if any(NO_HOOKS_ENV in t for t in toks[:h]) or NO_HOOKS_ENV in (ctx.get('all') or ''):
            return f'`{shown}` with {NO_HOOKS_ENV} set starts a session that runs without this machine\'s hooks'
        why = claude_starts(args) and start_files(ctx)
        if why:
            return f'`{shown}` starts a session, and {why} (a session that runs without this machine\'s hooks)'
    if kind == 'codex':
        sub = codex_sub(args)
        if sub not in CODEX_SAFE:
            return (f'`{shown} {sub}`' if sub else f'`{shown}`') + \
                ' starts an agent session that runs no Claude Code hooks'
    return None


def sourced_files(toks):
    """The files a command reads as shell text into the shell that runs what follows: `. f`, `source f`, and a
    shell's --rcfile/--init-file."""
    out = []
    for i, t in enumerate(toks):
        if t in ('.', 'source') and i + 1 < len(toks):
            out.append(toks[i + 1])
        elif t in ('--rcfile', '--init-file') and i + 1 < len(toks) and i and os.path.basename(toks[0]) in SHELLS:
            out.append(toks[i + 1])
    return out


def env_split(toks):
    """`env -S 'A=1 cmd x' y` / --split-string: the shell-like text env splits and runs, or None."""
    h, _ = head(toks)
    for i, t in enumerate(toks[:h if h is not None else len(toks)]):
        if os.path.basename(t) != 'env':
            continue
        for j in range(i + 1, len(toks)):
            a = toks[j]
            if a in ('-S', '--split-string') and j + 1 < len(toks):
                return ' '.join([toks[j + 1]] + [shlex.quote(x) for x in toks[j + 2:]])
            if a.startswith('--split-string=') or (a.startswith('-S') and len(a) > 2):
                v = a.partition('=')[2] if a.startswith('--') else a[2:]
                return ' '.join([v] + [shlex.quote(x) for x in toks[j + 1:]])
            if not a.startswith('-') and not is_assign(a):
                break
    return None


def claude_starts(args):
    """Whether claude with these words starts a session (not `claude --version`, `claude mcp list` ..)."""
    return not any(a in CLAUDE_TOOLS for a in args[:1]) and not any(a in ('--version', '-v', '--help', '-h')
                                                                    for a in args)


def codex_sub(args):
    """codex's subcommand: the first word that is no option and no option's value (`codex -m o3 exec x`: exec),
    or a safe option (--version); None for the interactive session."""
    i = 0
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        if a in CODEX_SAFE and a.startswith('-'):
            return a
        if a == '--':
            return args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else None
        if a in CODEX_VALUE:
            i += 2
            continue
        if not a.startswith('-'):
            return a
        i += 1
    return None


def segment_reason(toks, ctx, loose):
    h, via = head(toks)
    why = nested_agent(toks, h, ctx)
    if why:
        return reason(why, 'Run the work in this session (or a nested `claude -p` without --bare), or ask the '
                           'owner.')
    split = env_split(toks)
    if split:   # env -S 'A=1 cmd x': read the text env runs as a command
        r = decide_text(split, ctx)
        if r:
            return r
    flow = ctx['flow']
    for j in flag_values(toks, h):   # the other reading: the flag takes no value and the word starts the command
        base = os.path.basename(toks[j])
        if base in SHELLS or base in NET_CLIENTS or base in TEXT_ARGS or base in AWKS or INTERPRETERS.match(base) \
                or is_gh(toks[j], ctx) or (not hidden(toks[j]) and cmd_files(toks[j], ctx, True)) \
                or base in PKG_RUNNERS or agent_kind(toks[j], ctx):
            r = segment_reason(toks[:j - 1] + toks[j:], ctx, loose)
            if r:
                return r
    extra = [WILD] if 'xargs' in via else []   # xargs adds words nobody can read here
    code = h is not None and INTERPRETERS.match(os.path.basename(toks[h]))   # xargs -0 -a f python3 -c: f is code
    for name in xargs_files(toks) if 'xargs' in via else ():   # xargs -a file: the words come from that file
        tok = named_file(name, ctx) or (code and data_token(name, ctx, runs=True))
        if tok:
            return unproven(tok, 'in the file that `xargs` takes the words of its command from (-a/--arg-file)')
    for i, t in enumerate(toks):   # gh anywhere in the command: behind find -exec, ssh, a wrapper not listed here
        if is_gh(t, ctx):
            r = gh_reason(toks[i + 1:] + extra, dict(ctx, cfg_hidden=ctx.get('cfg_hidden') or any(
                hidden(a) and re.match(r'(GH_CONFIG_DIR|XDG_CONFIG_HOME|HOME)=', a) for a in toks[:i])), sure=i == h)
            if r:
                return r
    word = os.path.basename(toks[h]) if h is not None else ''
    # plain: the word is the program of that name for certain (no function, no alias, no file in another folder)
    plain = h is not None and not hidden(toks[h]) and toks[h] not in ctx.get('funcs', ()) \
        and toks[h] not in ctx.get('aliases', {}) and ('/' not in toks[h] or os.path.dirname(toks[h]) in STD_BINS) \
        and not (word in DATA and '/' not in toks[h] and path_script(toks[h], ctx))
    kind = word if plain else ''
    gh_word = h is not None and is_gh(toks[h], ctx)
    shellish = bool(via & {'shell', 'eval'})
    interp = INTERPRETERS.match(kind) is not None or kind in AWKS
    # gh-as <app> -- gh ..: gh with an app token (a wrapper that may run this guard on the gh words too)
    rest = toks[h + 1:] if kind == 'gh-as' else []
    cut = rest.index('--') if '--' in rest else -1
    gh_as = rest[cut + 2:] if 0 <= cut <= 2 and rest[cut + 1:cut + 2] == ['gh'] else None
    lost = h is not None and not (shellish or gh_word or interp or kind in DATA or kind in NET_CLIENTS
                                  or kind in TEXT_ARGS or kind == 'git' or gh_as is not None)
    texts = []   # words that are shell text themselves
    if 'eval' in via and h is not None:
        texts.append(' '.join(toks[h:]))
    if (shellish and not interp) or loose or kind in TEXT_ARGS or lost:
        texts += [t for t in toks if SHELL_TEXT.search(t)]   # also behind a word the guard does not know (sg -c ..)
    elif h is not None and re.search(r'\s', toks[h]):   # env -S ".." and other wrappers that take one string
        texts.append(toks[h])
    for t in toks[:h] if h is not None else toks:   # --split-string=".." and the like
        if t.startswith('-') and re.search(r'\s', t):
            texts += [t.partition('=')[2], t[2:]]
    how, proven = ctx.get('writes'), False
    # echo .. | tee x.sh; sh x.sh: a pipe into a word that writes a file the text runs carries the script too
    how = how or (ctx.get('feeds_script') if kind in READERS | {'echo', 'printf'} else '')
    if how and h is not None:   # echo/printf/tee .. > x.sh; sh x.sh: the words are the script
        said = toks[h + 1:]
        filed, made = [], []
        perlish = kind.startswith(('perl', 'ruby'))
        # cat p.txt > x.sh, cp p.txt x.sh, sort q.txt > r.sh, perl -pe '' q.txt > r.sh: the files (and the
        # input) are the script
        if kind in TEXT_FILTERS | COPIERS | FILE_WRITERS and kind != 'tee' or perlish:
            srcs = file_writes(toks, h, ctx)[1] if kind in FILE_WRITERS else \
                code_args(said, kind.startswith('perl'), script=True)[1] if perlish else said
            filed += [ctx['stdin']] if ctx.get('stdin') else []
            for a in (a for a in srcs if not a.startswith('-') and not hidden(a)):
                text = read_named(a, ctx)
                tok = text is None and named_file(a, ctx)
                if tok:
                    return unproven(tok, f'in the file {redact(a)}, which the same command writes under a name '
                                         'it runs, and which the guard cannot read in full')
                filed += [] if text is None else [text]
        else:
            made = [' '.join(said), ' '.join(said[1:])] + [t for t in said if re.search(r'\s', t)]
        made += [t.replace('\\n', '\n') for t in made if '\\n' in t]
        if how == 'code' or (how == 'exec' and any(shebang(t.lstrip()) not in SHELLS | {''} for t in made + filed)):
            tok = named(' '.join(toks)) or named(ctx.get('stdin') or '') or next(filter(None, map(named, filed)), None)
            if tok:
                return reason(f'a script that the same command writes and runs names {tok}; the guard reads shell '
                              'scripts only, not code', 'Run the calls as gh commands the guard can read.')
        else:
            texts += made + filed
            proven = True
    ctx = dict(ctx, writes='', feeds_script='', proven=proven)
    texts += [t.replace('\\n', '\n') for t in texts if '\\n' in t]   # printf 'a\ngh ..' | sh
    inner = dict(ctx, nogate=True) if lost and not loose else ctx   # the gate below speaks for the whole command
    for text in dict.fromkeys(texts):
        r = text and decide_text(text, inner)
        if r and lost and not loose and text in toks[h + 1:]:   # -m/--what/-p text of a tool the guard does not know
            what = r[len(PREFIX):] if r.startswith(PREFIX) else r
            return PREFIX + (f'an argument of `{redact(word)}`, a tool the guard does not know, is text that reads as '
                             'shell, and read as shell it is refused. If it is only text (a message, a title, a '
                             'prompt), put it in a file and pass the file (--body-file, -F), or reword it; to run it, '
                             f'run it as a command of its own. As shell: {what}')
        if r:
            return r
    own, fed = named(' '.join(toks)), named(ctx.get('stdin') or '')
    if h is None:
        if own and exported(toks, flow):
            flow['env'] = own
        if ('shell' in via or bare_shell(toks)) and ctx.get('stdin') is None:
            flow['unknown'] = 'a shell that reads a pipe or a file the guard cannot read'
        if xargs_runs_words(toks, via):   # cat f | xargs -L1 env, xargs -a f sh -c: the words read are run
            flow['unknown'] = 'xargs that runs the words it reads'
            for name in xargs_files(toks):
                tok = data_token(name, ctx, runs=True)
                if tok:
                    return unproven(tok, 'in the file whose words `xargs` runs as commands (-a/--arg-file)')
        return None
    if (ctx.get('cmd') or {}).get('pat') and not own:   # a `case` pattern, not a command
        return None
    args = toks[h + 1:] + extra
    if not gh_word and (hidden(toks[h]) or toks[h] in ctx.get('funcs', ())):   # "$GH" .., g() { gh "$@"; }; g ..
        r = gh_reason([WILD] + args, ctx, sure=False) or net_reason('', args, ctx)
        if r:
            return r
    expansions = ctx.get('aliases', {}).get(toks[h], ())
    for exp in expansions:   # alias g='gh workflow'; g ..: read as the text it stands for
        r = decide_text(exp + ' ' + ' '.join(quote(a) for a in args), ctx)
        if r:
            return r
    if kind in DATA and not shellish:
        if 'xargs' in via and (own or kind in ('sed', 'find')):
            flow['unknown'] = 'xargs'
        return data_gate(kind, args, ctx, own, fed, loose)
    if ctx.get('feeds_code'):   # awk 1 d.txt | bash, perl -pe '' q.txt | sh, tool x < f | sh: any filter, one rule
        tok = fed or next(filter(None, (data_token(a, ctx, runs=True) for a in args
                                        if not a.startswith('-') and not hidden(a))), None) \
            or refuse_script_named(ctx.get('stdin') or '')
        if tok:
            return unproven(tok, f'in the input of `{redact(word or toks[h])}`, whose output goes on into a shell or '
                                 'a program that runs it')
    if kind == 'rsync':   # rsync -a ./ ~/.local/bin/: the files become command words, as with cp -r
        r = into_path_reason(args, ctx)
        if r:
            return r
    flow['ran'] = True
    if 'xargs' in via:
        flow['unknown'] = 'xargs'
    pre = named(' '.join(toks[:h]))
    if pre and not ctx.get('nogate'):
        return unproven(pre, 'in a variable or an option set in front of the command word, which the program may '
                             'take as a command (PAGER, GIT_SSH_COMMAND, a wrapper option ..)')
    if gh_word:
        if not plain:
            flow['unknown'] = 'gh under another name or path'
        return gh_gate(args, ctx, own)
    if gh_as is not None:
        return gh_reason(gh_as, ctx, sure=True) or gh_gate(gh_as, ctx, own)
    pos = positional(args, {'-m', '-W', '-X', '--eval', '-e', '-c', '-r', '--require'})
    script = toks[h] if not interp else (pos[0] if pos else '')
    for rs in REFUSE_SCRIPTS:   # the machine file's refuseScripts: a project tool that disables workflows itself
        if os.path.basename(script) == rs['name'] and rs['flag'] in args \
                and any(os.path.isfile(resolve_path(script, d)) for d in folders(ctx)):   # a real run, not prose
            return reason(f'`{rs["name"]} {rs["flag"]}` {rs.get("why") or "is refused by this machine's settings"}')
    if interp:
        files = interp_files(kind, args, pos, ctx)
        if any(is_self(p, ctx) for p, _ in files):
            return None
        if own or fed:
            return unproven(own or fed, f'in inline code, arguments or input of `{word}`: the guard reads shell '
                                        'text and gh/curl calls, not code in other languages', CODE_HINT)
        r = input_runs_reason(kind, args, ctx)
        if r:
            return r
        flow['unknown'] = word
        for p, d in dict.fromkeys(files):
            r = file_reason(p, dict(ctx, cwd=d))
            if r:
                return r
        return None
    if shellish:
        tok = named(' '.join(t for t in toks if not (SHELL_TEXT.search(t) and named(t))))
        if tok:   # sh -c 'curl -X PUT "$1"' _ <url>: the token is no shell text the guard has read
            return unproven(tok, 'in the arguments of a shell, `eval` or a script, outside the shell text itself')
        if 'eval' in via:
            if any(hidden(t) for t in toks[h:]):
                flow['unknown'] = 'eval'
            return None
        if any(re.fullmatch(r'-[A-Za-z]*c', t) for t in toks[:h]):   # sh -c word: the word is the whole text
            return None if SHELL_TEXT.search(toks[h]) else decide_text(toks[h], ctx)
        files = [] if hidden(toks[h]) else cmd_files(toks[h], ctx, True)
        for p, d in files:
            r = file_reason(p, dict(ctx, cwd=d), force_shell=True)
            if r:
                return r
        if not files and not SHELL_TEXT.search(toks[h]):
            flow['unknown'] = 'a script that is not there to read (written later, or named by a variable)'
        return None
    if kind == 'parallel':   # parallel -a f, parallel :::: f, .. | parallel: the lines are commands or arguments
        tok = next(filter(None, (data_token(f, ctx, runs=True) for f in parallel_files(args))), None) \
            or refuse_script_named(ctx.get('stdin') or '')
        if tok:
            return unproven(tok, 'in a file or input whose lines `parallel` runs as commands or their arguments '
                                 '(-a, ::::, stdin)')
    if kind in TEXT_ARGS:   # ssh, su, parallel, trap: their quoted arguments were read as shell text above
        tok = named(' '.join(t for t in toks if not (SHELL_TEXT.search(t) and named(t)))) or fed
        if tok:
            return unproven(tok, f'in the words or the input of `{word}`, outside one quoted shell text')
        if kind != 'trap':
            flow['unknown'] = word
        return None
    if kind in NET_CLIENTS:
        return net_reason(word, args, ctx) or net_gate(word, toks, h, ctx, own or fed)
    if kind == 'git':
        for a in args:   # git -c alias.x='!cmd' x, git config alias.x '!cmd': the text after ! is a shell command
            bang = a[1:] if a.startswith('!') else a.partition('=!')[2]
            r = bang and decide_text(bang, ctx)
            if r:
                return r
        return git_gate(args, ctx, own, fed)
    # Every other command word: a script file the guard can read, a function, or a word it does not know.
    if hidden(toks[h]):
        return launcher_gate(toks, h, ctx, own, fed, loose)
    if toks[h] in ctx.get('funcs', ()) or expansions:
        if own or fed:   # its body is in the text and was read; the words it gets may go anywhere
            flow['free'] = own or fed
            flow['free_to'] = f'hands to the shell function `{redact(toks[h])}`'
        return None
    files = cmd_files(toks[h], ctx, False)
    if any(is_self(p, ctx) for p, _ in files):
        return None
    bare = '/' not in toks[h] and which(toks[h], ctx)
    if any(gh_file(p, ctx) for p in [q for q, _ in files] + ([bare] if bare else [])):
        return gh_reason(args, ctx, sure=True) or gh_gate(args, ctx, own)   # gh, renamed or linked earlier
    read = False
    for p, d in files:
        f = facts(p, ctx)
        if not f['text']:
            r = unread(p)
            if r:
                return r
            continue
        read = True
        if (own or fed) and not ctx.get('nogate'):
            return unproven(own or fed, f'and hands it to the script `{redact(word)}`, whose use of its '
                                        'arguments and input the guard cannot follow')
        # run as a command, a text file without a `#!` line is run by the shell itself (execve fails, bash reads it)
        r = file_reason(p, dict(ctx, cwd=d), force_shell=f['bang'] is None)
        if r:
            return r
        if not (p.endswith(('.sh', '.bash', '.zsh')) or f['bang'] in SHELLS or f['bang'] is None):
            flow['unknown'] = redact(word)   # code in another language: only looked through for guarded tokens
    if read:
        return arg_scripts_reason(redact(word), toks[h + 1:], ctx)
    return launcher_gate(toks, h, ctx, own, fed, loose)


def parallel_files(args):
    """The files GNU parallel reads lines from: -a FILE, -aFILE, --arg-file FILE, --arg-file=FILE, and the words
    after :::: (up to the next ::: group)."""
    out, i, after = [], 0, False
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        i += 1
        if a in ('::::', '::::+'):
            after = True
        elif a in (':::', ':::+'):
            after = False
        elif after:
            out.append(a)
        elif a in ('-a', '--arg-file'):
            out += args[i:i + 1]
            i += 1
        elif a.startswith('--arg-file='):
            out.append(a.partition('=')[2])
        elif re.fullmatch(r'-a.+', a):
            out.append(a[2:])
    return [f for f in out if f and f != '-' and not hidden(f)]


LOGIN_RUNNERS = {'sudo', 'doas', 'runuser'}   # with no command word: a shell that reads its input (sudo -s, sudo -i)
NO_SHELL = {'-v', '-k', '-K', '-l', '-ll', '-V', '-h', '-e', '--validate', '--list', '--version', '--help', '--edit',
            '--reset-timestamp', '--remove-timestamp'}   # ... unless a flag says it starts none


def bare_shell(toks):
    """`sudo -s`, `sudo -i`, `doas -s`, `runuser -u x`, a bare `sudo`: no command word, so they start a shell that runs
    what a pipe, heredoc or < file feeds it. Also `su`, `su - x`, `sudo su` with no -c, and `at`/`batch` with no
    -f file, and `make -f -`: each runs the text it reads as commands. A part with no words (`( bash ) < f` has
    one, `< f` alone) starts nothing."""
    if not toks:
        return False
    h, via = head(toks)
    if h is not None and not via & {'shell', 'eval', 'xargs'} and not hidden(toks[h]):
        word, args = os.path.basename(toks[h]), toks[h + 1:]
        if word == 'su':
            return not any(re.fullmatch(r'-[a-zA-Z]*c.*|--(?:co|ses|session-c).*', a) or a in NO_SHELL for a in args)
        if word in ('at', 'batch'):
            return not any(re.fullmatch(r'-[a-zA-Z]*[flcdrV].*|--.*', a) for a in args)
        if word in ('make', 'gmake'):
            return any(re.fullmatch(r'-[a-zA-Z]*f-?|--(?:file|makefile)=-', a) and
                       (a.endswith('-') or args[i + 1:i + 2] == ['-']) for i, a in enumerate(args))
    return h is None and not via & {'shell', 'eval', 'xargs'} and not any(t in NO_SHELL for t in toks) \
        and any(os.path.basename(t) in LOGIN_RUNNERS for t in toks)


def xargs_runs_words(toks, via):
    """xargs with no command word of its own but a runner (`xargs -L1 env`, `xargs sudo`, `xargs sh -c`): the words
    it reads are the command it runs. A bare `xargs` runs echo."""
    if 'xargs' not in via:
        return False
    at = next(i for i, t in enumerate(toks) if os.path.basename(t) == 'xargs')
    return any(os.path.basename(t) in WRAPPERS - {'xargs'} for t in toks[at + 1:])


XARGS_VALUE = set('adEILnPs')   # xargs short flags that take a value (also glued: -aFILE, -0aFILE)


def xargs_files(toks):
    """The files xargs reads its words from: -a FILE, -aFILE, -0aFILE, --arg-file FILE, --arg-file=FILE and any
    --a.. prefix of it (GNU getopt takes one), up to the command word xargs runs."""
    out, i = [], next(i for i, t in enumerate(toks) if os.path.basename(t) == 'xargs') + 1
    while i < len(toks):
        t = toks[i]
        i += 1
        if t == '--' or not t.startswith('-'):
            break
        if t.startswith('--'):
            name, eq, value = t.partition('=')
            if len(name) >= 3 and '--arg-file'.startswith(name):
                out.append(value if eq else (toks[i] if i < len(toks) else ''))
                i += 0 if eq else 1
            elif not eq and name in ARG_FLAGS:
                i += 1
            continue
        for j, ch in enumerate(t[1:], 1):
            if ch in XARGS_VALUE:
                value = t[j + 1:]
                if not value:
                    value, i = (toks[i] if i < len(toks) else ''), i + 1
                if ch == 'a':
                    out.append(value)
                break
            if ch in 'eil':   # an optional value, glued only
                break
    return [f for f in out if f and f != '-' and not hidden(f)]


def reads_stdin_as_shell(toks):
    """bash, sh -s, eval, sudo -s ..: a shell that runs what a pipe or heredoc feeds it."""
    h, via = head(toks)
    if 'eval' in toks or bare_shell(toks):
        return True
    return 'shell' in via and (h is None or std_stream(toks[h]))


def written(toks, cmd, env, ran, ctx=None):
    """How a file this command writes (> file, tee file) is run by a command of the same text: 'shell' (through a
    shell or `source`), 'exec' (as a command: its `#!` line picks the reader), 'code' (as an interpreter's script)
    or '' (not run, as far as the text shows). Files are matched by name, not by folder."""
    h, _ = head(toks)
    targets = [t for op, w in cmd['redirs'] if op.startswith(('>', '&>')) for r in readings({'words': [w]}, env)
               for t in [''.join(r)]]
    targets += file_writes(toks, h, ctx)[0]
    names = {os.path.basename(t) for t in targets if os.path.basename(t)}
    # tee ~/.local/bin/* (also ??, *?, $n): a name that is only hidden text can be any file of its folder, so any
    # name the text runs, when the folder is named or the name is unquoted (a glob, $f); a quoted "$f" alone still
    # matches only hidden names
    # a target that ends in / is a folder: cp/mv put the sources' own names there (in names already), > fails
    anyname = any(not visible(os.path.basename(t)) and (visible(os.path.dirname(t)) or WILD in t)
                  for t in targets if not t.endswith('/'))
    return next((k for k in ('shell', 'exec', 'code') if ran[k] and anyname
                 or any(same_name(a, b) for a in names for b in ran[k])), '')


FILE_WRITERS = {'install', 'dd', 'rsync'} | AWKS   # words that write the text of files they name into other files


def script_texts(files, ctx):
    """The texts of script files (sed -f, awk -f) and whether one of them could not be read (stdin, a hidden
    name, a file not there yet): a script the guard cannot read may write any file."""
    texts, unread = [], False
    for f in files:   # a file the same command writes first is not read: its text then is not what runs
        text = None if std_stream(f) or ctx is None or written_here(f, ctx) else read_named(f, ctx)
        texts += [] if text is None else [text]
        unread = unread or text is None
    return texts, unread


# gawk's long flags (GNU getopt takes any unique prefix: --fi is --file, --ex is --exec)
AWK_LONG = ('--assign', '--file', '--source', '--field-separator', '--exec', '--include', '--load', '--bignum',
            '--characters-as-bytes', '--traditional', '--copyright', '--debug', '--dump-variables', '--gen-pot',
            '--help', '--lint', '--lint-old', '--use-lc-numeric', '--non-decimal-data', '--optimize', '--no-optimize',
            '--profile', '--pretty-print', '--posix', '--re-interval', '--sandbox', '--version', '--csv')
AWK_VALUE = {'--assign', '--file', '--source', '--field-separator', '--exec', '--include', '--load'}


def awk_args(args, ctx=None):
    """awk's arguments as gawk reads them: (program texts, variables, input files, whether a program file could not
    be read). A program is the value of -e/--source and the text of -f/--file, -E/--exec and -i/--include files
    (also glued: -vf=x, -fprog, a cluster -bf prog, a long flag cut short); without either the first word that is no
    flag. -l/--load (a compiled extension) and a long flag that is no unique gawk flag count as a program the guard
    cannot read. Variables come from -v/--assign and from `name=value` operands, which awk takes as assignments, not
    as files."""
    progs, files, vars_, inputs, i, ended, given, lost = [], [], {}, [], 0, False, False, False
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        i += 1
        if ended or a == '-' or not a.startswith('-'):
            name, eq, value = a.partition('=')
            if eq and re.fullmatch(r'[A-Za-z_]\w*', name) and (given or progs):
                vars_[name] = value
            elif not given and not progs:
                progs.append(a)
            else:
                inputs.append(a)
            continue
        if a == '--':
            ended = True
            continue
        if a.startswith('--'):
            name, eq, value = a.partition('=')
            full = [f for f in AWK_LONG if f.startswith(name)]
            flag = name if name in AWK_LONG else full[0] if len(full) == 1 else None
            if flag is None:
                lost = True
                continue
            if flag in AWK_VALUE and not eq:
                value, i = (args[i] if i < len(args) else ''), i + 1
            pairs = [(flag, value)] if flag in AWK_VALUE else []
        else:
            pairs = []
            for j, ch in enumerate(a[1:], 1):   # a cluster: -bf prog, -vx=1
                if ch in 'vfeEiFl':
                    value = a[j + 1:]
                    if not value:
                        value, i = (args[i] if i < len(args) else ''), i + 1
                    pairs.append(('-' + ch, value))
                    break
                if ch in 'dDLop':   # an optional value, glued only
                    break
        for flag, value in pairs:
            if flag in ('-v', '--assign'):
                name, _, val = value.partition('=')
                vars_[name] = val
            elif flag in ('-f', '--file', '-E', '--exec', '-i', '--include'):
                files.append(value)
                given = True
            elif flag in ('-e', '--source'):
                progs.append(value)
                given = True
            elif flag in ('-l', '--load'):
                lost = True
        if a.startswith(('-E', '--ex')) and pairs:   # -E file ends the flags
            ended = True
    texts, unread = script_texts(files, ctx)
    return progs + texts, vars_, inputs, unread or lost


def awk_joined(text, vars_):
    """The file name an awk target built by joining spells: > "dir/" "ls" is dir/ls, > d "ls" with -v d=x is xls,
    > "out_" $1 ".txt" is out_<hidden>.txt (a hidden part can be any text, also a folder). Anything else in the
    expression (an operator, a call, a bracket): WILD, any file."""
    name, pos = '', 0
    piece = re.compile(r'[ \t]*(?:(%s)|(\$(?:\w+|\([^()]*\)))|([A-Za-z_]\w*)\b(?![ \t]*[(\[]))' % AWK_STRING)
    while True:
        m = piece.match(text, pos)
        if not m or m.end() == pos:
            break
        pos = m.end()
        lit, field, var = m.groups()
        name += lit[1:-1] if lit else HID if field else vars_.get(var, HID)
    rest = text[pos:].lstrip(' \t')
    return name if name and rest[:1] in ('', ';', '}', '\n', ')', '|', '#') else WILD


def awk_writes(progs, vars_):
    """The files an awk program writes: print/printf > or >> a "literal", a variable (-v f=x) or WILD (an
    expression the guard does not follow), and the files a "command" it pipes into writes (print | "tee x",
    print | "cat > x")."""
    out = []
    for prog in progs:
        for m in re.finditer(r'\b(?:print|printf)\b(?:%s|[^;}>|"\n])*(?<!>)>>?\s*(%s|[A-Za-z_]\w*\b(?!\s*\()|\S?)'
                             % (AWK_STRING, AWK_STRING), prog):
            t, after = m.group(1), prog[m.end():].lstrip(' \t')
            if after[:1] not in ('', ';', '}', '\n', ')', '|', '#'):   # > "dir/" "ls", > d "ls": built text
                out.append(awk_joined(t + ' ' + after, vars_))
            else:
                out.append(WILD if not t else t[1:-1] if t.startswith('"') else vars_.get(t, WILD))
        for m in re.finditer(r'\|&?\s*(%s)' % AWK_STRING, prog):
            for toks in (seg.split() for seg in re.split(r'[;&|]+', m.group(1)[1:-1])):
                h, _ = head(toks) if toks else (None, set())
                out += re.findall(r'>>?\s*([^\s;&|<>]+)', ' '.join(toks)) + file_writes(toks, h)[0]
    return out


def file_writes(toks, h, ctx=None):
    """(files the command word writes, files whose text it writes there) as the text shows them: tee FILE, cp/mv/ln,
    install and rsync (cp p.txt ~/.local/bin/ls, cp ls ~/.local/bin/: every word, the sources' names land there
    too), dd of=/if=, sed w FILE and s///w FILE (also in -f files), awk print > "FILE" or > VAR, print | "tee FILE"
    (also in -f files). A script file the guard cannot read (ctx None: not read) writes WILD, any name. Other
    writers (a program's -o FILE) are not read."""
    if h is None:
        return [], []
    word, args = os.path.basename(toks[h]), toks[h + 1:]
    if word == 'tee':
        return [t for t in args if not t.startswith('-')], []
    if word in COPIERS or word in ('install', 'rsync'):
        words = [t.partition('=')[2] or t for t in args if not t.startswith('-') or t.startswith('--target-directory=')]
        return words, [t for t in args if not t.startswith('-')]
    if word == 'dd':
        return [a[3:] for a in args if a.startswith('of=')], [a[3:] for a in args if a.startswith('if=')]
    if word == 'sort':   # sort -o FILE, -oFILE, --output=FILE (any --ou.. prefix)
        outs = []
        for i, a in enumerate(args):
            name, eq, value = a.partition('=')
            long = len(name) >= 4 and '--output'.startswith(name)
            if a == '-o' or (long and not eq):
                outs += args[i + 1:i + 2]
            elif long or re.fullmatch(r'-[a-zA-Z]*o.+', a):
                outs.append(value if long else a[a.index('o') + 1:])
        return outs, [a for a in args if not a.startswith('-') and a not in outs]
    if word == 'sed':
        scripts, files, inputs, sandbox = sed_args(args)
        texts, unread = script_texts(files, ctx)
        return ([] if sandbox else [w for t in scripts + texts for w in sed_program(t)[2]] + [WILD] * unread), inputs
    if word in AWKS:
        progs, vars_, inputs, unread = awk_args(args, ctx)
        return awk_writes(progs, vars_) + [WILD] * unread, inputs
    return [], []


def visible(text):
    """The text of a word that is not hidden (no $X, glob or {a,b} part), without the / between folders."""
    return text.replace(HID, '').replace(WILD, '').strip('/')


def same_name(a, b):
    """Whether two file names can be the same file: equal (two hidden names count as equal: f=$(mktemp); cat > "$f"
    ..; bash "$f"), or one has hidden parts and its visible text fits the other."""
    if a == b:
        return True
    if hidden(a) and hidden(b):
        return True
    for x, y in ((a, b), (b, a)):
        if hidden(x) and x.replace(HID, '').replace(WILD, '') and spells(x, y):
            return True
    return False


def doc_kind(toks, how, loose, body):
    """A heredoc (or <<< word, < file) is shell (fed to a shell), code (fed to an interpreter), script (written to
    a file that a command in this text runs as shell) or data. how: written() of the command."""
    h, via = head(toks)
    word = os.path.basename(toks[h]) if h is not None else ''
    if 'shell' in via or 'eval' in via or bare_shell(toks) or xargs_runs_words(toks, via):
        return 'shell'   # xargs -L1 env < f: the lines are commands
    if INTERPRETERS.match(word):
        return 'code'
    if how == 'exec':
        how = 'shell' if shebang(body) in SHELLS | {''} else 'code'
    if how:
        return 'script' if how == 'shell' else 'code'
    return 'shell' if loose else 'data'


def file_sub(cmd, env):
    """`$(< f)` is `$(cat f)` to the shell: the readings of a command substitution with no words and only `< f`,
    as cat's words, so the file's text goes on (a pipe, a word, a shell that runs it) as cat's would. Also with
    output redirects beside it (`$(< f 2>/dev/null)`, `2>&1`; the parser drops the fd number): bash 5.2 gives no
    text there, another shell or version may give the file's, so the guard reads the stricter way."""
    bare = not any(cmd['words']) or [w for w in cmd['words'] if w] == [[('L', 'cat', False)]]   # also $(cat < f)
    if not cmd.get('sub') or not bare or cmd['docs'] or not any(op == '<' for op, _ in cmd['redirs']) \
            or any(op != '<' and not op.startswith(('>', '&>')) for op, _ in cmd['redirs']):
        return None
    return [['cat'] + [t for op, w in cmd['redirs'] if op == '<' for t in redir_texts(op, w, env)[:1]]]


def stdin_of(cmd, env, ctx):
    """The text a command gets on stdin when the command text shows it (heredoc, <<< word, < file that is there to
    read), else None (a pipe, the terminal, a file written later)."""
    parts = list(cmd['docs'])
    for op, w in cmd['redirs']:
        if op not in ('<<<', '<'):
            continue
        words = redir_texts(op, w, env)
        word = words[0] if len(words) == 1 else HID   # a variable with several values: not known
        text = word if op == '<<<' else read_named(word, ctx)
        if text is None:
            return None
        parts.append(text)
    return '\n'.join(parts) if parts or any(op == '<<<' for op, _ in cmd['redirs']) else None


def redir_texts(op, w, env):
    """Every text the word of a redirection can be (`<<< word` is not split into words)."""
    return list(dict.fromkeys((' ' if op == '<<<' else '').join(r) for r in readings({'words': [w]}, env)))


def fed_texts(cmd, env, ctx):
    """The texts a command reads from `<<< word` and `< file`, in every reading; what cannot be read is left out."""
    out = []
    for op, w in cmd['redirs']:
        if op in ('<<<', '<'):
            for word in redir_texts(op, w, env):
                text = word if op == '<<<' else read_named(word, ctx)
                if text and text not in out:
                    out.append(text)
    return out


def runs_hidden(toks, cmd):
    """Whether a command runs a word the text does not show as shell or code: eval/bash -c/source/python3 with a
    hidden word, or a shell or interpreter fed by a hidden <<< word or < file (bash <<< "$(..)", sh < <(..))."""
    if not toks:
        return False
    h, via = head(toks)
    word = os.path.basename(toks[h]) if h is not None else ''
    if not (via & {'shell', 'eval'} or word in ('source', '.', 'eval') or INTERPRETERS.match(word)):
        return False
    return any(hidden(t) for t in toks) or \
        any(op in ('<', '<<<') and any(p[0] != 'L' for p in w) for op, w in cmd['redirs'])


def pure_word(variants):
    """Whether in every reading the command word is a data word (tee, grep, sort ..) that passes text on."""
    return bool(variants) and all(toks and head(toks)[0] is not None and not head(toks)[1]
                                  and os.path.basename(toks[head(toks)[0]]) in DATA for toks in variants)


def inert(variants, ctx):
    """Whether a command only handles text and files in every reading, and runs nothing: a data word under its own
    name (pure_word: no wrapper, function, alias or PATH script of that name) with no find -exec, sed e or -f,
    rg --pre or sort --compress-program."""
    if not pure_word(variants):
        return False
    for toks in variants:
        h = head(toks)[0]
        word, args = os.path.basename(toks[h]), toks[h + 1:]
        if toks[h] in ctx.get('funcs', ()) or toks[h] in ctx.get('aliases', {}) or path_script(toks[h], ctx) \
                or run_programs(word, args):
            return False
        if word == 'find' and any(a.startswith(('-exec', '-ok', '-fprint', '-fls')) for a in args):
            return False
        if word == 'sed' and (sed_runs_input(args) or any(sed_program(t)[0] for t in sed_args(args)[0])):
            return False
    return True


CREATORS = {'touch', 'mkdir', 'truncate', 'mkfifo', 'mknod', 'fallocate'}   # words that create the files they name


def text_writes(runs, env, ctx):
    """(the files the commands of a text may write or replace, whether one of them may write any file). Named:
    > file, tee, cp/mv/ln, dd of=, sed w and -i, sort -o, awk print > (file_writes), touch/mkdir. Any file: every
    word that is no data word (a program, an unpacker, install, rsync, perl -i, a script), a hidden command word,
    xargs, eval, find -exec, sed e, awk system(). Third: the readings (word lists) that may write any file."""
    out, by = [], []
    for cmd, variants in runs:
        out += [t for op, w in cmd['redirs'] if op.startswith(('>', '&>', '<>')) for t in redir_texts(op, w, env)
                if not NO_FILE.fullmatch(t)]
        for toks in (t for t in variants if t):
            h, via = head(toks)
            if h is None:
                by += [toks] if via or bare_shell(toks) else []
                continue
            word, args = os.path.basename(toks[h]), toks[h + 1:]
            out += file_writes(toks, h, ctx)[0]
            if word in CREATORS:   # touch json.py, mkdir json: a new file or folder there (python3 -m json.tool)
                out += [a for a in args if not a.startswith('-')]
            if word == 'sed' and any(re.match(r'-[^-efl]*i', a) or a.startswith('--in') for a in args):
                out += sed_args(args)[2]
            if hidden(toks[h]) or via & {'xargs', 'eval'}:
                may = True
            elif word == 'sed':   # e CMD, a bare e or s///e, also in its -f files (a file it cannot read: may)
                scripts, files = sed_args(args)[:2]
                texts, unread = script_texts(files, ctx)
                may = unread or any(sed_program(t)[0] or sed_program(t)[1] for t in scripts + texts)
            elif word in AWKS:
                progs = awk_args(args, ctx)[0]
                may = any(AWK_RUNS.search(p) or awk_pipe_runs(p) for p in progs)
            else:
                may = word not in DATA or not inert([toks], ctx)
            by += [toks] if may else []
    return out, bool(by), by


COMPRESSORS = {'gzip', 'bzip2', 'xz', 'zstd', 'lz4', 'lzma', 'brotli'}
# the only compressor words ends_text() takes: levels and output switches (no -d, no --decomp.. prefix, no files)
PACK_FLAGS = re.compile(r'-[0-9cfknqv]+|--(?:best|fast|stdout|to-stdout|keep|force|quiet|verbose|no-name)')
PAGERS = {'less', 'more'}
SSH_VALUE = set('BbcDEeFIiJLlmOoPpQRSWw')   # ssh flags that take a value
PY_SWITCHES = re.compile(r'-[BbdEIOPqsSuvxR]+')   # python flags that take no value and do not read stdin as code
PY_MODULES = {'json.tool'}   # the only `python3 -m` modules that read piped text as data
NODE_SWITCHES = {'--no-warnings', '--no-deprecation'}
# a written name a data reader may load as code: a module or package file, or a name with no dot (a folder: a
# package, node_modules); a module name has no dot
CODE_FILE = re.compile(r'\.(?:py[cdow]?|so|pth|zip|egg|whl|[cm]?js|node)$', re.I)
# the modules Python may load from the script's folder (or the cwd for -m) before its own library: Python's list
# of its standard modules; for a Python before 3.10, the ones `python3 -m json.tool` and a small script load
STDLIB_NAMES = frozenset(getattr(sys, 'stdlib_module_names', ())) or frozenset(
    'abc argparse ast codecs collections contextlib copy copyreg encodings enum functools gettext io json keyword '
    'linecache locale operator os re reprlib shutil signal sre_compile sre_constants sre_parse stat string struct '
    'subprocess sys tempfile textwrap token tokenize types typing warnings weakref'.split())
STREAM_RE = re.compile(r'/(?:dev|proc)(?:/|$)')


def std_stream(word):
    """'-' or a path into /dev or /proc in any spelling (//dev/stdin, /dev/./stdin, /dev/fd//0, /dev/fd/../fd/0,
    /proc/thread-self/fd/0, ../../dev/stdin): a stream such as stdin, never a script file the guard can read."""
    if word == '-':
        return True
    if word.startswith('/'):
        return bool(STREAM_RE.match(os.path.normpath('/' + word.lstrip('/'))))
    return '..' in word.split('/') and bool(re.search(r'(?:^|/)(?:dev|proc)(?:/|$)', word))


def written_here(name, ctx, precise=False):
    """Whether a command of the same text (or of a text around it) may write or replace the file `name` (ctx
    'wrote': > file, tee, cp/mv/ln/install/rsync, dd of=, sed -i, perl -i, sort -o, sed w, awk print >; 'wrote_any':
    a word that may write any file, such as a program or an unpacker). Then what the guard reads there now is not
    what runs. Matched by name: any folder of the path (cp -r new/ tools replaces tools/check.py) and any hidden
    name. precise: only the named targets, not 'wrote_any'."""
    if not ctx or not name:
        return False
    if ctx.get('wrote_any') and not precise:
        return True
    parts = [p for p in name.split('/') if p and p != '.']
    for t in ctx.get('wrote', ()):
        b = os.path.basename(t.rstrip('/'))
        if not visible(b) or any(same_name(p, b) for p in parts):
            return True
    return False


def data_file(word, ctx):
    """A script file that reads piped text only as its data may be trusted only when it is a real regular file
    outside /dev and /proc (resolved: //dev/stdin, symlinks) in every folder the command may run in, and the same
    text does not write it first."""
    if not word or hidden(word) or std_stream(word) or written_here(word, ctx, precise=True):
        return False
    for d in folders(ctx):
        p = resolve_path(word, d)
        if not os.path.isfile(p) or STREAM_RE.match(os.path.realpath(p)) or STREAM_RE.match(p):
            return False
    return True


def plants_code(ctx, variants=()):
    """Whether the text may put code where an interpreter loads its modules from (cp evil.py argparse.py, mkdir re,
    cp -r x json, touch json.py): a written file named like code (.py, .pyc, .so, .js ..), a name with no dot (a
    folder), a hidden name, or another command (not one of `variants`) that may write any file."""
    for t in ctx.get('wrote', ()):
        b = os.path.basename(t.rstrip('/'))
        if not visible(b) or '.' not in b or CODE_FILE.search(b):
            return True
    return any(not any(w is v for v in variants) for w in ctx.get('wrote_by', ()))


def shadows_stdlib(folder):
    """Whether a folder Python puts first on sys.path holds a module of its standard library's name: argparse.py,
    re.pyc, re.cpython-312-x86_64-linux-gnu.so, a json/ package (with __init__). A folder it cannot list: yes."""
    try:
        for e in os.listdir(folder):
            stem, p = e.partition('.')[0], os.path.join(folder, e)
            if stem not in STDLIB_NAMES:
                continue
            if os.path.isdir(p):
                if any(os.path.exists(os.path.join(p, '__init__' + x)) for x in ('.py', '.pyc')):
                    return True
            elif re.search(r'\.(?:py[cdow]?|so)$', e):
                return True
    except OSError:
        return True
    return False


def data_reader(word, args, ctx, variants=()):
    """python3 [switches] FILE, python3 -m json.tool, node FILE: an interpreter that reads piped text only as the
    data of a script file (data_file()). Every other flag, module or interpreter (deno, bun, perl ..) is no data
    reader: an allow-list, so a new way to read stdin as code fails closed. Also none when the text may plant a
    module it loads (plants_code), or, for python without -I/-P, when the folder Python searches first (the
    script's, the cwd for -m) holds a module named like one of its standard library (shadows_stdlib)."""
    py = word.startswith(('python', 'pypy'))
    if plants_code(ctx, variants):
        return False
    i, safe_path = 0, False
    while i < len(args):
        a = args[i]
        if py and a == '-m':
            mod = args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else ''
            top = mod.partition('.')[0]
            # a json.py or json/ in the folder (or written by the text) would load instead of the library
            return mod in PY_MODULES and not written_here(top, ctx, True) and not written_here(top + '.py', ctx, True) \
                and \
                not any(os.path.exists(resolve_path(n, d)) for d in folders(ctx) for n in (top, top + '.py')) \
                and (safe_path or not any(shadows_stdlib(d) for d in folders(ctx)))
        if py and PY_SWITCHES.fullmatch(a):
            safe_path = safe_path or bool(re.search('[IP]', a))
            i += 1
        elif py and a in ('-W', '-X'):
            i += 2
        elif py and re.fullmatch(r'-[WX].+', a):
            i += 1
        elif not py and a in NODE_SWITCHES:
            i += 1
        elif a.startswith('-'):
            return False
        else:
            return data_file(a, ctx) and (not py or safe_path or not any(
                shadows_stdlib(os.path.dirname(p)) or shadows_stdlib(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(p)))
                for d in folders(ctx) for p in [resolve_path(a, d)]))
    return False


def ends_text(variants, ctx=None):
    """Whether a command reads piped text only as data, whatever the text says (in every reading): a script file of
    python or node (python3 check.py, node x.js: data_reader(), an allow-list), `python3 -m json.tool`, `claude -p`
    (a prompt; not --bare, which skips the hooks), a compressor that does not unpack, a pager, `ssh host CMD` whose
    remote command is one such data word with no redirection. Used only for lines that call a refuseScripts entry
    (data_token): a guarded token still counts wherever its text goes."""
    for toks in variants:
        h, via = head(toks) if toks else (None, set())
        if h is None or h or via or hidden(toks[h]) or ('/' in toks[h] and os.path.dirname(toks[h]) not in STD_BINS):
            return False   # also no `VAR=x` in front (PYTHONPATH=., LESSOPEN=..)
        word, args = os.path.basename(toks[h]), toks[h + 1:]
        if word in COMPRESSORS:
            if not all(PACK_FLAGS.fullmatch(a) for a in args):
                return False
        elif word in PAGERS:
            pass
        elif word == 'claude':
            if not any(a in ('-p', '--print') for a in args) or any(a.startswith('--bare') for a in args):
                return False
        elif word == 'ssh':
            i, rest = 0, None
            while i < len(args):
                a = args[i]
                if a.startswith('-') and len(a) > 1:
                    i += 2 if a[-1] in SSH_VALUE and len(a) == 2 else 1
                    continue
                rest = args[i + 1:]
                break
            text = ' '.join(rest or [])
            if not text or re.search(r'[;&|<>`$()\n]', text) or hidden(text) \
                    or not pure_sink([segments(text)[0]] if segments(text) else []) or len(segments(text)) != 1:
                return False
        elif re.fullmatch(r'(?:python|pypy)[\d.]*|node|nodejs', word):
            # alias python3=bash, python3() { bash; }, a python3 script on PATH: no data reader under that name
            if ctx is None or toks[h] in ctx.get('funcs', ()) or toks[h] in ctx.get('aliases', {}) \
                    or path_script(toks[h], ctx) or not data_reader(word, args, ctx, variants):
                return False
        else:
            return False
    return bool(variants)


def pure_sink(variants):
    """Whether a command ends the text piped into it: in every reading its command word only handles text."""
    for toks in variants:
        h, via = head(toks) if toks else (None, set())
        if h is None or via or hidden(toks[h]) or os.path.basename(toks[h]) not in DATA - NO_SINK \
                or ('/' in toks[h] and os.path.dirname(toks[h]) not in STD_BINS) \
                or os.path.basename(toks[h]) == 'sed' and sed_runs_input(toks[h + 1:]):
            return False
    return bool(variants)


def sed_runs_input(args):
    """Whether a sed may run the text it reads as shell (a bare `e`, s///e, a script file the guard does not read
    here): then it is no sink for a pipe into it."""
    scripts, files, _, sandbox = sed_args(args)
    return not sandbox and (bool(files) or any(sed_program(s)[1] for s in scripts))


def stopped(text, what, flow, hint=''):
    """The guard stopped reading (a limit, a file it cannot read whole): refused when the text it did not read names
    a guarded token, else the command counts as running a word the guard cannot read (flow_reason)."""
    if LOOSE_RE.search(text) or named(text):
        return reason(what, hint)
    flow['unknown'] = what
    return None


# ---------- the skim: what the guard reads where it does not parse
# Two cases do not get the full reading: shell text past MAX_COMMANDS commands in one call (a 2 MB script took 4 s
# of the 5 s budget on an idle machine, and far more on a loaded one), and a command the guard ran out of time on.
# Neither may let a switch-off through, and neither may refuse a call that has nothing to do with one. So the guard
# reads what it needs for that: the text and every file it names (also behind NAME=value, in a folder the text names,
# a script on a PATH folder under $HOME, two files deep), for a guard word: a guarded token, gh, an HTTP client, the
# word workflow or disable, an agent session (claude, codex) or a refuseScripts line, quotes and backslashes removed
# too. A compiled program counts only as gh under another name. One guard word, a file it cannot read whole or open
# where something may run it, more than MAX_READINGS files or no time left: refused. A text without any: passes;
# what it does with its own words cannot switch anything off without one of them (a word built at run time is not
# covered by the full reading either).
MAX_COMMANDS = 10_000   # commands of shell text parsed in full per call (~25 us each on an idle machine)
SKIM_TIME = 2.0         # seconds for the skim after the guard ran out of time (it then still answers by ~7 s of 10)
SKIM_END = 8.0          # ... and never past this many seconds from the hook's start (its limit is 10 s)
SKIM_RE = re.compile(r'\bgh\b|curl|wget|httpie|\bxh\b|aria2c|api\.github|workflow|disable|\bclaude\b|\bcodex\b'
                     r'|CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE|graphql', re.I)
SKIM_PART = re.compile(r'[^\s;&|()<>`=:,]+')   # a word, or the part of one after `=` (NAME=f, --input=f) or `:`
SKIM_VAR = re.compile(r'\$\{?([A-Za-z_]\w*)\}?')
SKIM_SET = re.compile(r'(?:^|[\s;&|(`])(?:export\s+|local\s+|declare\s+)?([A-Za-z_]\w*)=([^\s;&|()<>`]*)')


class SkimRefused(Exception):
    """The skim's answer: a refusal, with the guard word and where it is."""


def skim_word(text):
    """The guard word a text holds, as a label, or None."""
    for t in (text, re.sub(r'[\'"\\]', '', text)):
        tok = named(t) or (REFUSE_SCRIPTS and refuse_script_named(t))
        if tok:
            return tok
        m = LOOSE_RE.search(t) or SKIM_RE.search(t)
        if m:
            return f'`{m.group(0)}`'
    return None


def skim_parts(text, env):
    """The words of a text that may name a file or a folder: quotes and backslashes removed, split at `=` and `:`,
    with each $NAME the text gives a plain value (NAME=value) put in, and $HOME. A part with another variable is left
    out: the full reading does not know such a file either (a hidden command word is one the guard cannot read)."""
    text = re.sub(r'[\'"\\]', '', text)
    values = {'HOME': [env.get('HOME') or os.path.expanduser('~')]}
    for m in SKIM_SET.finditer(text):
        if '$' not in m.group(2) and len(values.setdefault(m.group(1), [])) < 4:
            values[m.group(1)].append(m.group(2))
    out = {}
    for m in SKIM_PART.finditer(text):
        part = m.group(0).lstrip('@')   # gh -F x=@file, curl -d @file
        names = SKIM_VAR.findall(part)
        if not names:
            out[part] = None
        elif all(n in values for n in names) and SKIM_VAR.sub('', part).strip('/'):
            for v in values[names[0]]:   # $D/off.sh with D=. and D=sub: both
                out[SKIM_VAR.sub(lambda x: v if x.group(1) == names[0] else values[x.group(1)][0], part)] = None
    return [p for p in out if p and not p.startswith('-')]


def skim_files(text, dirs, ctx):
    """The regular files the parts of a text name, looked for in the folders and in every folder the text names
    (`cd sub && bash x.sh`, in the order it names them), and a bare word as a script in a PATH folder under $HOME
    (an earlier call may have left one there)."""
    parts, home = skim_parts(text, ctx['env']), os.path.realpath(ctx['env'].get('HOME') or os.path.expanduser('~'))
    dirs, listing = list(dict.fromkeys(dirs)), {}

    def names_in(d):
        if d not in listing:
            try:
                listing[d] = set(os.listdir(d)) if os.path.isdir(d) else set()
            except OSError:
                listing[d] = set()
        return listing[d]

    path_dirs = [d for d in (ctx['env'].get('PATH') or '').split(':')
                 if d and os.path.isabs(d) and d not in STD_BINS and os.path.realpath(d).startswith(home + os.sep)]
    found = {}
    for i, part in enumerate(parts):
        if i % 512 == 511 and ctx.get('stop_at') is not None and time.monotonic() > ctx['stop_at']:
            raise Slow()
        if '/' in part or part.startswith(('~', '.')):
            places = [resolve_path(part, d) for d in dirs]
        else:
            places = [os.path.join(d, part) for d in dirs + path_dirs if part in names_in(d)]
        for q in places:
            if os.path.isfile(q):
                found.setdefault(os.path.realpath(q), q)
            elif len(dirs) < 16 and q not in dirs and os.path.isdir(q):
                dirs.append(q)
    return list(found.values())


def skim_reason(text, ctx, what, hint):
    """None when the skim finds no guard word in the text or the files it names, else the refusal."""
    try:
        skim(text, list(folders(ctx)), ctx, 0, {}, 'the command')
    except SkimRefused as e:
        return reason(f'{what}. The guard skimmed it instead, for gh, HTTP clients, disabling words and agent sessions, '
                      f'in the text and in the files it names, and found {e}', hint)
    return None


def skim(text, dirs, ctx, depth, seen, where):
    if ctx.get('stop_at') is not None and time.monotonic() > ctx['stop_at']:
        raise Slow()
    tok = skim_word(text)
    if tok:
        raise SkimRefused(f'{tok} in {where}')
    runs = []   # whether a word of the text is a shell, interpreter or runner: worked out once, when needed
    for path in skim_files(text, dirs, ctx):
        if path in seen or is_self(path, ctx):
            continue
        seen[path] = True
        name = redact(os.path.basename(path))
        if len(seen) > MAX_READINGS:
            raise SkimRefused(f'more than {MAX_READINGS} files named (it reads no more)')
        if ctx.get('stop_at') is not None and time.monotonic() > ctx['stop_at']:
            raise Slow()
        body = read_text(path)
        if body is None:
            try:
                with open(path, 'rb') as f:
                    binary = b'\0' in f.read(8192)
            except OSError:
                binary = False
            if binary:
                if gh_file(path, ctx):
                    raise SkimRefused(f'gh under another name ({name})')
                continue
            if not runs:
                runs.append(any(crash_runner(w) for w in set(re.findall(r'[\w.+-]+', re.sub(r'[\'"\\]', '', text)))))
            if runs[0] or script_like(path):   # a big script, or one a shell or interpreter may run
                tok = file_token(path)
                if tok:
                    raise SkimRefused(f'{tok} in the file {name}' if tok not in (TOO_BIG, NO_OPEN)
                                      else f'the file {name}, which may run and is {tok}')
            continue
        if depth < MAX_DEPTH:
            skim(body, [os.path.dirname(path)] + dirs, ctx, depth + 1, seen, f'the file {name}')
        else:
            tok = skim_word(body)
            if tok:
                raise SkimRefused(f'{tok} in the file {name}')


def slow_reason(cmd, ctx):
    """The guard ran out of time on this command: the refusal, or None when the skim of it finds no guard word.
    The skim gets SKIM_TIME of its own (in the hook an alarm too); out of time again, it refuses."""
    took = min(SKIM_TIME, SKIM_END - process_age())   # a hook that is late already (a slow start) has less
    if took < 0.2:
        return SLOW
    ctx['stop_at'] = time.monotonic() + took
    hook = __name__ == '__main__' and hasattr(signal, 'SIGALRM')
    try:
        if hook:
            signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, out_of_time)
            signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, took + 0.3)
        return skim_reason(cmd, ctx, f'the guard could not finish reading this command and the scripts it runs '
                                     f'within {BUDGET:g} s', SLOW_HINT)
    except BaseException:   # noqa: BLE001 - out of time again, or a failure: refused, as before the skim
        return SLOW
    finally:
        if hook:
            alarm_off()


def pipe_span(runs, k):
    """The commands a pipe out of command k feeds: the next one, or every command of the group it starts
    (`cat f | (cd x && bash)`, `cat f | { true; bash; }`: the group's stdin is the pipe)."""
    if not runs[k][0].get('pipe') or k + 1 >= len(runs):
        return []
    mine, nxt = runs[k][0].get('groups', ()), runs[k + 1][0].get('groups', ())
    if len(nxt) <= len(mine) or nxt[:len(mine)] != mine:
        return [k + 1]
    g, j = nxt[len(mine)], k + 1
    while j + 1 < len(runs) and g in runs[j + 1][0].get('groups', ()) and not runs[j + 1][0].get('sub'):
        j += 1
    return list(range(k + 1, j + 1))


def decide_text(text, ctx, scan_raw=True):
    nest, flow = ctx.get('nest', 0), ctx.setdefault('flow', {})
    if ctx.get('stop_at') is not None and time.monotonic() > ctx['stop_at']:
        raise Slow()
    if nest > MAX_NEST:
        return stopped(text, 'shell text nested too deep to read', flow)
    cmds, plain, taint, rest = parse(text)
    tally = ctx.get('tally')
    if tally is not None:
        tally[0] += len(cmds)
        if tally[0] > MAX_COMMANDS:   # read only what it needs: the guard words, here and in the files named
            r = skim_reason(text, ctx, f'more than {MAX_COMMANDS:,} commands of shell text in this call (the '
                                       'command and the scripts it runs), more than the guard parses in time',
                            'Run the part that calls gh or an HTTP client as a Bash call of its own.')
            if r:
                return r
            flow['unknown'] = f'shell text past the guard\'s {MAX_COMMANDS:,} commands'
            return None
    if rest:   # more than 400 $(..) `..` <(..): what was not read is no harmless text
        r = stopped('\n'.join(rest) + '\n' + text, 'more than 400 $(..), `..` or <(..) in one text, more than the '
                    'guard reads', flow, 'Run it as smaller commands.')
        if r:
            return r
    env = variables(cmds, taint)
    env['base'] = {'HOME': ctx['env'].get('HOME') or ''}
    runs = [(c, file_sub(c, env) or readings(c, env)) for c in cmds]
    firsts = [r[0] for _, r in runs if r[0]]
    loose = any(reads_stdin_as_shell(t) for t in firsts)
    funcs, aliases, cfg = set(), {}, list(ctx.get('cfg', ()))
    ran = {'shell': set(), 'exec': set(), 'code': set()}   # the files the text runs, by how: see written()
    for m in FUNC_RE.finditer(plain):
        funcs.add(m.group(1) or m.group(2))
    for toks in (t for _, r in runs for t in r if t):
        h, via = head(toks)
        if h is None:
            continue
        word = os.path.basename(toks[h])
        if INTERPRETERS.match(word):
            ran['code'].update(os.path.basename(a) for a in positional(toks[h + 1:], set())[:1])
        elif word:
            ran['shell' if 'shell' in via else 'exec'].add(word)
    for toks in firsts:
        h, _ = head(toks)
        if h is None:
            continue
        word = os.path.basename(toks[h])
        if word == 'alias':
            for a in toks[h + 1:]:
                name, eq, val = a.partition('=')
                if eq:
                    aliases.setdefault(name, []).append(val)
    cwds, lost = list(folders(ctx)), False   # where the commands may run: `cd a && ./x` runs a/x
    every = cwds[-1]   # the folder after every `cd` so far worked
    for toks in firsts:
        h, _ = head(toks)
        if h is not None and toks[h] in ('cd', 'pushd', 'popd'):
            to = [a for a in toks[h + 1:] if not a.startswith('-')][:1]
            if to and not hidden(to[0]):
                every = resolve_path(to[0], every)
            if to and not hidden(to[0]) and len(cwds) < 16:
                cwds += [p for p in dict.fromkeys(resolve_path(to[0], d) for d in cwds) if p not in cwds]
            else:
                lost = True
                cwds += [every] if to and not hidden(to[0]) and every not in cwds else []   # past 16: one per cd
                r = to and not hidden(to[0]) and stopped(text, 'more than 16 folders to follow (cd)', flow,
                                                         'Run it as smaller commands.')
                if r:
                    return r
    cfg_hidden, closed = ctx.get('cfg_hidden', False), dict(env, open=False)
    for name, tail in (('GH_CONFIG_DIR', ''), ('XDG_CONFIG_HOME', 'gh'), ('HOME', '.config/gh')):
        dirs = var_texts(name, closed) if name in env['set'] else []
        cfg += [resolve_path(os.path.join(d, tail), w) for d in dirs if d and not hidden(d) for w in cwds]
        cfg_hidden = cfg_hidden or (name in env['standalone'] and any(hidden(d) for d in dirs)) or \
            (lost and any(d and not hidden(d) and not os.path.isabs(os.path.expanduser(d)) for d in dirs))
    c = dict(ctx, raw=plain if scan_raw else ctx.get('raw', text), scan_raw=scan_raw, nest=nest + 1, funcs=funcs,
             aliases=aliases, cfg=cfg, cfg_hidden=cfg_hidden, cwds=cwds)
    gh_set = list(ctx.get('gh_set', ()))
    for cmd, variants in runs:
        for toks in variants:
            gh_set += [a for a in text_aliases(toks, stdin_of(cmd, env, c), c) if a not in gh_set]
    c['gh_set'] = gh_set
    c['ghs'] = set(ctx.get('ghs', ())).union(*(gh_names(toks) for _, variants in runs for toks in variants if toks))
    c['agents'] = dict(ctx.get('agents', {}))
    for _, variants in runs:
        for toks in (t for t in variants if t):
            c['agents'].update(agent_names(toks, c))
    c['sourced'] = list(ctx.get('sourced', ())) + [f for _, variants in runs for toks in variants[:1] if toks
                                                    for f in sourced_files(toks)]
    # What the text may write (sed -f s.sed reads s.sed only when no command of the text writes it), and whether
    # every command of it only handles text (then nothing in it runs what a compressor or data reader got)
    wrote, wrote_any, by = text_writes(runs, env, c)
    c['wrote'], c['wrote_any'] = list(ctx.get('wrote', ())) + wrote, ctx.get('wrote_any') or wrote_any
    c['wrote_by'] = list(ctx.get('wrote_by', ())) + by
    plain_text = not ctx.get('impure') and all(inert(v, c) for _, v in runs)
    c['impure'] = not plain_text
    # Where each command's text goes on: into a file, into another command's words ($(..)), or down a pipe that
    # does not end in a word that only handles text.
    tgts = [[t for op, w in cmd['redirs'] if op.startswith('>') for t in redir_texts(op, w, env)
             if not NO_FILE.fullmatch(t)] for cmd, _ in runs]
    spans = [pipe_span(runs, k) for k in range(len(runs))]
    leaks = [False] * len(runs)
    for k in range(len(runs) - 1, -1, -1):
        piped = runs[k][0].get('pipe') and k + 1 < len(runs)
        leaks[k] = bool(runs[k][0].get('sub') or tgts[k] or (piped and (leaks[k + 1] or not all(
            pure_sink(runs[j][1]) for j in spans[k]))))
    hows = [written(variants[0], cmd, env, ran, c) for cmd, variants in runs]
    for how, (cmd, variants) in zip(hows, runs):   # .. | tee x.sh; sh x.sh: the script comes through a pipe
        h, _ = head(variants[0])
        if how in ('shell', 'exec') and not cmd['docs'] and h is not None \
                and os.path.basename(variants[0][h]) not in ('echo', 'printf'):
            loose = True
    # Where a command's output is run as code: a pipe into a shell or interpreter that runs its stdin, or into a sed
    # that runs its input (cat f | sed e), also through data filters (cat f | grep -v x | bash), and $(..) <(..) in
    # a text that runs a hidden word as shell or code (eval "$(cat f)", source <(cat f), bash <<< "$(cat f)")
    feeds_at = [False] * len(runs)
    for k in range(len(runs) - 2, -1, -1):
        if runs[k][0].get('pipe'):
            nxt = runs[k + 1][1][0]
            h = head(nxt)[0] if nxt else None
            feeds_at[k] = any(stdin_runs(runs[j][1][0], hows[j], loose) for j in spans[k]) or \
                (h is not None and os.path.basename(nxt[h]) == 'sed' and sed_runs_input(nxt[h + 1:])) or \
                (feeds_at[k + 1] and bool(runs[k + 1][0].get('pipe')) and pure_word(runs[k + 1][1]))
    sub_runs = any(not cmd.get('sub') and runs_hidden(variants[0], cmd) for cmd, variants in runs)
    outs = list(hows)   # cat p.txt | tee ~/.local/bin/ls; ls: the text a pipe carries into such a write
    for k in range(len(runs) - 2, -1, -1):
        if not outs[k] and runs[k][0].get('pipe') and outs[k + 1] and pure_word(runs[k + 1][1]):
            outs[k] = outs[k + 1]
    # Where a stage's text ends in a program that reads it only as data (cat msg.md | python3 check.py, .. | gzip >
    # m.gz, through data filters): a line there that calls a refuseScripts entry runs nowhere (data_token)
    # The text must hold nothing else that runs (a data reader or compressor stage aside): `.. | gzip > y.gz;
    # gunzip y.gz; bash y` and `printf .. > check.py; cat q.txt | python3 check.py` read on
    readers = {k + 1 for k in range(len(runs) - 1) if runs[k][0].get('pipe') and ends_text(runs[k + 1][1], c)}
    calm = not ctx.get('impure') and not ctx.get('wrote_any') and \
        all(k in readers or inert(v, c) for k, (_, v) in enumerate(runs))
    quiet = [False] * len(runs)
    for k in range(len(runs) - 2, -1, -1):
        cmd, nxt = runs[k][0], runs[k + 1]
        if not calm or not cmd.get('pipe') or cmd.get('sub') or tgts[k] or outs[k] or feeds_at[k]:
            continue
        on = nxt[0].get('pipe') and k + 2 < len(runs)
        reader = k + 1 in readers and not nxt[0].get('sub') and not hows[k + 1] and \
            (not tgts[k + 1] or all(head(t)[0] is not None and os.path.basename(t[head(t)[0]]) in COMPRESSORS
                                    for t in nxt[1])) and \
            (not on or not leaks[k + 1])
        passes_on = pure_word(nxt[1]) and not any(os.path.basename(t[head(t)[0]]) in WRITERS | NO_SINK
                                                  for t in nxt[1]) \
            and not nxt[0].get('sub') and not tgts[k + 1] and quiet[k + 1]
        quiet[k] = bool(reader or passes_on)
    # while read -r l; do $l; done < f: a loop that runs a hidden command word runs the lines it reads; so does a
    # read or mapfile of a file in a text that runs a hidden word (read -r x < f; $x)
    hid_runs = any(h is not None and hidden(t[h]) for t in firsts for h in [head(t)[0]])
    # { python3 -; } < f, ( bash ) < f, exec < f; python3 -: a part that starts no command hands its stdin to the
    # group before it, or (exec) to the rest of the text: read as fed to any interpreter of the text
    group_code = any(INTERPRETERS.match(os.path.basename(t[h])) for t in firsts for h in [head(t)[0]] if h is not None)
    for k, (how, (cmd, variants)) in enumerate(zip(hows, runs)):
        fed = list(cmd['docs']) + fed_texts(cmd, env, c)   # heredocs, <<< words, < files
        h0, via0 = head(variants[0]) if variants[0] else (None, set())
        loop = hid_runs and (variants[0][:1] == ['done'] or h0 is not None and not via0
                             and variants[0][h0] in ('read', 'mapfile', 'readarray'))
        no_cmd = h0 is None and not via0 & {'shell', 'eval', 'xargs'} and not cmd.get('sub')
        for op, w in cmd['redirs']:   # bash < big.sh: a file the shell runs that the guard could not read whole
            if op == '<' and stdin_runs(variants[0], how, loose):
                r = next((r for word in redir_texts(op, w, env) if not hidden(word) for d in folders(c)
                          for r in [unread(resolve_path(word, d))] if r), None)
                if r:
                    return r
        for body in fed:
            kind = 'shell' if loop else doc_kind(variants[0], how, loose, body)
            kind = 'code' if kind == 'data' and no_cmd and group_code else kind
            # python3 - < f, node < f, perl < f: a refuse-script line in code an interpreter runs (also a script it
            # gets as data unless it is an allow-listed data reader: ends_text), as for a shell
            tok = kind == 'code' and (named(body) or REFUSE_SCRIPTS and (
                stdin_runs(variants[0], how, loose) or how or not ends_text(variants, c)) and refuse_script_named(body))
            if tok:
                return unproven(tok, 'in inline code (a heredoc into an interpreter, or a script in another '
                                     'language that the same command writes and runs): the guard reads shell text '
                                     'and gh/curl calls, not code', CODE_HINT)
            if kind in ('shell', 'script'):
                r = decide_text(body, c)
                if r:
                    return r
        feeds = feeds_at[k] or (cmd.get('sub') and sub_runs)
        cc = dict(c, stdin=stdin_of(cmd, env, c), writes=how, cmd=cmd, leak=leaks[k], targets=tgts[k], feeds_code=feeds,
                  feeds_script='' if how else outs[k], quiet=quiet[k] and not feeds)
        for toks in variants:
            r = toks and segment_reason(toks, cc, loose)
            if r:
                return r
    return None


def decide(cmd, cwd, env, budget=None, use_id=None, saves=None):
    """None to let the command run, else the deny reason. budget: seconds the reading may take (None: no limit).
    use_id: the tool call's id (an owner approval is used up by one call; the same call's second hook run passes).
    saves: a list to hand the cache save to (the hook prints its answer first, then saves); None: save here, last.
    The owner marker is read before any save, while the hook's alarm still holds: a save that swallows an alarm
    (cache_save catches it) must not leave the marker's reads with no time limit."""
    if not cmd or (QUICK and quick_pass(cmd) and not quick_shadowed(cmd, cwd, env)):
        return None
    ctx = {'cwd': cwd or os.getcwd(), 'env': env, 'depth': 0, 'raw': cmd, 'all': cmd, 'memo': {}, 'flow': {},
           'stop_at': None if budget is None else time.monotonic() + budget, 'cache': {}, 'tally': [0]}
    try:
        why = decide_text(cmd, ctx) or flow_reason(ctx['flow'])
    except Slow:
        why = slow_reason(cmd, ctx)
        ctx['cache'] = {}   # a reading cut off half-way keeps nothing (nor what the skim after it looked up)
    if why:
        why = owner_marker(why, cmd, ctx['cwd'], env, use_id)
    if saves is not None:
        saves.append(ctx)
    else:
        save_quietly([ctx])
    return why


def save_quietly(ctxs):
    """Last, with the answer known (and printed, in the hook): an alarm or a failure in the save changes nothing."""
    for ctx in ctxs:
        try:
            cache_save(ctx)
        except BaseException:   # noqa: BLE001
            pass


def owner_marker(why, cmd, cwd, env, use_id=None):
    """The refusal, or None when the command is the one command the owner approved (agent_guards.approval): it ends
    with the approval's code, runs in its folder, and the approval was not used before (this use is recorded in the
    state folder, so it is used up). A code that is wrong, used, for another command or folder, or too old, or one
    with no approval file, adds why to the refusal."""
    if MARK_CMD not in cmd:
        return why
    try:
        from agent_guards import approval
        used = state_dir(env) or state_dir({'HOME': os.path.expanduser('~')})   # where its one use is recorded
        ok, problem = approval.approved(cmd, cwd, use_id=use_id, used_dir=used)
    except BaseException as e:   # noqa: BLE001 - no approval code: no approval
        ok, problem = False, f'the approval code cannot be loaded ({type(e).__name__})'
    if ok:
        return None
    return why + (f' (The owner marker is not accepted: {problem}.)' if problem else '')


def redact(s):
    s = re.sub(r'(gh[pousr]_|github_pat_)[A-Za-z0-9_]+', r'\1***', s)
    s = re.sub(r'(?i)(bearer\s+|token[=:\s]+)\S+', r'\1***', s)
    return s[:300]


def log(cmd, why, env):
    """One line per refusal. Written after the answer, and only until 2 s past the budget: a slow disk must not
    keep the hook past its limit (a hook that is cut off answers nothing)."""
    try:
        left = BUDGET + 2.0 - process_age()
        if left <= 0:
            return
        if hasattr(signal, 'SIGALRM'):
            signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, out_of_time)
            signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, left)
        d = state_dir(env) or state_dir({'HOME': os.path.expanduser('~')})
        if not d:
            return
        os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
        with open(os.path.join(d, 'log'), 'a') as f:
            f.write(time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ ', time.gmtime()) + json.dumps(
                {'cmd': redact(cmd), 'why': why[:160], 'cwd': os.getcwd()}) + '\n')
    except BaseException:   # also Slow: the answer is out already
        pass
    finally:
        alarm_off()


ANSWERED = []


TOOLS = ('Bash', 'Monitor')   # the tools whose tool_input.command is shell text


def with_problem(out):
    """The answer with a machine file problem added where the agent and the owner see it."""
    if not SETTINGS_PROBLEM:
        return out
    try:
        from agent_guards import config
        return config.notice('actions-disable-guard', SETTINGS_PROBLEM, {'hook_event_name': 'PreToolUse'}, out)
    except BaseException:   # noqa: BLE001 - no lib: say it plainly
        return dict(out or {}, systemMessage=f'actions-disable-guard: {SETTINGS_PROBLEM}; built-in defaults used. '
                                             'Tell the owner.')


def deny(why):
    out = {'hookSpecificOutput': {'hookEventName': 'PreToolUse', 'permissionDecision': 'deny',
                                  'permissionDecisionReason': why}}
    try:
        out = with_problem(out)
    except BaseException:   # noqa: BLE001 - the refusal matters more than the notice
        pass
    print(json.dumps(out), flush=True)
    ANSWERED.append(True)


def passed():
    """Let the command run; with a machine file problem, say so in the answer."""
    if SETTINGS_PROBLEM:
        try:
            print(json.dumps(with_problem(None)), flush=True)
            ANSWERED.append(True)
        except BaseException:   # noqa: BLE001
            pass
    return 0


def alarm_off():
    if hasattr(signal, 'SIGALRM'):
        signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, 0)


CRASH_TEXT = re.compile(r'[<$`\\]|>\(')   # text the command gets at run time: < f, heredoc, $(..), $x, `..`, >(..), \
CRASH_KEYWORDS = {'if', 'then', 'else', 'elif', 'fi', 'while', 'until', 'do', 'done', 'case', 'esac', 'in', '!', 'time',
                  'for', 'select', 'function', '[[', ']]'}
# words that run text the command does not spell out: a file, its stdin, a config or a script of their own
CRASH_RUNNERS = WRAPPERS | SHELLS | LOGIN_RUNNERS | {
    'alias', 'trap', 'at', 'batch', 'make', 'gmake', 'git', 'ssh', 'su', 'parallel', 'find', 'awk', 'gawk', 'mawk',
    'nawk', 'busybox', 'sed', 'perl', 'ruby', 'claude', 'npx', 'npm', 'pnpm', 'yarn', 'tmux', 'screen', 'crontab',
    'install', 'rsync', 'tar', 'unzip', 'cp', 'mv', 'ln', 'tee', 'dd', 'heavy', 'vp', 'hash', 'enable', 'fc',
    # launchers that run the words after them as a command
    'taskset', 'unshare', 'chrt', 'prlimit', 'setpriv', 'ssh-agent', 'dbus-run-session', 'chroot', 'nsenter', 'sg',
    'newgrp', 'pkexec', 'firejail', 'bwrap', 'proot', 'fakeroot', 'fakechroot', 'schroot', 'strace', 'ltrace', 'gdb',
    'lldb', 'valgrind', 'perf', 'catchsegv', 'xvfb-run', 'faketime', 'torsocks', 'proxychains', 'proxychains4',
    'numactl', 'cgexec', 'systemd-run', 'run-parts', 'start-stop-daemon', 'expect', 'vi', 'vim', 'nvim', 'view',
    'ex', 'emacs', 'less', 'more', 'man', 'Rscript', 'julia'}
# any sh or csh family name (rbash, mksh, tcsh ..), also after a link is resolved
CRASH_SHELL = re.compile(r'^r?(?:ba|z|k|mk|lk|ok|pdk|da|a|ya|po|tc|c|fi)?sh[\d.]*$')


def crash_runner(word):
    """After a crash: whether a word (or the program it names) is a shell, interpreter, wrapper or runner."""
    base = os.path.basename(word)
    return base in CRASH_RUNNERS or bool(INTERPRETERS.match(base) or CRASH_SHELL.match(base))


def crash_passes(raw, env):
    """After a crash while the guard read a command that names no gh, HTTP client or disabling word: whether it
    still surely runs only text it spells out, so passing it is safe. Refused (False) when it may run text from
    elsewhere: a < file, heredoc or here-string, $(..), `..`, a variable, >(..), a backslash, a brace expansion
    (`{bash,f}`), a shell, interpreter, wrapper, launcher or runner word anywhere in a part, quotes removed (eval,
    source, ., xargs, sudo, taskset, unshare, git, make, awk, ba''sh, rbash ..), a command word with a / (a script)
    or a glob, or one that is no compiled program in a system folder on PATH (a script there, a project's PATH
    folder) or whose real path is a shell or interpreter (a link).
    Input that is no event is read by the same rule as text; anything else it cannot work out counts as refused."""
    try:
        try:
            ev = json.loads(raw)
            cmd = (ev.get('tool_input') or {}).get('command') or ''
        except (ValueError, AttributeError):
            cmd = raw
        if not isinstance(cmd, str) or CRASH_TEXT.search(cmd) or BRACE.search(cmd):   # `{bash,f}` runs `bash f`
            return False
        dirs = (env.get('PATH') or '').split(os.pathsep)
        for part in re.split(r'[;&|()\n]+', cmd):
            words = [re.sub('[\'"]', '', w) for w in part.split()]   # every quote, also inside a word (ba''sh)
            words = [w for w in words if not is_assign(w) and w not in CRASH_KEYWORDS and not re.match(r'\d*>', w)]
            if not words:
                continue
            word = words[0]
            if not word or '/' in word or word.startswith('-') or re.search(r'[*?\[]', word) \
                    or any(crash_runner(w) for w in words):   # any word: `taskset 1 bash f`, `nice -n1 bash f`
                return False
            for d in dirs:
                p = os.path.join(d, word)
                if not os.path.isabs(d):
                    return False
                if os.path.isfile(p) and os.access(p, os.X_OK):
                    if d not in STD_BINS or crash_runner(os.path.realpath(p)):   # rbash -> bash
                        return False
                    with open(p, 'rb') as f:
                        if f.read(4) != b'\x7fELF':
                            return False
                    break
        return True
    except BaseException:   # noqa: BLE001
        return False


def main():
    """The hook. Exit 1 or no answer lets the command run, so whatever happens on the way ends in an answer: a
    late Slow or a crash in the handlers below (named, log, deny) still refuses."""
    del ANSWERED[:]
    try:
        return answer()
    except BaseException as e:
        alarm_off()
        if ANSWERED:   # a second answer would break the first one (two JSON objects are no answer)
            return 0
        deny(reason(f'the guard itself failed ({type(e).__name__}) while it answered, so it refuses; tell the owner '
                    'if this repeats'))
        return 0


def answer():
    raw, env = '', dict(os.environ)
    try:
        # The limit counts from the start of the hook process (Python's start-up and a slow machine use it up too),
        # so the guard answers BUDGET seconds after that start at the latest: 5 s before the hook's 10 s limit.
        # A command the fast exit passes needs no time, so the clock is looked at only after that.
        left = BUDGET - process_age()
        if hasattr(signal, 'SIGALRM'):   # also when one long step never comes back to the clock check
            signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, out_of_time)
            signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, max(left, 0.5))   # reading stdin may hang
        raw = sys.stdin.read()
        if SETTINGS_PROBLEM:
            try:
                from agent_guards import config
                config.warn('actions-disable-guard', SETTINGS_PROBLEM)
            except BaseException:   # noqa: BLE001
                try:
                    sys.stderr.write(f'actions-disable-guard: {SETTINGS_PROBLEM}; built-in defaults used.\n')
                except Exception:   # noqa: BLE001
                    pass
        if env.get('ACTIONS_DISABLE_GUARD_TEST_CRASH'):
            raise RuntimeError('test crash')
        ev = json.loads(raw)
        if ev.get('tool_name') not in TOOLS:
            alarm_off()
            return passed()
        cmd = (ev.get('tool_input') or {}).get('command') or ''
        cwd = ev.get('cwd') or os.getcwd()
        if QUICK and quick_pass(cmd) and not quick_shadowed(cmd, cwd, env):
            alarm_off()
            return passed()
        left = BUDGET - process_age()   # none left (a slow start): decide() goes straight to the skim
        saves = []
        why = decide(cmd, cwd, env, left - 1.0, ev.get('tool_use_id'), saves)
        if why:
            deny(why)   # the answer first: a slow or hung file system then holds up only the save, cut by the alarm
        save_quietly(saves)
        alarm_off()
    except Slow:
        alarm_off()   # first: the handlers below must not be cut off by the alarm (that answers nothing)
        why, cmd = SLOW, raw
    except Exception as e:
        alarm_off()
        # also when quotes split the words, or the word is put together from variables: gh or an HTTP client is enough
        try:
            tok = named(raw)
        except Exception:
            tok = True
        if tok or LOOSE_RE.search(raw) or LOOSE_RE.search(re.sub(r'["\'\\]', '', raw)) or CRASH_RE.search(raw):
            why = reason(f'the guard itself failed ({type(e).__name__}) on a command that uses gh or an HTTP client '
                         'or names disabling Actions, so it refuses; tell the owner if this repeats')
            cmd = raw
        elif not crash_passes(raw, env):   # ( bash ) < f: a crash fails closed where text comes from elsewhere
            why = reason(f'the guard itself failed ({type(e).__name__}) on a command that may run text it does not '
                         'spell out (a file, a redirect, $(..), a variable, a shell, interpreter or script), so it '
                         'refuses; tell the owner if this repeats')
            cmd = raw
        else:
            return passed()
    if why:
        if not ANSWERED:
            deny(why)   # first: logging may be slow
        log(cmd, why, env)
        return 0
    return passed()


if __name__ == '__main__':
    sys.exit(main())
